window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Week 1 Content","Week 2 Content","Not Tested","A ★ marks something the instructor said outright would be on an exam."]},{"s":"Week 1 Content▾","a":"week-1-content","k":["Week 1 Content▾","First Sub-Topic","★ A thing she said you will be tested on — stated in about twenty words, with the specific number or threshold included.","A second point: high-yield, no star, because she never said it would be tested.","Second Sub-Topic","Group related facts into one bullet rather than splitting them — a bullet that carries a definition, its threshold, and its consequence is one reference item, not three.","Keep the wording tight. Drop the mechanism asides, the percentages you won't be asked for, and \"she said…\" on any bullet that already has a star.","Quick Reference Table","A compact table for material the instructor said to know exhaustively — the kind of list that would be twenty bullets otherwise.","First item — What lets you recognize it, including the discriminating test. — What is given. — What happens if it isn't.","Second item — Four columns is about the practical limit for readability. — Keep cells short. — One clause each."]},{"s":"Week 2 Content▾","a":"week-2-content","k":["Week 2 Content▾","Sub-Topic","Copy a .hint-group block per sub-topic, and an h2.block per week."]},{"s":"Not Tested▾","a":"not-tested","k":["Not Tested▾","Explicitly Excluded","Things the instructor said you do not need to memorize — exact doses, specific percentages, brand names, and so on.","Record these the moment you hear them. They are the cheapest studying you will ever skip."]}]},{"f":"week1-respiratory-labs-diagnostics.html","t":"Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","How Lab Results Are Obtained","Pulse Oximetry","Sputum Tests","Imaging","TB Testing","Bronchoscopy","Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One","Thoracentesis","Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Every body system in Med-Surg gets its own labs-and-diagnostics lecture first. Related nursing care — pre- and post-procedure — is fair game on exams."]},{"s":"How Lab Results Are Obtained▾","a":"how-lab-results-are-obtained","k":["How Lab Results Are Obtained▾","Venous draw — most standard labs.","Fingerstick — most blood glucose checks.","Arterial draw — used for an ABG (arterial blood gas). At UK this is typically done by the respiratory therapist, not the nurse.","ABGs are respiratory content, but this course saves ABG interpretation for block two — it isn't covered in depth on this page."]},{"s":"Pulse Oximetry▾","a":"pulse-oximetry","k":["Pulse Oximetry▾","MNEMONIC: think of oxygen delivery like a bus system. The red blood cell is the bus; hemoglobin (built from iron) is the seats; O2 is the passengers sitting in those seats. Pulse ox / O2 sat = how full the buses are. Hematocrit = the total …","Normal pulse ox / O2 sat: ≥ 95%.","A low O2 sat can point to hypoventilation, atelectasis, a pneumothorax, or other lung issues.","Non-invasive; can be intermittent (with routine vitals, e.g. Q4) or continuous (common right after surgery).","Commonly used to titrate oxygen levels in hospitalized patients.","Best site is the fingertip — remove nail polish first. The earlobe and toe can be used, but the finger is what the sensor is actually made for."]},{"s":"Sputum Tests▾","a":"sputum-tests","k":["Sputum Tests▾","Culture & Sensitivity (C&S) — Whether there's bacteria in the sputum, and what it's sensitive to. — Guides antibiotic choice.","Cytology — Cancer cells — ordered when lung cancer is suspected. — Part of the lung cancer diagnostic workup.","Acid-Fast Bacillus (AFB) — Active tuberculosis. — Confirms whether latent TB has converted to active/reactivated TB.","Collect a deep specimen (not saliva) in a sterile container and get it to the lab right away — don't let it sit at the bedside. The morning specimen is best: secretions are more concentrated after a night of lying still, and the patient has…"]},{"s":"Imaging▾","a":"imaging","k":["Imaging▾","Chest X-Ray — A 2-D snapshot of the lung. Most common views: PA and lateral. — Patient removes metal between neck and waist (bra straps, necklaces).","CT (CAT) Scan — Cross-sectional structures — a 3-D-style picture built from slices. — May require sedation (patient must lie still, or if claustrophobic). Loud clicking noise — warn the patient. Contrast is provider preference and is iodine…","MRI — Assesses lesions that are hard to see on CT; distinguishes vascular from non-vascular structures. — Remove all metal. May need sedation for claustrophobia in a closed MRI. Contrast used is not iodine-based.","PET Scan — Physiology and function (blood flow, sugar uptake) rather than just structure — uses an injected radioactive tracer. — Used heavily for cancer diagnosis, and for some brain disorders (Alzheimer's, epilepsy, brain tumors).","CT with contrast — check renal function first. Contrast is nephrotoxic. Check creatinine (kidney-specific) and BUN before the scan, and notify the provider if either is elevated. Patient should be encouraged to drink fluids afterward to hel…","A shellfish allergy is not actually linked to contrast reactions — the real concern is an iodine allergy. Some standardized tests (including the NCLEX) can still ask about it the old way, so know it for boards even though it's outdated in p…","Expect a patient getting IV contrast to feel a warm flush spread through their body — that's normal, not a sign of a reaction, but tell them beforehand so it doesn't alarm them."]},{"s":"TB Testing▾","a":"tb-testing","k":["TB Testing▾","TB Skin Test (Mantoux)","Injected intradermally, at only a 10–15° angle — just enough to raise a bleb under the skin.","Must be read in person at a follow-up visit.","IGRA (blood test)","Interferon Gamma Release Assay — a blood draw instead of a skin test.","No need to return to have it read.","Neither option is better than the other — they're just two different ways to get the same answer."]},{"s":"Bronchoscopy▾","a":"bronchoscopy","k":["Bronchoscopy▾","Scopes the bronchi — used to visualize the airway and/or obtain a biopsy sample (e.g. secretions the patient can't expectorate on their own).","Consent signed beforehand — It's an invasive procedure.","NPO 6–12 hours before — Reduces nausea/vomiting and aspiration risk once sedated.","Sedation given; oral pharynx anesthetized — So the patient doesn't gag or vomit around the scope.","Keep NPO after the procedure until the gag reflex returns — The throat is numbed by the anesthetic used during the procedure.","Blood-tinged mucus after the procedure = expected finding — Minor trauma from the scope. Document and continue to monitor — no need to notify the provider for this alone.","Watch for bleeding and pneumothorax after any bronchoscopy or lung biopsy. An unexplained increased heart rate can signal hemorrhaging; abrupt shortness of breath can signal a pneumothorax (collapsed lung). Notify the provider."]},{"s":"Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One▾","a":"lung-biopsy-four-ways-to-get-one","k":["Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One▾","Transbronchial Lung Biopsy — Via bronchoscopy — a piece of tissue is taken through the scope. — Endoscopy suite.","Trans-Thoracic Needle Aspiration — A needle is put directly through the thorax, with CT guidance. — Radiology, with CT guidance.","Open Lung Biopsy — Provider goes in surgically. — OR.","VATS (Video-Assisted Thoracic Surgery) — Video-assisted, through the thorax. — OR.","\"Scoping\" always refers to going in through a natural orifice with a camera — a bronchoscopy scopes the bronchus the way a colonoscopy scopes the colon or a cystoscopy scopes the bladder. Endoscopy is the umbrella term for all of them."]},{"s":"Thoracentesis▾","a":"thoracentesis","k":["Thoracentesis▾","A large-bore needle removes fluid (or air) from the pleural space — most often done for a pleural effusion, since fluid there reduces the surface area available for oxygen delivery and can cause respiratory problems.","Consent signed — Invasive procedure.","Position: sitting upright, leaning forward, elbows on an overhead table — Pulling the elbows toward the overhead table opens up the intercostal spaces the provider needs to access.","Instruct the patient not to talk during the procedure — Patient needs to stay very still.","Chest X-ray after the procedure — Confirms the provider didn't inadvertently nick the pleura and cause a pneumothorax.","Assess for signs of hypoxia after — Same reason — ruling out a pneumothorax.","A paracentesis is the equivalent procedure for fluid collected in the peritoneum (covered later, in hepatobiliary) — same idea, different space. Fluid sitting somewhere it doesn't belong and serves no purpose is called third spacing."]},{"s":"Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)▾","a":"pulmonary-function-tests-pfts","k":["Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)▾","Total Lung Capacity — Inhale as deep as possible, then exhale as deep as possible. — The full range of lung volume, not just a normal breath.","FEV1 (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 Second) — Patient takes a deep breath in, then pushes the air out as fast as possible — how much comes out in that first second. — Patients with obstructive pulmonary disease (e.g. COPD) have trouble gett…","Peak Flow Meter — A small device the patient carries and blows into themselves to check their own FEV1. — Mainly an asthma-monitoring tool (covered in patho-pharm) — a falling number relative to the patient's personal best can signal an ear…","PFTs are performed by a respiratory therapist: the patient's nose is pinched, mouth sealed around the device, breathing in and out on command."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient asks why a PET scan was ordered in addition to the CT scan already completed. Which explanation should the nurse give?","A It produces a clearer two-dimensional image of the chest.","B It shows how tissue is functioning rather than only its structure.","C It avoids the need for any injected contrast material.","D It distinguishes vascular from non-vascular structures.","Immediately following a thoracentesis, which nursing action takes priority?","A Encouraging the patient to cough and deep breathe hourly","B Assessing for hypoxia and obtaining the ordered chest x-ray","C Measuring the patient's abdominal girth for comparison","D Keeping the patient NPO until the gag reflex has returned","A patient is taught to use a peak flow meter at home. Which finding should the patient be taught to report?","A A reading that has fallen below the patient's personal best","B A reading obtained before rather than after inhaler use","C A reading that varies by a small amount between efforts","D A reading taken while seated rather than while standing","A patient is going to radiology for a PA and lateral chest x-ray. Which instruction should the nurse give?","A Remain NPO until the images have been obtained.","B Report any allergy to iodine before the images.","C Expect a loud clicking noise during the images.","D Remove metal objects from the neck to the waist.","A nursing student asks what a pulse oximetry reading actually reflects. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","A The total volume of blood made up of red blood cells","B The amount of iron available to build hemoglobin","C How much of the hemoglobin is carrying oxygen","D The rate at which oxygen crosses the alveolar wall","A provider is preparing to perform a thoracentesis at the bedside. How should the nurse position the patient?","A Supine with the head of the bed flat and arms at the sides.","B Sitting upright and leaning forward onto an overhead table.","C Side-lying on the affected side with the knees drawn up.","D High Fowler's with both arms raised above the head.","A patient is scheduled for an MRI. Which pre-procedure action is most important for the nurse to complete?","A Verify that all metal has been removed from the patient.","B Confirm the patient has no history of shellfish allergy.","C Ensure the patient has been NPO for at least six hours.","D Check that the patient's creatinine is within normal limits.","A patient is scheduled for a CT scan with contrast. Which laboratory results should the nurse review and report to the provider if elevated?","A Hemoglobin and hematocrit","B White blood cell count and differential","C Platelet count and international normalized ratio","D Creatinine and blood urea nitrogen","A patient is scheduled for a trans-thoracic needle aspiration. Where should the nurse tell the patient the procedure will take place?","A In the operating room suite","B In the endoscopy suite","C In the radiology department","D At the patient's bedside","Two hours after a bronchoscopy, a patient's sputum is streaked with a small amount of blood. Vital signs are unchanged from baseline and the patient denies dyspnea. Which action should the nurse take?","A Document the finding and continue routine monitoring.","B Notify the provider that hemorrhage is suspected.","C Obtain a stat chest x-ray to rule out a pneumothorax.","D Hold the patient's next scheduled anticoagulant dose.","A patient being screened for tuberculosis tells the nurse they are unable to return to the clinic later in the week. Which screening option addresses this?","A An interferon gamma release assay","B A Mantoux tuberculin skin test","C A sputum specimen for acid-fast bacillus","D A chest x-ray read during the same visit","A nurse is administering a Mantoux tuberculin skin test. Which technique is correct?","A Inject subcutaneously at a 45-degree angle","B Inject intramuscularly at a 90-degree angle","C Inject intradermally at a 45-degree angle","D Inject intradermally at a 10- to 15-degree angle","A provider suspects lung cancer and orders a sputum study. Which study should the nurse anticipate?","A Sputum for acid-fast bacillus","B Sputum for culture and sensitivity","C Blood for interferon gamma release assay","D Sputum for cytology examination","A patient receiving IV contrast during a CT scan reports a warm sensation spreading through the body. What is the nurse's interpretation of this report?","A An expected response that requires no intervention","B An early sign of an allergic reaction to the contrast","C An indication that the contrast has extravasated","D A sign that the patient's renal function is impaired","A nurse is preparing a patient for a bronchoscopy. Which actions belong in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","A Confirm that informed consent has been signed","B Keep the patient NPO for 6 to 12 hours beforehand","C Anticipate that the oral pharynx will be anesthetized","D Withhold oral intake afterward until the gag reflex returns","E Position the patient leaning forward over an overhead table","F Report any blood-tinged mucus to the provider immediately","A patient with dark nail polish requires continuous pulse oximetry. Which action should the nurse take?","A Apply the probe to the earlobe, which is the preferred site.","B Remove the polish and place the probe on a fingertip.","C Apply the probe over the polish and note it in the record.","D Obtain intermittent readings with routine vital signs instead.","A patient with COPD completes pulmonary function testing. Which result is most consistent with this patient's disease process?","A An increased total lung capacity with an unchanged FEV1","B A reduced forced expiratory volume in one second","C A reduced total lung capacity with an unchanged FEV1","D An improvement in FEV1 with each repeated effort","A patient returns to the unit after a bronchoscopy and asks for a drink of water. Which finding must the nurse confirm before honoring the request?","A Oxygen saturation has returned to baseline.","B The patient can sit upright without dizziness.","C The patient's gag reflex has returned.","D The patient is fully alert after sedation.","Thirty minutes after a transbronchial lung biopsy, a patient becomes abruptly short of breath. Which complication does this finding most suggest?","A Hemorrhage at the biopsy site","B An allergic reaction to the sedation","C A pneumothorax on the biopsy side","D Aspiration from an anesthetized pharynx","A nurse is collecting a sputum specimen for culture and sensitivity. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","A Collect the specimen first thing in the morning","B Have the patient expectorate a deep specimen rather than saliva","C Place the specimen in a sterile container","D Transport the specimen to the laboratory promptly","E Have the patient drink several glasses of water immediately before collecting","F Leave the specimen at the bedside until the next scheduled lab pickup"]}]},{"f":"week1-upper-respiratory-problems.html","t":"Upper Respiratory Problems","secs":[{"s":"Upper Respiratory Problems","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","Epistaxis (Nosebleed)","Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)","Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)","Acute Pharyngitis","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions"]},{"s":"Epistaxis (Nosebleed)▾","a":"epistaxis-nosebleed","k":["Epistaxis (Nosebleed)▾","Epistaxis is just the clinical name for a nosebleed.","Common Causes","Dry air (irritated nasal mucosa) — including going somewhere with drier air, like the mountains.","Trauma, infection, or a foreign body (think pediatric patients).","Tumors.","Hypertension.","A blood disorder / blood dyscrasia — e.g. a patient on anticoagulants with thin blood, or someone who's had chemotherapy.","If a patient has a spontaneous nosebleed, ask whether they're on anticoagulants — it likely explains the cause, and affects how quickly you can get it to stop.","IV Heparin — Protamine sulfate.","Warfarin — Vitamin K.","Anterior Nosebleed","The one you'd typically think of — up in the front part of the nose.","Position the patient upright, leaning forward (not backward — blood going down the back of the throat could cause aspiration).","Lateral pressure on the nose, with ice, to vasoconstrict.","Discourage nose blowing — especially important if the patient has increased ICP, since blowing increases pressure in the nose.","Pinch just below the bony prominence, on the soft tissue — pinching the bone itself won't do any good.","Posterior Nosebleed","A bigger deal — usually requires hospitalization.","Hard to stop: you can't pinch bleeding happening at the back of the bone.","May need a balloon catheter or posterior packing (a tamponade — pressure applied to obstruct the capillaries and stop the bleed).","With packing in place, keep an eye on respiratory status (it can impede breathing) — humidification, oxygenation, bed rest, pain control, oral care, and patient teaching (e.g. saline spray).","Avoid aspirin and NSAIDs (they thin the blood) and avoid strenuous activity.","A posterior nosebleed is an emergency. It's difficult to control and usually requires hospitalization."]},{"s":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)▾","a":"obstructive-sleep-apnea-osa","k":["Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)▾","Apnea = a cessation of breathing. Hypopnea = breathing slows considerably (both are respiratory-effort-related).","Caused by repetitive collapsing of the upper airway during sleep — the mandible drops back and blocks the airway, and the patient isn't aware it's happening.","Obesity — A lot of adipose tissue and a large neck circumference literally impedes the airway by pressing on it.","Nasal/Pharyngeal Structural Abnormalities — —","Increased Age — —","Male Sex — —","Smoking — ★ Also a risk factor, but a less well-established one than the four above.","Signs and symptoms: patients are often unaware they have it — they're just sleepy, and it's usually their significant other who reports the snoring/noises. Also: morning headaches (from lack of quality sleep), a large neck/waist circumferen…","Polysomnography (a sleep study, done at a sleep center) is the gold standard for diagnosing OSA.","CPAP","Continuous Positive Airway Pressure — the mainstay treatment for OSA, and what's used most often.","Simple, works well, and cheap.","One constant pressure the whole time; no supplemental oxygen involved (though it may be added at home).","Pushes positive pressure into the airway to help it stay open on both inhalation and exhalation.","BiPAP","A little fancier and more expensive than CPAP; non-invasive (as opposed to the BiPAP used in the ICU).","Two pressures: one during inhalation, a lower one during exhalation — easier for the patient to push air out.","Works well for a patient who has trouble exhaling air, e.g. COPD.","Also used for neuromuscular disease and chest wall deformities.","One of the last steps before intubation — trying to prevent having to intubate and send the patient to the ICU.","An estimated 20–40% of patients with a CPAP/BiPAP order don't actually use it, because it's uncomfortable — ask about this and look for patient education opportunities. Other treatments: weight reduction, exercise, avoiding alcohol and smok…"]},{"s":"Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)▾","a":"tracheostomy-care-revisit","k":["Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)▾","A quick revisit, since trachs come up in med-surg — this is fair game for clinical and for exams.","Phalange — the plate on the outside.","Outer cannula — goes into the stoma; the inner cannula goes inside the outer cannula.","Obturator — used to reinsert a trach that's been accidentally coughed out. It has a point that helps ease entry into the stoma.","Shiley (plastic, white)","Has a cuff (can be inflated or not).","What a patient gets initially, fresh post-op — even if the trach will end up being long-term.","Disposable inner cannula — thrown away and replaced at trach care, not scrubbed.","Jackson (metal)","No cuff.","Switched to once the patient has had the trach long enough to be long-term.","Inner cannula is cleaned with a scrub brush, not thrown away.","Either type still needs its obturator taped to the bedside at all times.","Extend the patient's neck — Straightens the neck out to open the tissue.","Insert the obturator into the outer cannula, then insert into the stoma — The obturator's point facilitates entry into the stoma.","Remove the obturator — Leaving it in would obstruct the airway.","Reinsert the inner cannula — Completes the trach.","Check breath sounds and secure the trach — Confirms placement and keeps it from coming back out."]},{"s":"Acute Pharyngitis▾","a":"acute-pharyngitis","k":["Acute Pharyngitis▾","\"-itis\" always means inflammation — pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharynx and tonsils.","Viral — The most common type by far.","Bacterial (Streptococcal) — Only about 1 in 10 adults with a sore throat actually have bacterial pharyngitis. Sudden-onset sore throat, tonsillar hypertrophy (swollen, red, erythematous, tender tonsils), lymphadenopathy, and fever.","Fungal — Seen in patients on antibiotics — commonly called thrush, caused by candida albicans.","Viral and bacterial pharyngitis are difficult to tell apart by symptoms alone — a rapid strep test determines which one it is.","Only bacterial pharyngitis needs antibiotics. A patient with viral pharyngitis should not be put on antibiotics — inappropriate use drives antibiotic resistance.","Bacterial — Antibiotics.","Fungal (Thrush) — Nystatin — a swish-and-spit or swish-and-swallow solution.","Viral — Symptomatic care only: soothing treatments, analgesics, anti-pyretics."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient's partner reports loud snoring and pauses in breathing during sleep. Which diagnostic study should the nurse anticipate?","A Polysomnography","B Pulmonary function testing","C An overnight pulse oximetry trend","D A chest x-ray","A patient with obstructive sleep apnea and COPD reports difficulty exhaling against the current device. Which order should the nurse anticipate?","A Continuous positive airway pressure","B Supplemental oxygen by nasal cannula","C Bilevel positive airway pressure","D An oral appliance worn during sleep","A patient is admitted with posterior nasal packing in place. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","A Monitor respiratory status closely","B Provide humidification and oxygenation","C Maintain bed rest","D Provide oral care","E Encourage the patient to blow the nose hourly","F Administer aspirin for discomfort","A patient with mild obstructive sleep apnea asks what can be done besides using a device at night. Which instruction should the nurse include?","A Sleep on your back with an extra pillow.","B Have a small alcoholic drink before bed.","C Sleep on your side rather than your back.","D Limit all fluids for four hours before bed.","A nurse is admitting a patient with a new tracheostomy. Where should the obturator be kept?","A In the patient's bedside supply drawer","B In the clean utility room with trach supplies","C Attached to the patient's gown","D Taped at the head of the patient's bed","A patient presents with a spontaneous nosebleed and no history of trauma. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask?","A Do you take any blood-thinning medication?","B Have you recently traveled to a higher altitude?","C Have you had a recent upper respiratory infection?","D Is there a family history of frequent nosebleeds?","A patient with a posterior nosebleed has just had posterior packing placed. Which assessment is the priority?","A The patient's respiratory status","B The patient's reported pain level","C The condition of the oral mucosa","D The patient's oral fluid intake","A patient who recently completed a course of antibiotics reports a sore throat with white patches in the mouth. Which treatment should the nurse anticipate?","A A second course of oral antibiotics","B A rapid strep test before treatment","C Nystatin swish-and-swallow solution","D Antipyretics and increased fluids","A patient with a sore throat has a negative rapid strep test. Which treatment should the nurse anticipate?","A A course of oral antibiotics","B Nystatin swish-and-swallow solution","C A repeat rapid strep test in 48 hours","D Analgesics and antipyretics for comfort","Which cluster of findings would most suggest bacterial rather than viral pharyngitis?","A Gradual sore throat with clear drainage and no fever","B Sudden sore throat with tender enlarged tonsils and fever","C Chronic sore throat with hoarseness and weight loss","D Mild sore throat with white patches after antibiotic use","A patient with a CPAP order admits to using it only a few nights each week. Which response by the nurse is most appropriate?","A Ask what makes the device uncomfortable to use.","B Explain that the device must be used every night.","C Suggest the patient request a BiPAP machine instead.","D Reassure the patient that partial use is acceptable.","A patient's tracheostomy tube is accidentally dislodged. After extending the neck and inserting the outer cannula with the obturator in place, what should the nurse do next?","A Reinsert the inner cannula","B Secure the obturator to the bedside","C Auscultate the patient's breath sounds","D Remove the obturator from the tube","A nurse is teaching a patient with increased intracranial pressure who has just had a nosebleed. Which instruction is most important?","A Avoid blowing your nose while it heals.","B Apply ice to the back of your neck.","C Lie flat if the bleeding starts again.","D Rinse the nostrils with tap water.","A patient taking warfarin is admitted with a posterior nosebleed. Which medication should the nurse anticipate to reverse the anticoagulation?","A Protamine sulfate","B Vitamin K","C Aspirin","D Ibuprofen","Which findings in a patient's history are well-established risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea? Select all that apply.","A Obesity","B Male sex","C Increased age","D Nasal or pharyngeal structural abnormalities","E A history of seasonal allergies","F Female sex","A patient newly prescribed CPAP asks whether the machine will deliver oxygen. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","A The machine delivers a set percentage of oxygen.","B The machine delivers oxygen only during inhalation.","C The machine delivers oxygen only if apnea is detected.","D The machine delivers pressure, not supplemental oxygen.","A nurse is applying pressure to control an anterior nosebleed. Where should the pressure be applied?","A Over the bony bridge of the nose","B On the soft tissue just below the bony prominence","C At the base of the nostrils against the upper lip","D Along both sides of the nose at the inner canthus","A nurse is reviewing a sleep study report that documents episodes of hypopnea. What does this term describe?","A A complete cessation of breathing","B An increase in the depth of breathing","C A considerable slowing of breathing","D An irregular alternating breathing pattern","A patient in the clinic develops an anterior nosebleed. Which position should the nurse use?","A Supine with the head turned to one side","B Sitting upright with the head tilted backward","C Sitting upright and leaning slightly forward","D Side-lying with the head of the bed elevated","A nurse is performing tracheostomy care for a patient with a metal Jackson tube. Which action is correct?","A Discard the inner cannula and insert a new one","B Clean the inner cannula with a scrub brush","C Deflate the cuff before removing the inner cannula","D Keep the obturator in the unit supply room"]}]},{"f":"week1-head-neck-cancer.html","t":"Head & Neck Cancer","secs":[{"s":"Head & Neck Cancer","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Risk Factors & Presentation","Treatment","Laryngectomy — What Changes","Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy","Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","What makes head & neck cancer so devastating is as much about the physical modification as anything else — a patient can lose their mandible, tongue, or larynx, which affects body image as well as function. And the airway runs right through…"]},{"s":"Risk Factors & Presentation▾","a":"risk-factors-presentation","k":["Risk Factors & Presentation▾","Tobacco use causes 85% of head & neck cancers.","More common in men than women.","Most often diagnosed at > 50 years old.","If the patient is < 50, it's often associated with HPV (human papillomavirus) instead.","Most patients already have locally advanced disease by the time they're diagnosed."]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Surgery is the first line of treatment. Radiation and chemotherapy are also used, but the cancer-specific details of those are covered later, in oncology — not in this lecture.","Vocal Cord Stripping — A less extensive option than removing the larynx entirely.","Laryngectomy — Removal of the larynx (the voice box) — \"-ectomy\" means removal.","Tracheostomy — Often follows a laryngectomy — \"-ostomy\" means opening.","Lymph Node Removal / Neck Dissection — Done if the cancer has metastasized or spread into the muscle — may involve dissecting neck muscles like the sternocleidomastoid. Can be an extensive surgery depending on how far the cancer has spread."]},{"s":"Laryngectomy — What Changes▾","a":"laryngectomy-what-changes","k":["Laryngectomy — What Changes▾","Worth pausing on, because it's simple but easy to overlook: normally, food goes down the esophagus, and air comes in through the mouth or nose and down the trachea. After a laryngectomy, the larynx (and the connection between the airway and…","Eating is still possible — the GI tract connection (esophagus) is intact. There may be a temporary tube feeding while post-op swelling heals, but eating normally is the eventual goal.","Air now enters only through the stoma — never again through the nose or mouth.","When the patient coughs, sputum comes out through the stoma — nothing comes out of the nose or mouth, because they're no longer connected to the airway.","In a code situation, bag the stoma — not the mouth or nose. A patient with a laryngectomy no longer takes air in through their mouth or nose. Use a pediatric adapter (a smaller face mask that fits over the stoma) to bag them."]},{"s":"Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy▾","a":"restoring-communication-after-a-laryngectomy","k":["Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy▾","Artificial Larynx — A handheld device placed against the throat that provides vibration to produce speech. — Easy to use, can be used immediately after surgery — the most common option today. Can sound a bit robotic, but understandable.","Tracheoesophageal Voice Restoration — A valve device placed surgically that lets the patient talk. — —","Esophageal Speech — The patient sucks air into the esophagus, then forms words while \"burping\" the air back out. — Hands-free (no device needed) — but hard to learn, and can be difficult to understand depending on the patient's skill with i…"]},{"s":"Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care▾","a":"radical-neck-dissection-post-op-care","k":["Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care▾","A radical neck dissection removes part of the neck muscles — and possibly salivary glands or major blood vessels — because of how far the cancer has spread. It can be disfiguring; part of the neck may visibly cave in where tissue was remove…","Airway patency — priority #1 — There's a lot going on in the neck after this kind of surgery.","Humidify the trach collar / O2 — Normally the nose and mouth humidify air before it reaches the lungs; a new trach bypasses that, so add humidification until the patient adjusts to breathing without it.","Get the patient up and coughing regularly; suction as needed — Keeps the airway clear.","Blood-tinged sputum in the first couple of days = expected finding — Trauma from the surgery itself.","Pulmonary toilet: manage secretions, stoma care, and pain — Keeps the patient comfortable enough to participate in their own recovery.","Start tube feedings right away (sometimes even before surgery) — These patients are often malnourished pre-op (haven't felt like eating) — good nutrition enhances healing.","Physical therapy and speech therapy, post-operatively — —","Blood-tinged secretions as an expected post-procedure finding comes up again here — same principle as blood-tinged mucus after a bronchoscopy, review in Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics if needed"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient scheduled for a total laryngectomy asks whether they will ever eat normally again. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","A Eating by mouth will not be possible after this surgery.","B Eating by mouth will require a permanent feeding tube.","C Eating by mouth will depend on whether a trach is placed.","D Eating by mouth is expected once post-op swelling resolves.","A patient asks why lymph nodes are being removed during their cancer surgery. Which explanation should the nurse give?","A It is done routinely for all head and neck cancer surgeries.","B It is done to create space for a tracheostomy tube.","C It is done to reduce swelling after radiation therapy.","D It is done when the cancer has spread beyond the original site.","A patient newly diagnosed with head and neck cancer asks what treatment to expect first. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","A Chemotherapy is typically started first.","B Radiation is typically started first.","C Surgery is typically the first treatment.","D Treatment usually begins with observation.","A 42-year-old patient who has never used tobacco is diagnosed with head and neck cancer. Which statement best explains this presentation?","A Head and neck cancer under age 50 is often associated with HPV.","B Head and neck cancer is most often diagnosed before age 50.","C Tobacco use accounts for nearly all head and neck cancers.","D Head and neck cancer occurs more often in women than in men.","A patient is 3 days post-laryngectomy and wants to communicate with family. Which method can be used at this point?","A Esophageal speech","B Tracheoesophageal voice restoration","C An artificial larynx","D Speech using a cuffed tracheostomy tube","A patient returns to the unit after a radical neck dissection. Which nursing assessment takes priority?","A Patency of the patient's airway","B Output from the Jackson-Pratt drain","C Appearance of the surgical incision","D The patient's current nutritional status","A patient is considering esophageal speech after a laryngectomy. Which information should the nurse include?","A It requires a handheld device held against the neck.","B It requires a surgically implanted valve device.","C It requires no device but is difficult to learn.","D It requires an intact larynx in order to produce sound.","Which interventions should the nurse include in the post-operative plan of care after a radical neck dissection? Select all that apply.","A Humidify the tracheostomy collar or oxygen source","B Suction the airway as needed","C Begin tube feedings early in the recovery period","D Arrange physical therapy and speech therapy consults","E Maintain strict bed rest until the drain is removed","F Report blood-tinged sputum in the first 48 hours to the provider","Which method of restoring speech after a laryngectomy requires a surgical procedure?","A Esophageal speech using swallowed air","B An artificial larynx held to the neck","C Tracheoesophageal voice restoration","D Speech therapy without a device","A patient with a new tracheostomy has thick, tenacious secretions. Which intervention addresses the underlying cause?","A Increasing the frequency of suctioning","B Adding humidification to the oxygen source","C Repositioning the patient more frequently","D Deflating the tracheostomy cuff periodically","A patient with an early laryngeal lesion is scheduled for vocal cord stripping. What should the nurse understand about this procedure?","A It removes the larynx and creates a permanent stoma.","B It is less extensive than removing the entire larynx.","C It requires a tracheostomy in every case.","D It is performed only after radiation has failed.","A patient scheduled for a radical neck dissection has lost weight over the past 2 months. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?","A Beginning tube feedings before or immediately after surgery","B Delaying surgery until the patient regains the lost weight","C Restricting oral intake to clear liquids until surgery","D Providing high-calorie supplements only after discharge","Which statement reflects why head and neck cancer is often difficult to treat at the time of diagnosis?","A Most patients are diagnosed before age 50.","B Most tumors do not respond to surgical removal.","C Most patients decline treatment when it is offered.","D Most patients already have locally advanced disease.","On the second post-operative day after a radical neck dissection, a patient's sputum is blood-tinged. What is the nurse's interpretation of this finding?","A An expected result of surgical trauma","B An early indication of a wound infection","C A sign that a major vessel has eroded","D Evidence that suctioning has been too aggressive","A patient's operative report lists a laryngectomy followed by a tracheostomy. What do these two procedures indicate?","A The larynx was opened and the trachea was removed","B Both the larynx and the trachea were partially removed","C Both procedures created openings to bypass a tumor","D The larynx was removed and an opening was made in the trachea","A patient is withdrawn and refuses to look in the mirror after a radical neck dissection. Which nursing action is most appropriate?","A Remove the mirror from the patient's room.","B Acknowledge the change in appearance and stay present.","C Reassure the patient the appearance will fully return.","D Postpone the topic until discharge teaching.","Which statements should the nurse include when teaching a patient about life after a total laryngectomy? Select all that apply.","A Air will enter your lungs only through the stoma","B Coughed-up secretions will come out through the stoma","C You will be able to eat by mouth once swelling resolves","D Emergency responders must ventilate through your stoma","E You can still breathe through your nose if the stoma is blocked","F You will need a permanent feeding tube for nutrition","A patient who had a total laryngectomy is found unresponsive and apneic. How should the nurse deliver rescue breaths?","A With a bag-valve mask over the mouth and nose","B With a bag-valve device fitted over the stoma","C With a mouth-to-mouth seal and the nose pinched","D With a mask over the mouth, occluding the stoma","A community health nurse is planning education aimed at reducing head and neck cancer. Which topic should receive the greatest emphasis?","A Human papillomavirus vaccination","B Tobacco use and smoking cessation","C Recognizing symptoms before age 50","D Nutrition to prevent malnutrition","A family member asks why the patient's secretions come out of the neck opening when they cough. Which explanation should the nurse give?","A The stoma is the only remaining connection to the airway.","B Swelling is temporarily blocking the nose and mouth.","C The tracheostomy tube is diverting secretions upward.","D The esophagus and trachea have been surgically joined."]}]},{"f":"week1-lower-respiratory-problems.html","t":"Lower Respiratory Problems","secs":[{"s":"Lower Respiratory Problems","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","Pneumonia","COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)","Tuberculosis (TB)","Lung Cancer","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions"]},{"s":"Pneumonia▾","a":"pneumonia","k":["Pneumonia▾","Pneumonia is an acute infection of the lung parenchyma — the functional unit of the lung (e.g. the alveoli, where O2/CO2 exchange happens).","Risk Factors","Older age.","An altered level of consciousness or a weakened cough — either can put a patient at risk for aspiration pneumonia, since they can't effectively expectorate secretions.","Immobility / prolonged bed rest, a debilitating illness, malnutrition, and smoking.","Tracheal intubation — a patient on a ventilator in the ICU (this is why VAP is an ICU concept, not med-surg).","CAP (Community-Acquired) — Occurs in people who haven't been hospitalized, or who've been in a long-term care facility for <14 days of symptoms.","HAP (Hospital-Acquired) — Begins 48+ hours after admission — not present at the time of admission.","VAP (Ventilator-Associated) — HAP that develops in an intubated ICU patient on a ventilator. If the patient isn't intubated, it's classified as HAP instead.","Viral — The most common type. Usually mild/self-limiting (resolves in 3–4 days on its own) — but can still cause life-threatening problems.","Bacterial — Usually more serious than viral, and often requires hospitalization.","Aspiration — Abnormal entry of material from the mouth or stomach into the trachea/lungs, where it doesn't belong. Risk factors: decreased LOC, dysphagia (thin liquids are especially risky), and NG/feeding tubes. Can be silent — no vomiting…","Opportunistic — Occurs in immunocompromised patients (e.g. HIV) — covered further when opportunistic diseases come up later.","For a patient with an NG/feeding tube, placement matters: those tiny yellow feeding tubes (\"dobhoff\" tubes) should sit post-pyloric (past the pyloric sphincter). If a tube migrates back up out of the stomach and up the esophagus, the tube f…","In an elderly patient, watch for new-onset confusion as an early sign of infection — including pneumonia. It's often one of the first things you'll notice, sometimes before the more \"typical\" symptoms show up (the same pattern comes up agai…","Signs & Symptoms","Fever, chills, cough, malaise.","Pain with deep breaths and coughing — so the patient may avoid taking good deep breaths, which becomes its own problem.","Dyspnea (shortness of air), fatigue, myalgia (achy muscles).","Bacterial pneumonia: typically a productive, purulent cough. Viral: usually scanty or non-productive.","Severe cases can progress to respiratory distress and respiratory failure.","MNEMONIC: Leukocytosis = an increase in white blood cells (leukocytes) — seen with an acute bacterial infection. Leukopenia = a decrease. Easy to mix up on an exam — worth committing to memory on its own.","Chest X-ray shows infiltrates — Haziness where the film should be clear — suggests infection (e.g. in the left lower lobe).","CBC with diff shows leukocytosis, with a \"shift to the left\" — An increase in neutrophils — and specifically an increase in immature neutrophils (the mature ones normally circulate; immature ones get released early to help fight an acute ba…","Sputum culture & sensitivity is positive — Identifies the bacteria and which antibiotics will kill it. A viral infection won't grow any bacteria on culture — helps differentiate viral from bacterial.","Prevention & Nursing Care","Pneumococcal vaccine for anyone at risk or of the recommended age; don't smoke; balanced diet.","For a hospitalized patient: identify aspiration risk and act on it (positioning, diet texture, tube placement).","Turn, cough, deep breathe; ambulate; pulmonary mobilization — keeps secretions moving.","Strict hand hygiene and standard precautions.","Monitor vital signs and pulse ox regularly, and trend the data — a new fever or a dropping O2 sat is a meaningful change.","Listen to lung sounds at the start of your shift to set a baseline — so you can tell whether new crackles a few hours later are actually new.","Supplemental O2 as needed to keep pulse ox > 95%; incentive spirometer (very few contraindications — most patients should be using one, even if it's just sitting unused by the window); chest physiotherapy (percussion/postural drainage) to h…","Increase fluids to thin secretions, unless contraindicated (e.g. severe heart failure).","Energy conservation — bundle care for a weak patient with activity intolerance, but still get them up and moving.","Antibiotics if bacterial; analgesics/antipyretics for pain or fever; discharge teaching."]},{"s":"COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)▾","a":"copd-chronic-obstructive-pulmonary-disease","k":["COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)▾","COPD is an umbrella term covering chronic bronchitis and emphysema (asthma is technically an obstructive disease too, but it's covered separately in patho-pharm, not here). Most patients with COPD have some of both — collectively, this cour…","Emphysema","Alveolar damage — the alveoli get overinflated/stretched and don't work as well.","Impaired gas exchange at the alveolar level (where O2/CO2 actually exchange) — this is the critical piece.","Chronic Bronchitis","Different pathology than emphysema — its key feature is excessive secretion production.","COPD is preventable and treatable. The most common cause of an exacerbation (a flare-up) is a respiratory infection — a patient might get a mild cold, and the next thing you know they've developed a full flare requiring hospitalization. Dia…","Exam Findings","Barrel chest — years of working hard to exhale expands the thorax; the AP diameter moves from the normal 2:1 ratio toward 1:1.","Decreased/diminished breath sounds, often with adventitious sounds — wheezing, rhonchi, crackles (especially during an exacerbation).","Prolonged expiration — it takes longer to get the air out, because (unlike a normal passive exhale) they have to push it out.","Pursed-lip breathing in advanced disease — purses the lips while blowing air out, creating positive pressure between the lips and alveoli that helps push the trapped air out. It's a compensatory mechanism most patients learn on their own.","Advanced disease: neck vein distention and peripheral edema (from pulmonary hypertension — high blood pressure inside the lungs makes blood back up systemically) and cachexia (excessively malnourished — breathing simply takes an enormous nu…","Example from the lecture: a 63-year-old male with a history of COPD presented with 2 weeks of worsening cough, yellow sputum, and dyspnea; O2 sat was 87% in the ED. His chest X-ray showed pneumonia (infiltrates/consolidation), some pleural …","Nursing Problems to Anticipate","Activity intolerance — poor exercise tolerance; O2 sat may even drop while talking or eating.","Ineffective breathing pattern — too fast, too slow, too shallow, or too deep. Breathing too fast and deep is called hyperventilation.","Ineffective airway clearance — thick secretions the patient is too weak or too congested to cough up.","Impaired gas exchange — from the alveolar damage itself.","Anxiety — very common; patients can be jittery or agitated because they're having such a hard time breathing.","Poor nutritional status (see cachexia, above).","Smoking cessation — The single most effective, cost-effective intervention to reduce COPD risk and slow its progression.","Flu shot and other indicated vaccines — For high-risk patients.","Early detection/treatment of respiratory infections — Infections are the most common trigger for an exacerbation.","Inhaler therapy adherence — The mainstay of COPD self-management — patients who can't afford their medication or skip it are the ones who often end up hospitalized with an exacerbation.","Tripod positioning (leaning forward, hands on legs) — Expands the thorax and increases the surface area available for alveoli to exchange oxygen. Most patients adopt this on their own.","Pursed-lip breathing, huff coughing — Huff coughing: a series of small coughs followed by a hard cough, to help produce and expectorate secretions.","Energy conservation and relaxation techniques — —","Nutritional support — These patients burn an extraordinary number of calories just breathing.","Psychosocial support — Not being able to catch your breath is genuinely scary.","Long-Acting Beta Agonist (LABA) — Bronchodilates. A mainstay of treatment.","Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (LAMA) — Also bronchodilates.","Inhaled Steroids — Potent anti-inflammatories — decrease the inflammatory response and dilate the bronchi.","MDI (Metered Dose Inhaler)","What the patient is using at home, most of the time.","Nebulizer","Used when the patient is sick enough to be hospitalized.","Hooked up to a machine with humidification, with medication in it as well.","CO2 narcosis — never withhold oxygen from a hypoxic patient, even a \"CO2 retainer.\" A CO2 retainer is a COPD patient whose body has adapted, over a long period of time, to chronically high CO2 — so their stimulus to breathe becomes a low ox…","Exacerbation management: worsening dyspnea, cough, and sputum beyond the patient's typical day-to-day variation, requiring a change in treatment plan. The mainstay of treatment is oxygen to treat the hypoxemia — but always titrated with the…"]},{"s":"Tuberculosis (TB)▾","a":"tuberculosis-tb","k":["Tuberculosis (TB)▾","Caused by an infectious organism, Mycobacterium tuberculosis. If it's resistant to isoniazid (INH) and rifampin, it's called MDR-TB (multi-drug-resistant TB).","Risk Factors","Disproportionately affects poor, underserved, and minority populations.","IV drug users; people in crowded living situations or with less-than-ideal sanitation.","Immunosuppression, for any reason.","Primary TB — Bacteria is inhaled and triggers an inflammatory reaction; the immune system responds and encapsulates the organism — hopefully for the rest of the patient's life.","Latent TB — A persistent immune response to the bacterium, with no clinical manifestations of active disease. Asymptomatic and non-contagious — but will test positive on a skin or blood test. Can convert to active TB at any point, even year…","Active / Reactivated TB — Develops from latent TB in about 5–10% of patients, sometimes years afterward — risk is much higher in the elderly and immunosuppressed. This is the point at which the patient becomes contagious.","How the TB skin test and IGRA are actually done is covered on Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics — this page covers what the results mean, not how to give the test","Reading a TB skin test: come back 48–72 hours later and check for induration (a raised area) — not just redness (erythema). Redness alone isn't considered positive.","Healthy, no risk factors — ≥ 15 mm.","Higher-risk (immigrants, IV drug users, nursing home residents, young children) — ≥ 10 mm.","Immunosuppressed (HIV, recent contact with active TB, organ/lung transplant) — ≥ 5 mm.","If either the skin test or the IGRA is positive, the next step is a chest X-ray. A clear CXR (no Ghon nodule) means latent TB. A positive CXR (nodules noted) means the patient needs further testing to confirm active disease.","Isoniazid (INH) — Hepatotoxicity (along with acetaminophen — the two drugs in med-surg most associated with liver toxicity) and peripheral neuropathy (numbness/tingling in the fingers).","Rifampin — Turns excretions red or orange — including urine. Important to recognize this isn't hematuria (blood in the urine).","Ethambutol — Affects visual acuity and can cause difficulty differentiating between colors.","Strict adherence to drug therapy is essential — treatment for reactivated TB can run for months to years. For patients at high risk of non-adherence (e.g. a homeless patient), DOT (Direct Observational Therapy) means a provider directly sup…","Airborne precautions, negative-pressure room, HEPA mask — A negative-pressure room pushes air out rather than recirculating it to other patients' rooms — prevents aerosolized secretions from spreading. Staff must be fit-tested for the HEPA …","Home: prophylactic medication for high-risk household contacts — E.g. a TB-positive patient's spouse may also be started on medication.","Teach the patient to cover their mouth/nose when coughing and wear a mask in crowds — Reduces transmission.","Sputum for AFB every couple of weeks; 3 negative cultures = no longer infectious — Confirms the patient is no longer contagious with the reactivated version."]},{"s":"Lung Cancer▾","a":"lung-cancer","k":["Lung Cancer▾","One of the most prevalent lung cancers, particularly in Kentucky — worth its own voiceover. Often somewhat asymptomatic early on: 10% of the time it's discovered incidentally on an X-ray taken for some other reason. Symptoms are frequently …","Chest X-ray, CT, MRI, sputum for cytology, and the three ways to biopsy the lung are all covered on Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics — this page only covers what's specific to a lung cancer workup","Chest X-ray: may show a mass or an infiltrate (also called a consolidation, or just haziness).","CAT scan or MRI: used to assess for metastasis (spread).","Sputum for cytology: only about 20–30 out of 100 patients who actually have lung cancer will test positive on cytology alone.","A biopsy is the definitive diagnostic test — actual tissue, not just cells in the sputum.","Nursing Problems","Ineffective breathing pattern.","Anxiety — very common, compounded by the diagnosis and prognosis.","Malnutrition and fatigue.","Knowledge deficit — helping the patient understand the diagnosis and how to take care of themselves.","Collaborative care includes health promotion (smoking cessation) and anxiety reduction — much of nursing care in oncology is simply establishing rapport to help reduce a patient's anxiety.","Pneumonectomy — The entire lung is removed.","Lobectomy — Just a lobe is removed (the right lung has 3 lobes, the left has 2).","Wedge / Segmental Resection — An even smaller portion removed. Which option is used comes down to surgeon preference, depending on how the cancer has spread.","Radiation and/or chemotherapy are also often used in addition to surgery — the cancer-specific details of those are covered later, in oncology, not in this lecture.","Post-pneumonectomy assessment: expect no breath sounds on the side where the lung was removed — there's no lung there to move air. On a chest X-ray, that side will appear white instead of black, since air shows up black on X-ray and there's…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient is admitted with active tuberculosis. Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","A Place the patient in a negative-pressure room","B Wear a fit-tested HEPA mask when entering the room","C Teach the patient to cover the mouth and nose when coughing","D Anticipate prophylactic medication for household contacts","E Place the patient in a positive-pressure room","F Discontinue precautions once the patient becomes afebrile","Which intervention should the nurse emphasize as the most effective way to slow the progression of COPD?","A Adhering to prescribed inhaler therapy","B Receiving the annual influenza vaccine","C Stopping all tobacco use permanently","D Practicing energy conservation methods","A nurse is comparing the two conditions grouped under COPD. Which finding is most characteristic of chronic bronchitis?","A Overstretched alveoli that impair gas exchange","B A reduction in total lung capacity over time","C Excessive production of secretions","D Loss of the normal cough reflex","Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care for a patient with COPD? Select all that apply.","A Teach pursed-lip breathing and huff coughing","B Encourage tripod positioning during dyspnea","C Provide nutritional support","D Reinforce adherence to prescribed inhalers","E Restrict fluids to reduce secretion production","F Encourage a supine position to conserve energy","A patient admitted 3 days ago for a hip fracture develops a fever and a new infiltrate on chest x-ray. How should this pneumonia be classified?","A Community-acquired pneumonia","B Ventilator-associated pneumonia","C Hospital-acquired pneumonia","D Opportunistic pneumonia","A postoperative patient with pneumonia is reluctant to deep breathe because of pain. Which nursing action best addresses this?","A Postponing deep breathing until the pain resolves","B Substituting continuous oxygen for deep breathing","C Limiting activity to reduce the patient's oxygen demand","D Administering analgesia before pulmonary hygiene","A patient with COPD has an oxygen saturation of 84% and increased work of breathing. A nursing student suggests withholding oxygen because the patient may be a CO2 retainer. Which response by the nurse is correct?","A Oxygen is withheld until an arterial blood gas confirms retention.","B Oxygen is given and titrated to a target around 88 to 90 percent.","C Oxygen is given only if the saturation falls below 80 percent.","D Oxygen is withheld and noninvasive ventilation is used instead.","Which patient is at highest risk for aspiration pneumonia?","A A patient with a productive cough and a fever","B A patient recovering from a viral upper respiratory infection","C A patient using an incentive spirometer every hour","D A patient with dysphagia who drinks thin liquids","A patient's CBC with differential shows leukocytosis with a shift to the left. What does this indicate?","A A decrease in circulating white blood cells","B A release of immature neutrophils into blood","C A predominance of lymphocytes over neutrophils","D A viral rather than a bacterial process","A nurse auscultates a patient one day after a right pneumonectomy and hears no breath sounds over the right lung field. What is the nurse's interpretation of this finding?","A An expected finding after this procedure","B A finding that suggests a pneumothorax","C A finding that suggests mucus plugging","D A finding that requires immediate suctioning","An 82-year-old patient admitted for observation becomes newly confused overnight. Vital signs are within normal limits. Which action should the nurse take first?","A Assess the patient for an underlying infection","B Reorient the patient and recheck in an hour","C Request an order for a sedative at bedtime","D Raise all four side rails for patient safety","A patient with COPD is observed exhaling through pursed lips. What does this indicate to the nurse?","A The patient is experiencing acute respiratory distress.","B The patient has developed a compensatory breathing technique.","C The patient is attempting to increase the rate of breathing.","D The patient requires immediate bronchodilator therapy.","A patient with a long smoking history is being evaluated for lung cancer. Which symptom is most commonly reported?","A Coughing up blood with exertion","B A persistently productive cough","C Pleuritic chest pain at rest","D Unexplained loss of weight","A patient with a positive interferon gamma release assay has a clear chest x-ray and no symptoms. What should the nurse explain about this result?","A The patient has latent infection and is not contagious.","B The patient has active disease requiring isolation.","C The patient's test result was most likely a false positive.","D The patient will require no further follow-up.","A patient's sputum cytology is negative, but a chest x-ray shows a mass. What should the nurse tell the patient about the next step?","A The negative cytology rules out a malignancy.","B A repeat chest x-ray in 3 months is the next step.","C A biopsy is needed to establish the diagnosis.","D A CT scan alone can confirm the diagnosis.","A patient taking medication for tuberculosis calls the clinic alarmed that their urine is orange. Which response by the nurse is appropriate?","A This suggests bleeding and requires an immediate visit.","B This indicates the medication should be held until seen.","C This suggests the dose is too high and needs adjustment.","D This is an expected effect of one of your medications.","A patient taking isoniazid reports numbness and tingling in the fingers. What does the nurse recognize about this report?","A An early sign of medication resistance","B An expected effect that resolves in 48 hours","C A finding unrelated to tuberculosis therapy","D A known adverse effect of the medication","A nurse notes that a patient's anteroposterior chest diameter approaches a 1:1 ratio. Which finding does this describe?","A A barrel chest from years of air trapping","B A tripod position that expands the chest","C Cachexia from the calories breathing requires","D Prolonged expiration during forced exhalation","A patient with active TB is experiencing homelessness and has missed several clinic appointments. Which approach should the nurse anticipate?","A Shortening the course of therapy to improve completion","B Direct observational therapy for each dose","C Switching to a single-drug regimen for simplicity","D Delaying therapy until stable housing is arranged","A nurse reads a tuberculin skin test on a patient with HIV 60 hours after placement and measures 6 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","A Negative, because the induration is less than 10 mm","B Negative, because it was read more than 48 hours later","C Positive, because the patient is immunosuppressed","D Invalid, because erythema was not also measured"]}]},{"f":"week2-hematology-labs-diagnostics.html","t":"Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","secs":[{"s":"Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 1","The CBC — RBCs, WBCs & Platelets","RBC Morphology — Size, Shape & Hemoglobin Content","Hemoglobin & Hematocrit (H&H)","Iron Studies","Other Anemia Diagnostics","Anemia — Classification by Cause","Anemia — Severity & Why the Symptoms Happen","Self-Test Flashcards","The hematologic system covers three cell lines — red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets. This page focuses on RBCs: the labs used to evaluate them, and the terminology and classification system used across every ane…"]},{"s":"The CBC — RBCs, WBCs & Platelets▾","a":"the-cbc-rbcs-wbcs-platelets","k":["The CBC — RBCs, WBCs & Platelets▾","A CBC (complete blood count) looks at all three cell lines: RBC count, hemoglobin & hematocrit (H&H), and WBC count.","Normal WBC count: 5,000–10,000.","Normal platelet count: 150,000–400,000 (also called thrombocytes).","A differential (\"WBC with diff\") breaks the WBC count into the five types below — as percentages that always add up to 100%.","MNEMONIC — Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas: Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, Monocytes, Eosinophils, Basophils — the five WBC types on a differential, from most to least plentiful.","Leukopenia — Low WBC count.","Leukocytosis — High WBC count — seen with infection.","Neutropenia — Low neutrophil count. High-stakes: neutrophils are the first responders to an acute bacterial infection, so a neutropenic patient has little defense against one.","Thrombocytopenia — Low platelet count.","Shift to the left: in an acute bacterial infection, neutrophils are released fastest and rise first on the differential — including immature \"band\" neutrophils, not just mature ones (\"segs\"). A rising band count is the body releasing everyt…"]},{"s":"RBC Morphology — Size, Shape & Hemoglobin Content▾","a":"rbc-morphology-size-shape-hemoglobin-content","k":["RBC Morphology — Size, Shape & Hemoglobin Content▾","Morphology (an RBC's size, shape, and color under the microscope) is one of the two ways anemia is classified — the other is etiology (cause), covered further down this page. Providers use morphology to help narrow down the cause of an anem…","MNEMONIC — the bus analogy: the RBC is the bus; hemoglobin is the seats; iron is what the seats are made of; oxygen is the passenger sitting in the seat. O2 saturation = how full the buses are (want ≥95%). Hematocrit = the total volume of t…","MCV (Mean Corpuscular Volume) — Size of the RBC. — Normocytic (normal size) · Microcytic (small — e.g. iron deficiency anemia) · Macrocytic (large — e.g. megaloblastic anemias: B12 or folic acid deficiency)","MCHC (Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin Concentration) — How concentrated the RBC is with hemoglobin. — Normochromic (normal) · Hypochromic (low)","You won't be expected to memorize the actual MCV/MCHC numbers — just recognize and use the terms (hypochromic, normochromic, microcytic, macrocytic) the way they'd come up in clinical or on a question."]},{"s":"Hemoglobin & Hematocrit (H&H)▾","a":"hemoglobin-hematocrit-h-h","k":["Hemoglobin & Hematocrit (H&H)▾","Hemoglobin (Hgb) — The iron-containing pigment of the RBC. — Women: 12–16 · Men: 14–18","Hematocrit (Hct / \"crit\") — The percentage of total blood volume made up of RBCs. — Women: 37–47% · Men: 42–52%","Trend H&H, don't read it as a standalone value. Example: a 47-year-old male, post-op day 2 from a total colectomy, has a 7 AM H&H of 9.2/28.1. Alarming on its own — but if yesterday's H&H was even lower, this is trending upward, a reassurin…","False low hematocrit: a patient retaining fluid (heart failure, kidney failure) has extra plasma, which drops the hematocrit percentage without the patient actually being anemic.","False high hematocrit: a very dehydrated patient has too little plasma, which raises the hematocrit percentage without the patient actually having polycythemia (too many RBCs — its own topic, covered later this week)."]},{"s":"Iron Studies▾","a":"iron-studies","k":["Iron Studies▾","MNEMONIC: transferrin transports iron; ferritin stores it.","Serum Iron — Low in iron deficiency anemia.","Serum Transferrin — The protein that transports iron — decreased in iron deficiency anemia.","Serum Ferritin — The protein that stores iron. Decreased ferritin is the most sensitive test for iron deficiency anemia.","Iron studies are ordered when the cause of a patient's anemia is unknown — not when it's already explained (e.g. a patient who lost blood in surgery yesterday doesn't need a workup for why they're anemic)."]},{"s":"Other Anemia Diagnostics▾","a":"other-anemia-diagnostics","k":["Other Anemia Diagnostics▾","Intrinsic Factor Antibody Test — Checks for antibodies against intrinsic factor (a substance made by stomach parietal cells, needed to digest B12). Positive = pernicious anemia — an autoimmune cause of B12 deficiency. Not every B12 deficien…","Serum B12 & Serum Folate — Directly assess for B12 or folic acid deficiency.","Direct Coombs Test — Ordered when a transfusion reaction is suspected — checks whether incompatible blood was hung on the patient (a human error, and potentially life-threatening).","Indirect Coombs Test — A pre-transfusion compatibility screen (\"type and screen\") — the blood bank matches a sample of the unit against the patient's blood before it's ever given.","Stool for Guaiac (Occult Blood) — Detects blood in the stool that isn't visible to the naked eye — ordered when a GI bleed is suspected as the source of an anemia of unknown origin.","Bone Marrow Biopsy — Aspirates red marrow (commonly from the iliac crest) to evaluate blood-forming tissue directly, when RBC/WBC/platelet counts are low and the cause is unclear. Nursing implications: informed consent, timeout, pre-medicat…","EGD / Colonoscopy — Endoscopic visualization of the GI tract to look for a bleed. EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy, sometimes just \"gastroscopy\") scopes the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum from above; colonoscopy scopes from below.","Blood you can see in a bowel movement is called frank blood — no occult testing needed since it's already visible. Melena is stool that's dark, tarry, and unusually foul-smelling from digested blood — also a significant finding, and a commo…"]},{"s":"Anemia — Classification by Cause▾","a":"anemia-classification-by-cause","k":["Anemia — Classification by Cause▾","Anemia is classified by morphology (covered above) or by etiology — the underlying cause. There are three buckets.","Decreased RBC Production — Nutritional deficiency (iron, B12/cobalamin, folic acid) · chronic kidney disease (↓ erythropoietin, which the kidney makes to drive RBC production) · liver disease (↓ iron availability) · aplastic anemia (bone ma…","Blood Loss — Acute (trauma, ruptured vessel/aneurysm) — sudden, no time to compensate, cardiac instability develops almost instantly. Chronic (e.g. heavy menstrual flow) — insidious, better tolerated even at a low hemoglobin because the bod…","Increased RBC Destruction — Sickle cell anemia (reviewed from Peds) · a hemolytic transfusion reaction from incompatible blood products","Megaloblastic anemias (B12 or folic acid deficiency) fall under decreased production — impaired DNA synthesis makes RBCs abnormally large with fragile membranes, so they're also destroyed easily."]},{"s":"Anemia — Severity & Why the Symptoms Happen▾","a":"anemia-severity-why-the-symptoms-happen","k":["Anemia — Severity & Why the Symptoms Happen▾","Mild — 10–14 — May be asymptomatic or mildly fatigued with exertion.","Moderate — 6–10 — Cardiopulmonary symptoms, possibly even at rest.","Severe — < 6 — Symptomatic at rest; can be life-threatening, especially with comorbidities.","Fatigue — Not enough oxygen reaching the muscles to produce energy.","Pallor (skin, palms, nail beds, conjunctiva) — Peripheral vasoconstriction shunts the blood that is available toward vital organs (heart, lungs) and away from the periphery.","Tachycardia / palpitations — Cardiac output rises as a compensatory mechanism, trying to circulate what oxygen is available faster.","Bone pain (sternum, iliac crest) — Erythropoietin secretion rises, driving the red marrow to work harder.","Angina, MI, worsening heart failure — The heart muscle itself isn't getting enough oxygen.","Dyspnea, increased respiratory rate, hypoxia — The body's attempt to bring in more oxygen to compensate.","Angina/MI, worsening heart failure, and hypoxia/dyspnea are the findings that can turn life-threatening — especially in a patient with existing cardiac or pulmonary comorbidities, which most hospitalized patients have. A hemoglobin under 6 …","Worth writing down — comes up repeatedly all semester: CO = HR × SV (cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume, the amount of blood ejected from the left ventricle per beat). A floor nurse can't measure cardiac output directly, so blood p…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-anemias-polycythemia.html","t":"Anemias & Polycythemia","secs":[{"s":"Anemias & Polycythemia","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 2","Iron Deficiency Anemia","B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency Anemia","Folate Deficiency Anemia","Aplastic Anemia","Nursing Problems & Collaborative Care","Medications for the Anemic Patient","Nutrition Teaching","General Nursing Care","Polycythemia","Self-Test Flashcards","The anemia types below all fall under decreased RBC production, one of the three causal categories introduced on the Hematology Labs & Diagnostics page. This page also covers the nursing care shared across anemia types, and finishes with po…"]},{"s":"Iron Deficiency Anemia▾","a":"iron-deficiency-anemia","k":["Iron Deficiency Anemia▾","Who gets it: inadequate intake (unable to eat, anorexia, limited financial means), GI absorption problems, or blood loss (menstruation, hemolysis).","Morphology: microcytic, hypochromic.","If chronic, the patient may have no symptoms at all — same acute-vs-chronic pattern as any anemia.","Pallor — The most common symptom. Check the conjunctiva — pull down the lower eyelid and look for a clear line of demarcation between the pale inner lid and the red, capillary-rich tissue. A pale patient loses that line.","Glossitis — An inflamed tongue — red, smooth, and sore. Caused by epithelial atrophy.","Cheilitis / Angular Cheilitis — Inflammation of the lips, especially at the corners of the mouth.","Brittle hair & spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia) — Signs of more severe, longer-standing anemia — not something that develops quickly.","Pica / Pagophagia — Pica is craving non-food items; pagophagia is specifically craving ice. Not every patient gets this, but it's a classic, distinctive finding.","Severe, longstanding iron deficiency anemia has driven patients to swallow genuinely dangerous non-food objects (nails, screws, and similar) — an extreme expression of pica, and a reminder to take the symptom seriously when a patient report…"]},{"s":"B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency Anemia▾","a":"b12-cobalamin-deficiency-anemia","k":["B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency Anemia▾","Who gets it: lack of intrinsic factor (secreted by gastric parietal cells, required to absorb B12) — most commonly from pernicious anemia, an autoimmune disease. Also: bowel surgery, malabsorption, strict vegan diet, current/chronic alcohol…","Of these causes, only pernicious anemia is autoimmune — the others are mechanical or dietary.","Morphology: megaloblastic — large cells (macrocytic), normochromic. The membrane is fragile despite the larger size.","Manifestations are insidious: GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain, glossitis, cheilitis).","What makes B12 deficiency unique: it's the only anemia on this page with neuromuscular manifestations — paresthesia (numbness/tingling in the fingers and toes, often the first thing a patient notices) and ataxia (uncoordinated, off-balance,…","Pernicious anemia must be treated for life — it's autoimmune and doesn't resolve. Contrast that with a vegan patient's B12 deficiency, which can resolve once the diet changes."]},{"s":"Folate Deficiency Anemia▾","a":"folate-deficiency-anemia","k":["Folate Deficiency Anemia▾","Who gets it: usually decreased intake, not an absorption problem — pregnancy (high demand) or alcohol abuse (poor diet) are the classic causes.","Morphology: microcytic, normochromic.","How to tell folate deficiency apart from B12 deficiency: the presentation is very similar and also insidious, but folate deficiency does not cause the neurologic problems (paresthesia, ataxia) that B12 deficiency does. Assessing for those n…","Flagged for verification: this course's lecture states folate deficiency's morphology as microcytic, normochromic — worth double-checking against the slide deck if you see it described elsewhere (e.g. a textbook) as macrocytic, since folate…"]},{"s":"Aplastic Anemia▾","a":"aplastic-anemia","k":["Aplastic Anemia▾","Cause: usually idiopathic (unknown) — sometimes suspected to be autoimmune, but nothing definitive.","What makes it different: aplastic anemia affects all three cell lines at once — RBCs, WBCs, and platelets. That combination is called pancytopenia.","Morphology: normocytic, normochromic — the cells themselves look normal, there just aren't enough of them.","Diagnosed with a bone marrow biopsy — same procedure and nursing implications already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","Why aplastic anemia looks different from the other anemias on this page: because all three cell lines drop, the patient is at risk for fatigue (low RBCs), infection (low WBCs — neutropenia), and bleeding (low platelets — thrombocytopenia) —…"]},{"s":"Nursing Problems & Collaborative Care▾","a":"nursing-problems-collaborative-care","k":["Nursing Problems & Collaborative Care▾","Fatigue — Tired even at rest, without any activity.","Activity Intolerance — Feels okay at rest, but doesn't tolerate getting up and moving — heart rate rises, palpitations, anxiety, shortness of breath. Probably the most common complication seen with anemia.","Nutrition is its own nursing problem when intake or absorption of iron, B12, or folate is inadequate. And a patient with aplastic anemia has three nursing problems layered on top of each other in the same person: risk for bleeding (thromboc…","Malnutrition — Increase intake.","Alcohol abuse — Support cessation; supplement.","Absorption issue (e.g. pernicious anemia) — Mega-dose PO B12 or parenteral (IM) injections.","Blood loss — Find and stop the source of bleeding.","Finding a bleeding source uses the same workup already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics — stool for guaiac, EGD, colonoscopy"]},{"s":"Medications for the Anemic Patient▾","a":"medications-for-the-anemic-patient","k":["Medications for the Anemic Patient▾","Iron (Ferrous Sulfate) — Preferred oral form. Once-daily dosing is now considered acceptable (older regimens gave it 3–4x/day). Absorbed in the duodenum — no enteric-coated or extended-release formulations. Best absorbed in an acidic enviro…","B12 (Cobalamin / Cyanocobalamin) — Can be given PO, IM, sub-Q, or intranasally. IM is preferred if the patient has severe neurologic symptoms. If some intrinsic factor is still present, evidence shows a PO mega-dose (1,000 mcg, of which onl…","Folic Acid — Given PO. No significant adverse effects. Especially important during pregnancy.","Epoetin (synthetic erythropoietin) — Used for chronic kidney disease and cancer patients who can't make their own erythropoietin. Given IV or sub-Q, roughly 3x/week — often together with iron, since RBC production needs both. Carries a blac…","Iron turning the stool black is expected, not melena. Melena (digested blood in the stool) is dark, tacky/sticky, and very malodorous. Iron-darkened stool is just dark from the iron itself — it doesn't have that same malodorous, sticky qual…","Why give iron and epoetin together: think buses and wheels — erythropoietin drives RBC production (the wheels), and iron builds the hemoglobin inside them (the seats). You need both for the system to actually work."]},{"s":"Nutrition Teaching▾","a":"nutrition-teaching","k":["Nutrition Teaching▾","Iron — A frequent NCLEX/HESI select-all-that-apply topic — know which foods are actually iron-rich.","B12 — Found only in animal products — this is exactly why strict vegans are at risk without supplementation.","Folic Acid — Green leafy vegetables, legumes, grains, orange juice, nuts.","Whether vitamin C actually enhances iron absorption is mixed in the current literature, but the course's teaching point is to give vitamin C alongside iron."]},{"s":"General Nursing Care▾","a":"general-nursing-care","k":["General Nursing Care▾","Trend lab values — Same principle as trending H&H rather than reading a single value in isolation.","Monitor vital signs and O2 saturation; assess for hypoxemia — Anemic patients have fewer \"buses\" available to carry oxygen.","Assess for neurologic changes — Especially important with B12 deficiency, given its unique neuromuscular manifestations.","Supplement oxygen as needed — Extremely anemic patients often need help getting oxygen from point A to point B.","Provide rest periods — Addresses activity intolerance and fatigue directly.","Keep the patient warm — but no heating pads — Anemic patients tend to feel chilly, but a heating pad risks a burn if the patient also has decreased sensation (paresthesia).","Anticipate a possible PRBC transfusion — Packed red blood cells — covered in more depth on the Blood Component page."]},{"s":"Polycythemia▾","a":"polycythemia","k":["Polycythemia▾","Polycythemia is an abnormally high H&H / RBC count — the opposite problem from everything covered so far on this page. There are three types.","Relative Polycythemia — Decreased plasma volume / dehydration — the same false-high hematocrit already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics. Not a true increase in RBCs, and the patient is not actually anemic or polycythemic.","Primary (Polycythemia Vera) — A genetic change acquired during the person's lifetime.","Secondary — A compensatory response to chronic tissue hypoxia — most commonly COPD, or long-term life at high altitude. This is the most common cause of polycythemia overall.","Relative polycythemia is the same false-high hematocrit concept already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","Quick-check ratio: hematocrit runs roughly 3× the hemoglobin — e.g. a hemoglobin around 12 pairs with a hematocrit around 36. Useful for a fast sanity check on whether two values line up.","Hypertension, headache, difficulty concentrating — Thicker, higher-volume blood causes congestion.","Ruddy (dusky red) complexion; possible cyanosis — Related to the excess of RBCs.","Angina/MI, TIA/stroke — Decreased perfusion from thick, sluggish blood — stroke is the most common serious acute complication.","Night sweats, weight loss — A hypermetabolic state from the increased blood viscosity and volume.","Pruritus (itching), worse after a hot bath — A classic sign, from the sheer increase in RBC number.","Pain in the fingers and toes — —","Increased clotting risk runs through this whole disease. Thick, high-volume blood predisposes to both clot formation and hemorrhage — this is why perfusion to the heart (angina/MI) and brain (TIA/stroke) are real risks, and why anticoagulan…","Phlebotomy is the mainstay treatment for chronic polycythemia — removing 300–500 mL of blood every other day initially, tapering to every few months as the volume comes down. Goal hematocrit: <42% in women, <45% in men.","Good hydration is an important teaching point — the blood is thick and viscous.","Anticoagulants may be used given the clotting risk.","Mobility is not automatically encouraged the way it usually is — the increased clotting risk cuts against the usual \"get up and move\" default.","Antihistamines (e.g. diphenhydramine) for pruritus.","Polycythemia vera is not preventable and is chronic — the patient needs ongoing evaluation and support for a disease that doesn't go away."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-blood-component-therapy.html","t":"Blood Component Therapy","secs":[{"s":"Blood Component Therapy","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 3","Blood Types & Compatibility","PRBCs — Pre-Transfusion Responsibilities","Platelets, FFP & Albumin","Responding to a Suspected Transfusion Reaction","Types of Transfusion Reactions","Leukocyte-Reduced Products","Minimizing Reaction Risk — Autologous Donation & Autotransfusion","Self-Test Flashcards","Four blood products come up regularly: PRBCs, platelets, FFP, and albumin — each for a different problem. The second half of this page covers what can go wrong during a transfusion, and how to respond."]},{"s":"Blood Types & Compatibility▾","a":"blood-types-compatibility","k":["Blood Types & Compatibility▾","Two antigen systems determine blood type: ABO (A, B, both, or neither) and Rh (present or absent).","A patient can only receive blood that doesn't contain an antigen they lack — receiving an antigen they don't have triggers a hemolytic reaction.","O negative = universal donor (no A, B, or Rh antigens to react against).","AB positive = universal recipient (already has every antigen, so nothing in a donor's blood is foreign).","Expect select-all-that-apply-style questions here — e.g. \"the patient is AB negative, which blood types can they receive?\" You don't need the exact population percentages (O+ and A+ are the most common types), just the logic of which antige…","Before a transfusion, the patient gets a type and cross-match — the cross-match step is the same indirect Coombs test already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics"]},{"s":"PRBCs — Pre-Transfusion Responsibilities▾","a":"prbcs-pre-transfusion-responsibilities","k":["PRBCs — Pre-Transfusion Responsibilities▾","< 6 — Transfusion recommended except in extenuating circumstances.","6–7 — Depends on symptoms (low BP, compensatory high heart rate).","7–8 — Appropriate for surgical patients (expected blood loss) or patients with heart disease (anemia + heart disease risks angina/MI).","8–10 — Usually only with serious comorbidities — oncology patients, MI/ACS risk, or active bleeding.","> 10 — Transfusion is uncommon outside exceptional cases.","Type & cross-match must be current within 72 hours — re-draw if it's older than that.","Confirm positive ID, signed consent, and patient teaching are done.","Pre-medication is sometimes ordered: Tylenol if at risk for fever, Benadryl if at risk for itching.","IV access: 20 gauge or larger (18 gauge is ideal). Avoid smaller gauges — they can shear and hemolyze the cells.","Special Y-tubing with an in-line filter is required — used only for blood products. Prime with normal saline first, then switch the roller clamp to the blood once it arrives; switch back to saline once the unit is in, to flush every last dr…","Normal saline only — never hang dextrose or lactated Ringer's with blood, which can cause hemolysis.","Typical volume: 250–350 mL per unit of PRBCs.","Timing is strict and non-negotiable. You have 30 minutes to start the transfusion once blood leaves the blood bank — if you can't make that window, it goes back to the blood bank, not the floor or a medication fridge. Once started, the enti…","Before starting — Final patient identity check at the bedside — textbook answer is two RNs (UK Healthcare uses barcode scanning for the second check).","First 15–30 minutes — The RN stays at the bedside the entire time to monitor for a reaction — this cannot be delegated to a tech or a student.","15 minutes in — Reassess vital signs to check for any concerning trend before increasing the rate.","Every hour until complete — Continue vital sign checks (roughly a 2-hour infusion, unless the patient needs it slowed further).","End of transfusion — Final vital signs and documentation.","Teach the patient what to expect and what to report: itching, chills, shortness of breath, or anything that \"feels weird\" — the blood itself is cold, so feeling cold at the IV site is expected, but flank/back pain is not and should be repor…"]},{"s":"Platelets, FFP & Albumin▾","a":"platelets-ffp-albumin","k":["Platelets, FFP & Albumin▾","Platelets — Thrombocytopenia — often chemotherapy patients. Threshold is roughly <20,000 (normal is 150,000). — Prepared from whole blood. Small bags (30–60 mL); multiple units (\"a six-pack\") are common. Stored at room temperature, good for…","FFP (Fresh Frozen Plasma) — Clotting factor deficiency — liver disease, hemophilia, Von Willebrand's. Not the same problem as thrombocytopenia, even though both affect clotting. — The plasma (serous) layer, rich in clotting factors (e.g. pr…","Albumin — Low serum albumin — e.g. a liver patient losing fluid into the interstitium (third spacing). — A colloid — has oncotic (\"pooling\") power that pulls fluid back into the intravascular space. Comes in different concentrations (5% is …","Third spacing was already introduced in Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics — fluid sitting somewhere it doesn't belong and serves no purpose"]},{"s":"Responding to a Suspected Transfusion Reaction▾","a":"responding-to-a-suspected-transfusion-reaction","k":["Responding to a Suspected Transfusion Reaction▾","Stop the transfusion immediately — First priority, regardless of which reaction it turns out to be.","Prime a new bag and tubing with normal saline; switch it in at the same IV site — Keeps the line open without pushing residual blood already in the old tubing into the patient — simply flipping back to an old saline line doesn't remove the …","Assess the patient and get vital signs — Determines severity and which reaction type this likely is.","Notify the provider and the blood bank — The blood bank will want to recheck everything to rule out a hang-up on their end or nursing's.","Save the blood bag, tubing, and filter; send back to the blood bank — Needed for their investigation, especially if a hemolytic reaction is suspected.","Monitor urine output closely — Especially important if a hemolytic reaction is suspected — the kidneys are what's at risk.","Complete a transfusion reaction report; collect any specimens the lab wants — Documentation requirement."]},{"s":"Types of Transfusion Reactions▾","a":"types-of-transfusion-reactions","k":["Types of Transfusion Reactions▾","Acute Hemolytic — The patient got incompatible blood — the most life-threatening type. Facial flushing, fever, headache, low back pain (kidneys), possible hematuria, dyspnea, dropping BP, cardiac arrest. — Support blood pressure (colloids/a…","Febrile Non-Hemolytic — Temperature rises gradually over the infusion (not immediate) — no back pain, no hematuria, no hemodynamic compromise. — Notify the provider; usually Tylenol and resume. Reduced by leukocyte-reduced products; a patie…","Mild Allergic — Sensitization to donor WBCs — pruritus, flushing, urticaria (hives). The most common transfusion reaction overall. — Stop the blood, notify the provider — diphenhydramine or a steroid, then usually a slow restart. A patient …","Severe Allergic / Anaphylactic — Anxiety, urticaria, respiratory compromise, bronchospasm, cardiovascular compromise, shock, possible cardiac arrest. — Treated as a code: CPR, oxygen, epinephrine, antihistamines/steroids, possibly a beta-2 …","Bacterial Sepsis — Rapid-onset chills, high fever, vomiting, diarrhea, marked hypotension. — Obtain blood cultures, stop and return the unit, treat with antibiotics, IV fluids, and a vasopressor if needed (often in the ICU). Prevented by in…","TACO (Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload) — Typically an older patient with heart failure given blood too quickly — dyspnea, crackles, increased respirations, headache, distended neck veins. — This is the one reaction where you slo…","Supporting blood pressure during a hemolytic reaction uses the same principle already covered in Hematology Labs & Diagnostics — BP as an indirect measure of cardiac output"]},{"s":"Leukocyte-Reduced Products▾","a":"leukocyte-reduced-products","k":["Leukocyte-Reduced Products▾","Leukocytes are naturally collected along with whole blood during donation and are considered a contaminating cellular component — they're linked to febrile non-hemolytic and allergic reactions. Removing them (pre-storage leuko-reduction) lo…"]},{"s":"Minimizing Reaction Risk — Autologous Donation & Autotransfusion▾","a":"minimizing-reaction-risk-autologous-donation-autotransfusion","k":["Minimizing Reaction Risk — Autologous Donation & Autotransfusion▾","Autologous donation: a patient pre-donates their own blood ahead of a scheduled surgery, stored (frozen, it lasts years) for use during that procedure. Guarantees no reaction, since it's the patient's own blood — but it's fallen out of favo…","Autotransfusion (cell salvage / \"cell saver\"): still commonly used. During surgery — open heart surgery is a good example — blood lost at the surgical site is collected, processed through the machine, and reinfused into the same patient rat…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-central-venous-catheters.html","t":"Central Venous Catheters","secs":[{"s":"Central Venous Catheters","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 4","Why a Patient Needs a CVC","Non-Tunneled (\"Deep\") Lines","Tunneled CVCs","Implantable Ports (Portacath / PowerPort)","PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter)","General CVC Complications & CLABSI","Removing a Non-Tunneled CVC or PICC (RN Scope)","Who Removes Which Line","Nursing Student Scope of Practice","Self-Test Flashcards","A CVC (central venous catheter) is any line whose tip lies in a large central vessel — usually the superior vena cava. There are four kinds, and the differences between them (how long they stay in, who can remove them, what a student can an…"]},{"s":"Why a Patient Needs a CVC▾","a":"why-a-patient-needs-a-cvc","k":["Why a Patient Needs a CVC▾","TPN (total parenteral nutrition): a very hypertonic IV solution (10–20% dextrose, vs. 5% in a standard solution) that peripheral veins can't tolerate.","Vesicant chemotherapy agents: drugs that cause serious tissue damage if they infiltrate — a central line avoids that risk. Many chemo patients also need long-term access, which a CVC provides.","Long-term antibiotic therapy — e.g. weeks of IV antibiotics for endocarditis.","Loss of peripheral access — a patient who's been stuck repeatedly with nothing left but small, fragile veins (a 24-gauge that blows the next day)."]},{"s":"Non-Tunneled (\"Deep\") Lines▾","a":"non-tunneled-deep-lines","k":["Non-Tunneled (\"Deep\") Lines▾","Placed by the provider or an acute care nurse practitioner — not tunneled, so it's essentially a large-vessel IV stick.","Inserted in the subclavian vein or the internal jugular (IJ).","Up to 5 ports possible (2–3 is typical). 7–10 inches long. Tip usually rests in the superior vena cava.","A chest X-ray is done after insertion — to confirm placement and rule out a pneumothorax.","Short-term use only — not intended for home or ambulatory-clinic use.","Can be removed by the RN.","Subclavian vs. IJ: subclavian risks nicking the pleura and causing a pneumothorax; IJ is a more direct shot to the heart and carries less of that risk, but the dressing tends not to stay put because the neck is so mobile. Neither is definit…"]},{"s":"Tunneled CVCs▾","a":"tunneled-cvcs","k":["Tunneled CVCs▾","Placed by the surgeon, usually in the OR — tunneled through the subcutaneous tissue so the exit site is further down the chest than the actual insertion point, which adds stability and lowers the chance of accidental removal.","Named types: Hickman, Groshong, Broviac.","Used for frequent or prolonged infusion therapy — commonly seen on the oncology floor and for dialysis access. Can stay in for a long time.","Advantage: no repeated needle sticks. Disadvantage: a prolonged break in skin integrity (ongoing infection risk).","Irrigation and site-care protocols vary by agency policy.","Discontinued by the provider only — the RN does not remove a tunneled CVC."]},{"s":"Implantable Ports (Portacath / PowerPort)▾","a":"implantable-ports-portacath-powerport","k":["Implantable Ports (Portacath / PowerPort)▾","Surgically implanted entirely under the subcutaneous tissue of the chest wall — no external tubing when not accessed, which patients often prefer for body image (some describe it as feeling like \"a button\" under the skin).","Accessed with a special Huber needle through the round \"donut\" septum.","Rated for up to 2,000 punctures in the chest, or 750 if placed in the arm, before it wears out.","Used for patients needing IV therapy for a year or longer.","When accessed, the needle is changed weekly; when not accessed, there's essentially no site care needed.","Discontinued by the surgeon, via a small incision to remove the port."]},{"s":"PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter)▾","a":"picc-peripherally-inserted-central-catheter","k":["PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter)▾","Inserted peripherally — typically in the antecubital space — but it's still a central line, because the catheter is threaded up the vessel until the tip lies in the superior vena cava.","8–10 inches long. Placed at the bedside by specially trained PICC nurses. Can stay in place for several months.","No blood draws or blood pressures in the arm with the PICC — risks impairing the infusion or damaging the catheter.","Lower complication risk than a deep line overall — no pneumothorax risk, no unstable neck dressing.","Dressing changes follow a set schedule per agency policy (e.g. done every Wednesday unless soiled).","Complications: phlebitis, catheter occlusion.","Can be removed by the RN."]},{"s":"General CVC Complications & CLABSI▾","a":"general-cvc-complications-clabsi","k":["General CVC Complications & CLABSI▾","Shared risks across CVC types: bleeding at the site, air embolus, catheter occlusion/clot formation, and infection.","Pneumothorax is specific to deep lines (subclavian especially), from the insertion process itself.","CLABSI (central line-associated bloodstream infection) — bacteria enters at the insertion site; accounts for a large share of hospital-acquired infections and can make a patient critically ill (sepsis, even death).","Dressings help prevent CLABSI: a stat-lock dressing secures the catheter and reduces the wiggling that widens the insertion site; a CHG (chlorhexidine gluconate)-impregnated dressing is used specifically to reduce infection risk."]},{"s":"Removing a Non-Tunneled CVC or PICC (RN Scope)▾","a":"removing-a-non-tunneled-cvc-or-picc-rn-scope","k":["Removing a Non-Tunneled CVC or PICC (RN Scope)▾","Check the INR before pulling — Especially important if the patient is anticoagulated — rules out excessive bleeding risk.","Gather supplies, including a measuring tape — After removal, the catheter is measured to confirm the full length came out — nothing was left behind in the vessel.","Position the patient supine or in Trendelenburg — Positioning that supports the Valsalva maneuver and reduces air-embolism risk.","Have the patient bear down (Valsalva maneuver) as it's pulled — Prevents atmospheric air from being sucked into the large vessel during removal.","Apply pressure for 3–5 minutes, then an occlusive dressing — Standard post-removal site care.","Measure the catheter length removed; compare to the documented insertion length — Confirms nothing broke off and was left in the patient.","Culturing the catheter tip after removal varies by agency — some facilities do it routinely, others rarely. A PICC is withdrawn the same careful way, but slower — inch by inch, since pulling too fast can trigger venous spasm."]},{"s":"Who Removes Which Line▾","a":"who-removes-which-line","k":["Who Removes Which Line▾","Non-tunneled (deep line) — RN","PICC — RN","Tunneled (Hickman, Groshong, Broviac) — Provider only","Implantable port — Surgeon only"]},{"s":"Nursing Student Scope of Practice▾","a":"nursing-student-scope-of-practice","k":["Nursing Student Scope of Practice▾","At UK Healthcare, nursing students are not permitted to provide direct care of a CVC — this policy exists specifically for CLABSI-bundle compliance. As a student, you may not: access or flush a port, push medications through a central line,…","One exception: a student may hang a secondary line (e.g. a medication) through an already-established primary line, with the instructor or RN present. You'll perform full CVC care once you're a licensed nurse — these limits exist for patien…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-legal-ethical-issues.html","t":"Legal & Ethical Issues in Nursing","secs":[{"s":"Legal & Ethical Issues in Nursing","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 1","Scope of Practice & the Nurse Practice Act","HIPAA","Patient Self-Determination Act, Advance Directives & Living Wills","Informed Consent","DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) Orders","Other Legal Acts to Know","Ethics Fundamentals","Professional Nursing Code of Ethics","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Fundamentals content, tested on the first exam alongside Week 1 & 2 material. Covers the legal acts every nurse needs to know, plus a brief intro to ethics."]},{"s":"Scope of Practice & the Nurse Practice Act▾","a":"scope-of-practice-the-nurse-practice-act","k":["Scope of Practice & the Nurse Practice Act▾","Scope of practice is a legal act that defines nursing and reflects the profession's values — regulated by the American Nurses Association (ANA) and other professional organizations. It shapes what nursing schools teach and what the NCLEX te…","The Joint Commission (which accredits hospitals) requires institutions to have written, accessible scope-of-practice policies — so scope can vary by institution. Example from lecture: at Baptist Health Lexington, nurses can pull arterial sh…","The Nurse Practice Act is the set of state laws governing licensure. Every nurse must be licensed in the state they work in — including travel nurses.","Compact licensure: a multi-state agreement that lets a nurse licensed in one participating state practice in another participating state without retesting or extra paperwork. Not every state participates — know your own state's status."]},{"s":"HIPAA▾","a":"hipaa","k":["HIPAA▾","The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act establishes a patient's right to privacy over their health information and records.","Before HIPAA, patients did not automatically have access to their own medical record. HIPAA established that patients own their medical record and can always request access to it.","HIPAA also limits who else can access a patient's information (e.g. a friend who works as a nurse on the floor cannot look up your chart without a care-related reason) and defines what counts as protected health information (PHI).","The lecture mentions there are 11 categories of protected health information worth knowing for the NCLEX-style questions, but didn't list them out loud on the recording — worth checking your textbook or slide deck for the exact list before …","Privacy Rule — Health information must be kept private; it's the hospital's responsibility to keep personal health records private.","Security Rule — Practical safeguards — e.g. firewalls, not leaving charts in an unlocked cabinet — that keep health information inaccessible to the public.","Breach Notification — If a breach of access occurs, the hospital must notify the affected patients.","Safety / Research Use — Identifiable PHI used to analyze and improve patient safety (e.g. in research or quality improvement) must still be protected."]},{"s":"Patient Self-Determination Act, Advance Directives & Living Wills▾","a":"patient-self-determination-act-advance-directives-living-wills","k":["Patient Self-Determination Act, Advance Directives & Living Wills▾","The Patient Self-Determination Act (PSDA) requires healthcare institutions to give patients written information about their right to make decisions about their own care — including the right to refuse treatment and to formulate an advance d…","It's broader than end-of-life care alone, but in practice it's most often applied to advance directives. Patients must be offered information about advance directives when they're admitted.","At minimum, an advance directive must state the patient's wishes about respiratory and cardiac arrest, and name their durable power of attorney for healthcare — the person who makes care decisions on their behalf if they become incapacitate…","The central legal/ethical question: is the patient currently decisionally capacitated (able to make their own care decisions)? This connects directly to informed consent, covered next.","A living will is a specific type of advance directive that goes into more detail about end-of-life preferences — often including do-not-resuscitate wishes and whether the patient wants enteral (tube) feeding if they can't eat or drink on th…","A patient must have decision-making capacity to create either an advance directive or a living will. If they've lost capacity before completing one (e.g. advanced dementia, or sedated after a traumatic injury), it's too late — care decision…"]},{"s":"Informed Consent▾","a":"informed-consent","k":["Informed Consent▾","Informed consent is an explanation of a procedure or treatment, required before every procedure performed at a hospital.","It must include the names and qualifications of everyone performing or assisting — the attending physician's name must be on the form even if a fellow or resident is actually doing the procedure.","It must describe possible complications, including serious harm up to and including death, and anticipated pain or discomfort.","It must state that the patient has the right to refuse the procedure or treatment.","If the patient is incapacitated (e.g. an emergency), consent authority falls to whoever holds their durable power of attorney for healthcare, per their advance directive."]},{"s":"DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) Orders▾","a":"dnr-do-not-resuscitate-orders","k":["DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) Orders▾","A DNR order does not mean \"do not care.\" It means only that if the patient's breathing or heart stops, the team will not begin CPR. Every other aspect of care continues as normal.","A DNR can be stated in a living will or an advance directive, and it can be revoked at any time — a patient can change their mind.","There is legal complexity around whether a durable power of attorney for healthcare can override a patient's own standing DNR order if the patient becomes incapacitated — this is the kind of situation that involves hospital legal counsel.","As the nurse, you need to know if your patient has a DNR order — check the chart, and be aware some facilities also use bracelets or door signage per their own policy."]},{"s":"Other Legal Acts to Know▾","a":"other-legal-acts-to-know","k":["Other Legal Acts to Know▾","EMTALA — Anyone presenting to an emergency department must be treated — regardless of insurance status, ability to pay, immigration status, or criminal history.","ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) — Protects the rights of people with physical or mental disabilities; can affect where and what kind of care a patient receives.","Mental Health Parity & Addiction Equity Act — Requires health insurance companies to provide at least some coverage for mental health and substance use treatment.","OBRA (Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act) — Protections for older adults, particularly in nursing homes — governs the legal use of physical and chemical restraints.","Good Samaritan Law — Limits liability and offers legal immunity for care given at the scene of an accident — but only for actions within your scope of practice (e.g. you can start CPR or a tourniquet, but you can't start a central line on t…","Public Health Laws — Laws intended to improve the health of individuals, populations, and communities (e.g. vaccine mandates) — controversial at times, but they do affect nursing practice.","Uniform Determination of Death Act — Establishes the legal criteria for what is considered death.","You're unlikely to be tested on fine detail for most of these — the instructor's guidance was to be able to talk about each in a general sense if asked, with HIPAA's protected health information being the one exception worth knowing more pr…"]},{"s":"Ethics Fundamentals▾","a":"ethics-fundamentals","k":["Ethics Fundamentals▾","Ethics stem from values — a deeply held personal belief about the worth of an idea, attitude, custom, or object that affects a person's choices and behavior. Values usually form during childhood and early adulthood, starting from family inf…","An ethical dilemma occurs when two opposing courses of action can both be justified by ethical principles — it almost always arises from conflicting values, and happens more often in healthcare than most people expect.","Values clarification is the process of resolving an ethical dilemma by distinguishing your own values from the facts of the situation, other people's opinions, and what you're actually being asked to do.","Moral distress is when a nurse feels the need to take a specific action while believing that action may be wrong. Lecture example: caring for a Jehovah's Witness patient (a faith that prohibits receiving blood products) after cardiac surger…","Common sources of ethical conflict in healthcare: social media, quality of life at the end of life, and access to healthcare (see Box 22.5 in your textbook for detailed examples)."]},{"s":"Professional Nursing Code of Ethics▾","a":"professional-nursing-code-of-ethics","k":["Professional Nursing Code of Ethics▾","As a licensed nurse, you commit to a professional code of ethics built on four pillars: advocacy (for the nursing profession and your patients), responsibility (practicing responsibly), accountability (owning your actions as a nurse), and c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nurse is reviewing an informed consent form with a patient prior to surgery. Which elements should the nurse confirm are present on the form before the patient signs? Select all that apply.","A The names and qualifications of everyone performing or assisting","B A statement of possible complications, up to and including death","C A description of the anticipated pain or discomfort involved","D A statement that the patient has the right to refuse the procedure","E An itemized estimate of the total cost of the procedure","F A guarantee that the procedure will resolve the underlying condition","During intake, a patient completes an advance directive that names their spouse as durable power of attorney for healthcare but does not document any wishes regarding resuscitation. Which statement best describes this document?","A The advance directive is invalid because a living will must also be filed for it to take legal effect.","B The advance directive is incomplete, because it must also state wishes about respiratory and cardiac arrest.","C The advance directive is complete, because naming a durable power of attorney satisfies all requirements.","D The advance directive needs resuscitation wishes only if the patient is admitted for cardiac or respiratory problems.","A hospital discovers that an unauthorized employee accessed 40 patients' electronic health records without a care-related reason over several months. Per HIPAA, what is the hospital required to do?","A Report the breach only if the employee involved is not terminated.","B Report the breach to the state board of nursing rather than to the patients.","C Withhold notification until an investigation determines whether patients were harmed.","D Notify the affected patients that a breach of their information occurred.","A patient has a documented DNR order in the chart. The patient develops a wound infection unrelated to their code status, and the nurse notes a new order for IV antibiotics. What is the nurse's best action?","A Administer the antibiotics as ordered, since the DNR order applies only to CPR.","B Hold the antibiotics and clarify with the provider whether they conflict with the DNR.","C Administer the antibiotics only after obtaining new informed consent for the DNR patient.","D Contact the ethics committee before giving any non-comfort intervention to a DNR patient.","An RN who is trained and hospital-privileged to insert central lines is driving home and comes upon a car accident. A bystander is in severe respiratory distress. Under the Good Samaritan Law, which action is covered by legal immunity?","A Inserting a central line at the scene, since the nurse is trained and licensed to perform this skill in a hospital.","B Performing any intervention the nurse judges necessary, since Good Samaritan Law grants immunity for actions taken with good intent regardless of training.","C Declining to act at all, since Good Samaritan Law only protects nurses who formally identify themselves as off-duty medical professionals.","D Starting CPR or applying a tourniquet, since Good Samaritan protection applies only to actions within the nurse's actual scope of practice.","A nurse who personally holds strong religious objections to elective pregnancy termination is assigned to provide post-procedure care for a patient who chose to end a pregnancy for medical reasons. Before entering the room, the nurse delibe…","A Informed consent, since it involves the patient's right to make decisions about her own body.","B Values clarification, which separates one's own values from the facts, others' opinions, and the request.","C The Patient Self-Determination Act, since it establishes the right to refuse or choose treatment.","D An ethical dilemma, since the facts support two opposing courses of action that are both justified.","A hospital unit installs a new firewall on its electronic health record system and begins locking the medication room's paper chart cabinet at all times. These actions are best described as fulfilling which HIPAA component?","A The Security Rule, which requires practical safeguards that keep health information inaccessible to the public.","B The Privacy Rule, which makes the hospital responsible for keeping personal health records private in all circumstances.","C The Breach Notification requirement, which mandates protective measures after a breach has already occurred.","D The Safety/Research Use provision, which protects identifiable PHI used in quality-improvement studies.","While riding in a hospital elevator, a nurse overhears two colleagues discussing a patient's diagnosis by name where other passengers can hear. Which pillar of the professional nursing code of ethics is most directly at risk of being violat…","A Confidentiality — keeping what is learned as a nurse private.","B Accountability — owning one's own actions as a nurse.","C Advocacy — advocating for the nursing profession and patients.","D Responsibility — practicing responsibly as a nurse.","A newly admitted patient asks the nurse why they were handed a packet of information about advance directives and the right to refuse treatment during the admission process. Which explanation by the nurse best reflects the purpose of the Pa…","A It is optional information hospitals provide only when a patient asks about their rights.","B Hospitals must give every patient written information about their right to make care decisions.","C It is required only for patients over 65 or those admitted with a terminal diagnosis.","D It documents that the patient completed a living will prior to admission to the facility.","A nurse who has worked for several years at Baptist Health Lexington, where nurses are permitted to pull arterial sheaths, transfers to UK Healthcare, where this task is outside the RN scope of practice at that facility. A physician on the …","A Perform the procedure, since arterial sheath removal is within RN scope of practice nationally.","B Decline the procedure and consult this institution's written scope-of-practice policy first.","C Perform the procedure only if the physician remains at the bedside for the entire removal.","D Refuse and report the physician to the state board of nursing for an out-of-scope request.","The nurse is reviewing several federal laws that affect healthcare practice. Which of the following statements are accurate? Select all that apply.","A The ADA can affect where a patient with a disability receives care and what kind of care is given.","B The Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act requires insurers to provide unlimited mental health coverage.","C OBRA governs the legal use of physical and chemical restraints for older adults in nursing homes.","D Public health laws like vaccine mandates aim to improve individual, population, and community health.","E The Uniform Determination of Death Act limits legal determination of death to brain-death criteria.","F The ADA primarily affects how hospitals handle insurance billing and reimbursement procedures.","A patient's chart includes a document specifying that if the patient is ever unable to eat or drink on their own at the end of life, they do not want a feeding tube placed. This level of detail is most characteristic of which document?","A Informed consent form, since it must describe the specific risks and benefits of placing a feeding tube.","B Durable power of attorney for healthcare, since it names the person authorized to decide about tube placement.","C A living will, since it spells out end-of-life preferences such as whether the patient wants tube feeding.","D The Patient Self-Determination Act, since facilities must inform patients about feeding-tube options at admission.","An RN holds a license issued by a state that participates in the nursing licensure compact and accepts a new position in another compact-participating state. According to compact licensure rules, what must the nurse do before starting work …","A Apply for a temporary compact permit valid for the first 90 days of employment.","B Retake the NCLEX in the new state to validate current licensure and competency.","C Nothing further is required, since the compact license permits practice in the new state.","D Submit proof of continuing education credits to the new state's board of nursing.","A patient with no health insurance and an active warrant for arrest arrives at the emergency department reporting chest pain. The triage nurse recognizes the patient from a prior visit where the bill went unpaid. Which action reflects the r…","A The patient must be evaluated and treated regardless of insurance status, ability to pay, or legal history.","B The patient may be triaged as lower priority until insurance coverage or ability to pay is confirmed.","C The patient must be transferred to a public hospital before treatment, given the unpaid balance on file.","D The patient can be treated only after law enforcement is notified of the patient's active warrant.","A nurse is caring for a Jehovah's Witness patient recovering from cardiac surgery who is hemorrhaging and would clinically benefit from a blood transfusion. The patient has clearly and competently refused blood products based on religious b…","A An ethical dilemma, because two ethical principles are in direct conflict for the nurse.","B Moral distress, because the nurse must take a specific action that feels ethically wrong.","C Values clarification, because the nurse is separating personal values from the patient's wishes.","D A breach of informed consent, because the patient was not informed of the risk of death.","A patient is admitted after a severe traumatic brain injury and is unconscious and unable to communicate. The patient never completed an advance directive or living will prior to the injury. Who will make care decisions for this patient?","A Decisions will fall to next-of-kin laws or whatever documentation already exists, since the patient lost capacity before completing a directive.","B The attending physician will make all decisions unilaterally, without input from family, until the patient regains capacity.","C The hospital ethics committee automatically assumes full decision-making authority whenever no advance directive exists in the chart.","D No treatment of any kind can be provided until the patient regains full decisional capacity to consent personally.","A nurse who works on a medical-surgical unit is not currently assigned to care for a particular patient, but the patient is a personal friend. The nurse wants to look up the friend's admission diagnosis and recent labs out of curiosity. Acc…","A Access the chart, since the nurse is a licensed healthcare employee of the same hospital.","B Access the chart, but only view the lab results, since diagnoses require separate authorization.","C Not access the chart, because access to protected health information requires a care-related reason.","D Access the chart, but document the reason for looking as \"personal interest\" in the audit log.","A patient with a documented DNR order tells the nurse, \"If my heart stops, I want you to do everything to bring me back.\" The patient is alert, oriented, and able to communicate clearly. What is the nurse's priority action?","A Continue following the existing DNR order until the physician formally rescinds it in writing.","B Ask the patient's durable power of attorney for healthcare to confirm the change, since only they can revoke a standing DNR.","C Tell the patient that a DNR order cannot be changed once it has been signed and filed in the chart.","D Notify the provider immediately so the DNR order can be revoked, since a DNR can be revoked by the patient at any time.","A new nurse notices that their strong belief in the importance of family involvement in end-of-life decisions strongly shapes how they counsel patients, and traces this belief back to how they were raised as a child. This belief is best des…","A An ethical dilemma, since it involves two opposing courses of action that are both ethically justified.","B Moral distress, since the nurse feels compelled to act in a way that may conflict with the patient's wishes.","C Values clarification, since the nurse is separating personal beliefs from the objective facts of the case.","D A value — a deeply held personal belief that shapes a person's choices, attitudes, and behavior.","A travel nurse holds an active RN license in State A and is completing a 13-week contract in State B, which does not participate in the nursing licensure compact. Which statement best reflects the legal requirement for this nurse?","A The nurse may practice in State B under the State A license because travel contracts are exempt from state licensure requirements.","B The nurse only needs to notify State B's board of nursing of intent to practice there.","C The nurse must obtain licensure in State B, since every nurse must be licensed in the state in which they are practicing.","D The nurse may practice in State B for up to 90 days before licensure there is required."]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-nursing-process-sbar.html","t":"Nursing Process, Clinical Judgment & SBAR","secs":[{"s":"Nursing Process, Clinical Judgment & SBAR","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 2","The Nursing Process","The Clinical Judgment Model (CJM)","SBAR — Preparing to Call","SBAR — The Four Components","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Three ways of thinking that show up constantly: the classic 5-step nursing process, the newer Clinical Judgment Model (CJM) built on top of it, and SBAR — the structured way to communicate a problem to a provider."]},{"s":"The Nursing Process▾","a":"the-nursing-process","k":["The Nursing Process▾","A conceptual/theoretical framework created in the 1950s by the National Collegiate Boards of Schools of Nursing (the same body that creates the NCLEX) to teach nurses how to think — to guide safe, competent, quality patient care.","Assessment: gathering information — not just the physical exam (subjective + objective findings), but also the medical record (the H&P is usually the best place to start), other chart documents, and labs/diagnostics. Be comprehensive; this …","Diagnosis: identifying and labeling the patient's problem — not the medical diagnosis. A patient with pneumonia might have the nursing problem \"ineffective airway clearance,\" not \"pneumonia.\" You'll be given a list of common nursing problem…","Planning: setting goals and desired outcomes, collaboratively with the patient/family where possible. Goals must be SMART — specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound. Example: nursing problem = constipation → goal = \"patient wi…","Implementation: performing the planned nursing interventions — assessing/monitoring, actually implementing (e.g. giving a stool softener, encouraging ambulation), collaborating with the care team, teaching the patient/family, and addressing…","Evaluation: going back to the goals and outcome criteria and asking — were they met, partially met, or not met?"]},{"s":"The Clinical Judgment Model (CJM)▾","a":"the-clinical-judgment-model-cjm","k":["The Clinical Judgment Model (CJM)▾","Introduced in 2019, after research found about 50% of nurses were involved in some kind of error, and roughly 65% of those errors traced back to poor clinical judgment. The CJM was built to teach that skill more explicitly — it doesn't repl…","Recognizing cues — identifying relevant clinical data (aligns with Assessment). A cue is anything you observe or become aware of that points toward a problem.","Analyzing cues — interpreting and clustering cues into patterns that link to a clinical problem (aligns with Diagnosis). Example: a patient who is tachypneic, short of air, and has a small arm laceration — the tachypnea and shortness of air…","Prioritizing hypotheses — deciding which problem is the priority, using ABC (airway, breathing, circulation) as the guiding framework. In the example above, the respiratory issue outranks the skin laceration.","Generating solutions — setting goals/outcomes and identifying what resources are needed (collaboration, equipment, medications).","Taking action — implementing the plan (performing a skill, giving a med, collaborating, teaching).","Evaluating outcomes — were the goals met, partially met, or not met?","Also called the Clinical Judgment Measurement Model (CJMM) — same thing. The key idea to remember: the nursing process's single \"Planning\" step becomes two CJM steps (Prioritize Hypotheses, then Generate Solutions), and \"cues\" is the CJM's …"]},{"s":"SBAR — Preparing to Call▾","a":"sbar-preparing-to-call","k":["SBAR — Preparing to Call▾","SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) is a structured communication technique for talking to a provider about something unexpected happening with a patient — designed to convey a lot of information succinctly.","This course focuses on the problem-based SBAR (used when you need to call a provider about a concern) rather than the patient-focused version (used for shift-to-shift handoff report) — problem-based is what you'll be tested on.","Five steps to prepare an SBAR, before you ever pick up the phone:","1. Identify the problem and its urgency — Is this important enough to call right now, or can it wait until rounds? This focuses what information you need to gather.","2. Do a focused assessment — Gather only information relevant to the problem — e.g. for a suspected broken ankle, level of consciousness, breathing, and the ankle itself (open fracture? pain level?) matter; unrelated details like BMI don't.","3. Think through relevant medical history — What in the patient's history could play a role in this specific problem? (E.g. for a fall: history of osteoporosis, prior fractures, arthritis.)","4. Look for trend data — Check provider/nursing notes and the admission H&P if time allows — helps you skim more effectively and understand the fuller picture.","5. Gather critical cues — Pull together the admission reason, allergies, meds, labs/diagnostics, and physical assessment findings that help tell the story — not every SBAR needs every category.","The most common mistake new nurses make: including everything you know about the patient \"just in case,\" instead of only what's directly relevant to why you're calling. That makes the story harder to follow, not easier. The whole call shoul…"]},{"s":"SBAR — The Four Components▾","a":"sbar-the-four-components","k":["SBAR — The Four Components▾","Situation — Only the symptoms that made you pick up the phone, plus who you are and where the patient is. Brief and specific. — \"This is Mary, a nursing student from 5 North, calling about Joe Brown in room 531 — admitted two days ago for a…","Background — What contributed to the problem — this is where your current assessment data actually goes (not in the \"Assessment\" section, despite the name). Takes 30–60 seconds if you know your critical cues. — \"His lips are cyanotic — new.…","Assessment — What you think is going on — one or two possibilities. It's okay to be uncertain, and okay to offer a guess as a newer nurse. — \"I'm concerned Mr. Brown may be having a pulmonary embolism — it doesn't appear pain-related, since…","Recommendation — Exactly what you want done, by whom, and by when — as specific as possible. This is the one section where more back-and-forth with the provider is normal. — \"I'd like to start him on supplemental oxygen by face mask — do yo…","If a provider's response doesn't feel urgent enough for how concerning the situation is, it's appropriate to push back: \"I appreciate what you're saying, but I'm very concerned this patient is deteriorating — are you able to come to the bed…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nursing student is preparing to notify the on-call provider about a patient whose oxygen saturation just dropped from 93% to 88%. Which version of SBAR should the student use, and why?","A Patient-focused SBAR, because it is designed to summarize the patient's overall status for a shift-to-shift handoff.","B Patient-focused SBAR, because it is the version emphasized for provider notification calls in this course.","C Problem-based SBAR, because it is used when calling a provider about a specific, unexpected concern.","D Problem-based SBAR, because it replaces the need for a focused assessment before calling.","A nursing student calling a provider begins with: 'This is Mary, a nursing student from 5 North, calling about Joe Brown in room 531, admitted two days ago for a total knee replacement — I'm calling because he has sudden shortness of air an…","A Background — it establishes the patient's relevant surgical history before describing findings.","B Situation — it identifies the caller, the patient, and the symptoms prompting the call.","C Assessment — it offers the caller's working impression of what is happening.","D Recommendation — it states specifically what action the caller wants taken.","The nurse is preparing to call the provider about a patient who fell and may have a fractured wrist. Following the recommended steps for preparing an SBAR call, which pieces of information should the nurse gather before picking up the phone…","A Level of consciousness, breathing status, and a focused assessment of the injured wrist","B Whether the patient has a history of osteoporosis, prior fractures, or arthritis","C The patient's calculated BMI from the admission history and physical, in case the provider asks","D Trends from recent provider and nursing notes, and the admission H&P if time allows","E The patient's admission reason, allergies, current medications, and relevant labs or diagnostics","F A full head-to-toe review of every system, regardless of relevance to the fall","While giving an SBAR report, the nurse asks the provider, 'Would you like me to order a stat CT scan, or would you prefer to come evaluate him first?' and the provider asks several clarifying questions in return. What does this exchange rep…","A Normal, expected back-and-forth for the Recommendation section of SBAR.","B A sign the nurse's Situation statement was unclear and needs to be restated.","C A breach of SBAR structure that should be redirected to a yes/no request.","D An indication the call should have waited until rounds instead.","A nursing instructor explains that the nursing process was developed decades before the Clinical Judgment Model existed. Which statement about its origin and purpose is accurate?","A It was created in the 1950s by the National Collegiate Boards of Schools of Nursing to teach nurses a structured way to think.","B It was created by the American Nurses Association in the 1980s to replace the Clinical Judgment Model.","C It was created by the National Collegiate Boards of Schools of Nursing in the 1990s specifically to reduce documentation errors.","D It was created by individual hospital systems in the 1950s and later standardized into the NCLEX exam blueprint.","A nurse educator explains why the Clinical Judgment Model (CJM) was introduced in 2019. Which statement best reflects the rationale behind it?","A Research found that about 65% of nurses were involved in an error, and 50% of those errors were due to poor communication with providers.","B Research found that about 50% of new graduate nurses failed the NCLEX on their first attempt due to weak assessment skills.","C Research found that about 65% of medication errors were caused by nurses skipping the Evaluation step of the nursing process.","D Research found that about 50% of nurses were involved in some kind of error, and roughly 65% of those errors traced back to poor judgment.","A student compares the 5-step nursing process to the 6-step Clinical Judgment Model and notices one extra step. Which nursing process step expands into two separate CJM steps, and what are they called?","A Assessment splits into Recognize Cues and Analyze Cues.","B Planning splits into Prioritize Hypotheses and Generate Solutions.","C Diagnosis splits into Analyze Cues and Prioritize Hypotheses.","D Implementation splits into Generate Solutions and Take Actions.","The nurse is implementing the care plan for a patient with constipation whose goal is to have a bowel movement by the end of the shift. Which of the following actions are appropriate parts of the Implementation step? (Select all that apply.…","A Administering an ordered stool softener (a dependent intervention)","B Encouraging the patient to ambulate in the hallway (an independent intervention)","C Asking the patient how they are coping emotionally with the hospital stay","D Comparing the patient's stool to the outcome criteria to see if the goal was met","E Writing the SMART goal statement for the constipation care plan","F Continuing to monitor the patient's bowel sounds and abdominal status","A nurse notices a patient's blood pressure is slightly below their baseline, but the patient is asymptomatic and stable. Before doing anything else, what is the primary purpose of the first step in preparing an SBAR call?","A To gather every piece of the patient's history in case the provider asks.","B To decide which SBAR component to skip based on time constraints.","C To decide whether the problem is urgent enough to call now or can wait.","D To determine which nurse on the unit should make the call.","A patient is admitted with a medical diagnosis of pneumonia and has a persistent, weak, non-productive cough. During the Diagnosis step of the nursing process, which nursing diagnosis should the nurse select?","A Pneumonia","B Ineffective airway clearance","C Impaired gas exchange secondary to pneumonia","D Risk for infection related to pneumonia","A nurse calls a provider about a patient with new-onset chest pain and low oxygen saturation, but the provider says it can wait until morning rounds. The nurse remains very concerned that the patient is deteriorating. What should the nurse …","A Document the provider's response and reassess the patient again at the start of the next shift.","B Restate the concern directly and ask the provider to evaluate the patient at the bedside now.","C Wait 30 to 60 minutes to see if the patient's condition changes before contacting anyone else.","D Repeat the full SBAR to a different provider without informing the original provider or the charge nurse.","The nurse is writing a care plan for a patient with the nursing problem of constipation. Which of the following is the best example of a properly written SMART goal for this patient?","A Patient will improve bowel function during hospitalization.","B Patient's abdomen will remain soft and non-tender throughout the stay.","C Patient will have a bowel movement by the end of the shift.","D Patient will understand the importance of fiber and fluids for bowel health.","The nurse assesses a patient who is tachypneic, visibly short of air, and has a small superficial laceration on the forearm. Using the Clinical Judgment Model, how should the nurse cluster these findings and decide what to prioritize?","A Cluster the tachypnea and shortness of air together as a respiratory problem, and rank it above the laceration by the ABC framework.","B Cluster all three findings together as a single traumatic injury pattern and treat the laceration first because it is actively bleeding.","C Cluster the tachypnea and laceration together as a single stress response, and address the shortness of air only after the wound is dressed.","D Treat all three findings as equally weighted cues that must be addressed strictly in the order they were observed.","A nurse completes all ordered treatments, monitors vital signs, and collaborates with physical therapy for a postoperative patient, but never asks how the patient is coping emotionally with a new diagnosis. Which component of the Implementa…","A Performing the ordered dependent interventions.","B Collaborating with the interdisciplinary care team.","C Addressing the patient's psychosocial needs.","D Reassessing and monitoring the patient's response.","For a patient with the nursing problem of constipation and the goal 'patient will have a bowel movement by the end of the shift,' which pair of statements represents appropriate outcome criteria for that goal?","A 'Patient understands the causes of constipation' and 'patient verbalizes a bowel regimen.'","B 'Patient ambulates in the hallway twice' and 'patient tolerates a regular diet.'","C 'Patient's pain is well controlled' and 'patient is free of nausea.'","D 'Soft, non-tender abdomen' and 'a moderate-sized, soft, formed stool.'","At the end of a shift, the nurse compares the patient's actual bowel movement to the goal and outcome criteria that were set. Which set of terms correctly describes the possible results of this Evaluation step?","A Achieved, deferred, or discontinued.","B Met, partially met, or not met.","C Resolved, unresolved, or reassessed.","D Confirmed, pending, or ruled out.","A newly admitted patient's nurse begins the Assessment step of the nursing process. Which source of information should the nurse consult first to get the most complete initial picture, before moving on to other chart documents and diagnosti…","A The History and Physical (H&P), as it's typically the best place to start.","B The most recent nursing shift note, since it reflects the patient's current status.","C The medication administration record, since it shows what has already been given.","D The most recent lab values, since abnormal labs typically drive the nursing diagnosis.","During an SBAR call, the nurse states the patient's current oxygen saturation, heart rate, and respiratory rate. Under which SBAR component does this information actually belong, and what is commonly misunderstood about it?","A Assessment — students often mistakenly place current vital signs in Background instead.","B Situation — students often mistakenly place current vital signs in Recommendation instead.","C Recommendation — students often mistakenly place it under Situation because both open the call.","D Background — students often mistakenly expect it under 'Assessment' because of the section's name.","After deciding that a patient's respiratory distress is the priority problem, the nurse next identifies that supplemental oxygen equipment and a respiratory therapy consult will be needed, and sets a goal for the patient's oxygen saturation…","A Analyzing cues.","B Prioritizing hypotheses.","C Taking action.","D Generating solutions.","During hand-off, a nurse notices a patient's skin is unusually pale and cool to the touch but isn't yet sure what it means. In Clinical Judgment Model terms, what has the nurse just done?","A Analyzed a cue, by linking the finding to a specific clinical problem.","B Prioritized a hypothesis, by ranking the finding against other findings.","C Recognized a cue, by noticing a finding that points toward a possible problem.","D Generated a solution, by identifying what resources the finding will require."]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-obesity-metabolic-syndrome.html","t":"Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 3","Classifying Obesity","Contributors to Obesity","Health Risks of Obesity","Nursing Assessment & Communication","Nursing Interventions: Lifestyle & Nutrition","Metabolic Syndrome","Nursing Interventions for Metabolic Syndrome","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Obesity is an excessively high amount of body fat/adipose tissue — a major risk factor for many leading causes of death (heart disease, type 2 diabetes, several cancers). Over 40% of U.S. adults are considered obese, with the highest preval…"]},{"s":"Classifying Obesity▾","a":"classifying-obesity","k":["Classifying Obesity▾","BMI = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)². Most common classification method — know the formula and the ranges shown above.","Waist circumference: >35 inches in females, >40 inches in males is associated with more health consequences.","Waist-to-hip ratio: looks at fat distribution — >0.95 in males or >0.8 in females indicates higher risk.","Body shape: Android/\"apple\" shape (fat carried in the waist/abdomen) carries a higher risk of heart disease and diabetes. Gynoid/\"pear\" shape (fat carried in the hips/lower body) carries a higher risk of osteoporosis, varicose veins, and ce…"]},{"s":"Contributors to Obesity▾","a":"contributors-to-obesity","k":["Contributors to Obesity▾","Primary obesity: excessive caloric intake relative to energy expenditure.","Secondary obesity: related to a cause outside of intake/output — congenital abnormalities or hormonal imbalances.","Genetics: a strong predisposition — about 70–75% of patients who become obese have a genetic predisposition, involving multiple genes rather than a single one.","Physiologic regulation: adipocytes (fat cells) produce over 100 different proteins, affecting appetite and contributing to insulin resistance, hyperlipidemia, and hypertension.","Environmental factors: larger U.S. portion sizes; lower socioeconomic status/education correlates with higher obesity risk; sedentary lifestyles/jobs.","Sleep: inadequate sleep decreases glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity, increases evening cortisol and ghrelin, and decreases leptin — all of which increase hunger and appetite.","Psychosocial factors: a culture of unhealthy eating, mindless/boredom eating, and using food as a reward — habits that start young and are hard to break as adults.","Childhood obesity is a specific public health focus — about 1 in 5 children are obese as early as ages 2–5, and children who are obese are much more likely to become obese adults."]},{"s":"Health Risks of Obesity▾","a":"health-risks-of-obesity","k":["Health Risks of Obesity▾","Depression and higher suicide risk, type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, pulmonary hypertension, exercise intolerance, musculoskeletal issues and impaired mobility, higher risk of several cancers, GI issues (gallstones, GERD), and significant card…"]},{"s":"Nursing Assessment & Communication▾","a":"nursing-assessment-communication","k":["Nursing Assessment & Communication▾","Assess: past health history, risk factors/contributors, what can realistically be modified (genetics and many environmental/socioeconomic factors can't be targeted directly — focus education on what the patient can change), medications that…","Never be judgmental. Use therapeutic communication and assess the patient's readiness to change — timing matters. Right after a new cancer diagnosis or an amputation is not the time to bring up weight loss; right after a cardiac event (e.g.…","Explore the patient's motivation for weight loss (seeing grandchildren graduate, keeping up with friends on a walk) — this builds connection and shows you care, beyond just clinical numbers."]},{"s":"Nursing Interventions: Lifestyle & Nutrition▾","a":"nursing-interventions-lifestyle-nutrition","k":["Nursing Interventions: Lifestyle & Nutrition▾","Achieving a \"goal BMI\" is often unrealistic. Modest weight loss of 3–5% of body weight has real health benefits — 1–2 lbs/week is a reasonable target (roughly 50 lbs/year).","Nutrition: don't encourage fad diets (keto, gluten-free, vegan/vegetarian marketed for fast results) — restrictive diets are hard to maintain and often rebound. The key is decreasing caloric intake relative to expenditure. A 1,200-calorie r…","Exercise: 30 minutes a day is a great goal, but meet the patient where they are — going from 2,000–3,000 steps/day to a 10,000-step goal is unrealistic; aiming for 4,000 is more appropriate. Consider access/safety (an unsafe neighborhood fo…","Behavior modification: self-monitoring (food diary), stimulus control (e.g. changing where/how snacking happens), and counseling.","Support groups (e.g. Weight Watchers) can help; be cautious recommending for-profit commercial weight-loss centers. Some insurers/employers offer benefits for these programs.","Drug therapy: several appetite-suppressing drugs exist, but only orlistat is FDA-approved. These take months to show results, should never be used alone (always paired with lifestyle changes), and have significant side effects. Discourage o…","Surgical intervention (bariatric surgery) can also help, but — like drugs — must be combined with ongoing lifestyle modification, not used as a stand-alone fix."]},{"s":"Metabolic Syndrome▾","a":"metabolic-syndrome","k":["Metabolic Syndrome▾","A group of metabolic risk factors that increase a person's risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke, and diabetes. About 1 in 3 U.S. adults have metabolic syndrome — more prevalent in adults over 60.","The main underlying risk factor is insulin resistance, driven largely by increased visceral fat (subcutaneous fat carries lower risk than visceral fat).","A patient must have at least 3 of the 5 criteria below to be diagnosed with metabolic syndrome.","Waist circumference — ≥40 in (102 cm) in men; ≥35 in (89 cm) in women","Triglycerides — >150 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L), or on drug treatment for elevated triglycerides","HDL (\"good\") cholesterol — <40 mg/dL (0.9 mmol/L) in men; <50 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L) in women, or on drug treatment for elevated cholesterol","Blood pressure — ≥130 mm Hg systolic or ≥85 mm Hg diastolic, or on drug treatment for hypertension","Fasting blood glucose — ≥110 mg/dL, or on drug treatment for elevated glucose","The instructor's framing: \"memorize these\" — meaning know that a patient with more of these risk factors present is at higher cardiovascular risk, and that 3 or more together is what defines metabolic syndrome specifically."]},{"s":"Nursing Interventions for Metabolic Syndrome▾","a":"nursing-interventions-for-metabolic-syndrome","k":["Nursing Interventions for Metabolic Syndrome▾","There is no single specific management for metabolic syndrome as a whole — instead, address each risk factor individually, watch for complications, and focus on lifestyle modification.","Bariatric surgery can be indicated for appropriate patients.","Long-term risk reduction is the key — healthy weight, physical activity, and personalized lifestyle changes based on the patient's specific profile.","The goal is always to prevent or control cardiovascular events, type 2 diabetes, and the associated risk of cancers and dementia."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient says, \"I've lost 8 lbs in two months, but I'm still nowhere near my goal BMI, so I feel like this isn't working.\" What is the nurse's best response?","A \"You're right to be concerned — weight loss should happen faster than 4 lbs a month to be meaningful.\"","B \"A goal BMI is the most important marker of success, so let's revisit your calorie target to speed things up.\"","C \"Plateaus mean your current plan has stopped working, so it's time to try a more restrictive diet.\"","D \"Even a 3–5% weight loss brings real health benefits, and your current pace is healthy and realistic.\"","A nurse assesses two patients being evaluated for significant weight gain. Patient 1 reports consistently eating more calories than she expends, with no other medical explanation. Patient 2 has newly diagnosed hypothyroidism and reports wei…","A Both patients have primary obesity, since intake exceeds expenditure in each case.","B Patient 1 has secondary obesity, and Patient 2 has primary obesity.","C Patient 1 has primary obesity, and Patient 2 has secondary obesity.","D Both patients have secondary obesity, since each has a diagnosed medical cause.","A patient admits to mindlessly snacking every night while watching television, regardless of hunger. The nurse suggests the patient try snacking only at the kitchen table and turning off the TV during meals. This intervention is an example …","A Self-monitoring","B Stimulus control","C Formal counseling referral","D Drug therapy adjunct","A patient who is 1.65 m tall weighs 90.3 kg. Using BMI = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)², the nurse calculates a BMI of approximately 33. How should the nurse classify this finding?","A Overweight","B Obese","C Extremely obese","D Normal weight","A sedentary patient who currently walks about 2,500 steps a day expresses interest in exercising more. Which exercise goal reflects the nurse's best initial recommendation?","A Increase gradually toward about 4,000 steps a day as an achievable next step.","B Set a goal of 10,000 steps a day now, since that is the standard exercise target.","C Avoid a step goal until the patient can commit to 30 minutes of exercise daily.","D Recommend a gym-based cardio program regardless of the patient's transportation access.","A patient newly diagnosed with metabolic syndrome asks the nurse, \"What's the one treatment that will fix all of this?\" Which response best reflects appropriate nursing knowledge of metabolic syndrome management?","A \"There is a single standardized medication protocol that treats metabolic syndrome as a whole.\"","B \"There isn't one specific treatment — each risk factor is addressed individually, along with lifestyle changes.\"","C \"Bariatric surgery alone is the recommended first-line treatment for anyone with metabolic syndrome.\"","D \"Once your waist circumference returns to normal, the other criteria typically resolve on their own.\"","A nursing student states, \"Fat tissue is just inactive storage — it doesn't really do anything else in the body.\" How should the nurse best correct this statement?","A \"Actually, adipocytes produce over 100 proteins that affect appetite and contribute to insulin resistance.\"","B \"Actually, fat tissue is inactive, but it directly damages joints through its physical weight.\"","C \"You're right — adipose tissue is inert storage with no hormonal or metabolic activity.\"","D \"Actually, fat tissue only affects appetite and has no effect on blood pressure or lipids.\"","A nurse is teaching a patient newly diagnosed with metabolic syndrome about its underlying pathophysiology. Which explanation is most accurate?","A Metabolic syndrome is primarily driven by subcutaneous fat stored just beneath the skin.","B Metabolic syndrome develops mainly from elevated LDL cholesterol independent of fat distribution.","C Metabolic syndrome is driven mainly by insulin resistance, largely related to increased visceral fat.","D Metabolic syndrome results primarily from decreased visceral fat combined with increased subcutaneous fat.","A patient asks the nurse about joining a for-profit commercial weight-loss center advertised on TV. What is the nurse's most appropriate response?","A \"Community and for-profit support groups are never appropriate resources for weight management.\"","B \"You should avoid all support groups, since only medication and surgery have proven benefit.\"","C \"Support groups can help, but I'd be cautious with for-profit centers — check your insurance.\"","D \"Any support group is equally effective, so cost and structure don't matter when choosing one.\"","A nurse is reviewing the health history of a patient with a BMI of 34 and identifying obesity-related health risks that require ongoing monitoring. Which findings should the nurse recognize as risks associated with obesity? Select all that …","A Increased risk for type 2 diabetes mellitus","B Increased risk for obstructive sleep apnea and pulmonary hypertension","C Increased risk for gallstones and GERD","D Decreased risk for depression and suicide","E Increased risk for several cancers","F Decreased cardiovascular risk factors overall","A nurse is deciding when to initiate a conversation about weight-loss readiness. With which patient is this conversation most likely to be well received?","A A patient recovering well one day after placement of a cardiac stent","B A patient who received a new diagnosis of breast cancer this morning","C A patient who underwent a below-the-knee amputation two days ago","D A patient just told their surgery is postponed for elevated blood glucose","A patient tells the nurse, \"My coworker lost 20 lbs on an 800-calorie-a-day diet in a month, so I'm going to try that on my own.\" What is the nurse's priority response?","A \"That's a great plan — 800 calories a day is the standard recommended intake for safe weight loss.\"","B \"An 800-calorie diet should be used only short-term and under a provider's supervision, not on your own.\"","C \"As long as you eat 5 servings of fruits and vegetables, intake below 1,200 calories is safe without supervision.\"","D \"You should aim for an even lower calorie intake to see faster results, since your coworker's approach worked.\"","A nurse reviews the chart of a 58-year-old male patient: waist circumference 42 in, triglycerides 165 mg/dL, HDL 38 mg/dL, blood pressure 128/78 mm Hg (no antihypertensive), fasting glucose 118 mg/dL, LDL 145 mg/dL. Which findings should th…","A Waist circumference 42 in","B Triglycerides 165 mg/dL","C HDL 38 mg/dL","D Blood pressure 128/78 mm Hg","E Fasting glucose 118 mg/dL","F LDL 145 mg/dL","A patient tells the nurse she wants to lose weight so she can \"still be able to get down on the floor and play with my grandkids.\" Which nursing response best reflects therapeutic communication principles for this situation?","A \"Tell me more about that — connecting your goals to what matters most to you can help guide your plan.\"","B \"Let's focus on your BMI number instead, since that's the most clinically relevant measure of progress.\"","C \"That's a nice thought, but let's talk about your goal BMI so we can set a more realistic target.\"","D \"I'll note that, but your cholesterol and blood pressure numbers are really what we should prioritize discussing today.\"","A patient starting orlistat for weight loss asks the nurse, \"If I take this, can I stop tracking my food and exercising as much?\" What is the nurse's best response?","A \"Yes, orlistat works best when you rely on it as your primary strategy rather than diet changes.\"","B \"You'll notice results within a week or two, so you can cut back on lifestyle changes once you see progress.\"","C \"Orlistat must always be combined with ongoing diet and exercise changes — it isn't intended to replace them.\"","D \"Since orlistat is available over the counter, it's safe to adjust your lifestyle changes as you see fit.\"","A patient reports sleeping only 4–5 hours a night and states, \"I've been so much hungrier lately, especially at night.\" Which explanation best reflects the relationship between sleep and appetite regulation?","A Inadequate sleep increases leptin and decreases ghrelin, which suppresses hunger during the day.","B Inadequate sleep improves glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity, which paradoxically increases appetite.","C Inadequate sleep decreases evening cortisol, which is the primary driver of increased nighttime hunger.","D Inadequate sleep increases evening cortisol and ghrelin while decreasing leptin, which increases hunger.","A patient describes the eating plan they intend to follow after teaching from the nurse. Which statement indicates the patient needs further teaching?","A \"I'll plan to weigh myself about once a week instead of every day.\"","B \"I'm going to try baking or broiling instead of frying most of my meals.\"","C \"I'm aiming for around 5 servings of fruits and vegetables a day.\"","D \"I found a strict gluten-free program online that promises fast results.\"","The nurse is comparing two patients during a wellness assessment. Patient A carries most of her excess weight in the hips and thighs. Patient B carries most of his excess weight around the abdomen and waist, with a waist-to-hip ratio of 1.0…","A Patient A's gynoid pattern places her at greater risk for type 2 diabetes and heart disease than Patient B.","B Patient B's waist-to-hip ratio of 1.05 is normal for males and indicates low cardiovascular risk.","C Patient A's gynoid fat distribution places her at greater risk for hypertension than Patient B.","D Patient B's android pattern places him at greater risk for heart disease and diabetes than Patient A.","During an obesity assessment, a nurse identifies that a patient has a strong genetic predisposition to obesity and lives in a lower-income neighborhood with limited access to affordable healthy food. How should the nurse best incorporate th…","A Focus education on overcoming genetic predisposition, since genetics is the strongest contributor.","B Avoid discussing weight at all, since genetics and socioeconomic status cannot be changed.","C Acknowledge these factors cannot be directly targeted, and focus education on modifiable lifestyle habits.","D Recommend the patient relocate to a higher-income area before beginning weight-related counseling.","A community health nurse is planning an obesity-prevention outreach program and reviewing epidemiologic data on childhood obesity. Which statement should guide the nurse's understanding of priority timing for intervention?","A Childhood obesity rates are highest among teenagers age 15–18, so outreach should target high schools.","B About 1 in 5 children are already obese by ages 2–5, so outreach should begin in early childhood.","C Childhood obesity has little relationship to adult obesity, so early intervention has limited value.","D Childhood obesity primarily resolves on its own by adolescence, so outreach can wait."]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-oxygenation-tracheostomy.html","t":"Oxygenation & Tracheostomy","secs":[{"s":"Oxygenation & Tracheostomy","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 4","Ventilation, Diffusion & Perfusion","Assessing Breathing: Rate, Depth & Pattern","Breath Sounds","Oxygen Saturation & Diagnostic Tests","Work of Breathing & Factors Affecting Oxygenation","Hypoxia — Signs, Symptoms & Chronic Changes","Airway Clearance: Cough, Secretions & Positioning","Oxygen Therapy Devices","Tracheostomy — Overview, Indications & Tube Types","Tracheostomy — Cuff Management & Communication","Tracheostomy — Nursing Care & Emergency Response","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","A large unit — 5 oxygenation lectures plus an intro to tracheostomies, combined into one page. Covers the mechanics of breathing, how to assess it, hypoxia, oxygen therapy devices, and the basics of tracheostomy care."]},{"s":"Ventilation, Diffusion & Perfusion▾","a":"ventilation-diffusion-perfusion","k":["Ventilation, Diffusion & Perfusion▾","Ventilation: movement of gas in and out of the lungs. Diffusion: the O2/CO2 exchange between the alveoli and red blood cells. Perfusion: distributing that newly oxygenated blood to body tissues.","Breathing rate/depth is driven by blood O2, CO2, and pH — rising CO2 signals the body to increase rate and depth to remove it faster.","Tidal volume is the amount of air exhaled following a normal inspiration. Lung volumes are determined by age, gender, and height; tidal volume itself is influenced by health status, activity, pregnancy, exercise, obesity, and obstructive/re…","Alveoli are where gas exchange actually happens — when oxygenation goes wrong, ask whether the problem is at the alveolar level or somewhere else."]},{"s":"Assessing Breathing: Rate, Depth & Pattern▾","a":"assessing-breathing-rate-depth-pattern","k":["Assessing Breathing: Rate, Depth & Pattern▾","Normal respiratory rate: 12–20/min. Also assess depth and rhythm — shallow breathing, or a rate outside 12–20, is abnormal.","Stress and exercise normally increase rate/depth (increased work of breathing) — but this should return to baseline once the exertion stops. A respiratory rate that stays elevated is a cue something is wrong; a rate greater than 27 increase…","Normal variation: infants/children breathe faster than adults. Males and children tend to use abdominal muscles more when breathing; females tend to use more thoracic muscles."]},{"s":"Breath Sounds▾","a":"breath-sounds","k":["Breath Sounds▾","Crackles / rales — Fine to coarse, bubbly — air passing through fluid or collapsed small airways (e.g. fluid overload).","Wheezes — High-pitched whistling — narrow/obstructed airways (asthma, allergic reaction). Note whether inspiratory, expiratory, or both when charting.","Rhonchi — Loud, low-pitched rumbling — fluid or mucus in the airway. Often resolves with coughing — reassess after the patient coughs.","Stridor — Associated with choking, or seen in children.","Pleural friction rub — From inflammation of the pleural space."]},{"s":"Oxygen Saturation & Diagnostic Tests▾","a":"oxygen-saturation-diagnostic-tests","k":["Oxygen Saturation & Diagnostic Tests▾","SpO2 (peripheral — measured with a probe via light transmission) vs. SaO2 (arterial measurement). Normal is 95–100%. A provider may prescribe a different target for a patient with chronic lung disease (e.g. keeping an obstructive-disease pa…","Pulse ox readings can be thrown off by movement, fingernail polish, or cold fingers — if a reading doesn't match how the patient looks, troubleshoot the probe/site, but always assess the patient, not just the number.","Sputum studies: a negative culture is normal. Culture and sensitivity identifies the organism and its antibiotic sensitivities — ordered if a patient isn't improving on empiric antibiotics. AFB (acid-fast bacillus) tests for TB and requires…","Pulmonary function tests (PFTs): basic ventilation study — amount and speed of air inhaled/exhaled. Peak flow: measures airway resistance, especially in asthma. Bronchoscopy: camera down into the lungs (patient usually sedated) — normal is …"]},{"s":"Work of Breathing & Factors Affecting Oxygenation▾","a":"work-of-breathing-factors-affecting-oxygenation","k":["Work of Breathing & Factors Affecting Oxygenation▾","Work of breathing = effort to move air in/out. Inspiration is active; exhalation is passive, relying on the lungs' elastic recoil. Surfactant maintains alveolar surface tension and prevents collapse — patients with certain pulmonary disease…","Work of breathing depends on rate/depth, accessory muscle use/nasal flaring, compliance (how easily the lungs expand), and airway resistance. Decreased compliance + increased resistance + accessory muscle use = increased work of breathing.","Factors that reduce oxygenation: decreased O2-carrying capacity (not enough hemoglobin/RBCs); carbon monoxide poisoning (hemoglobin binds CO instead of O2 — a functional anemia); hypovolemia (reduced circulating volume → tissue hypoxia); de…","The phrenic nerve controls the diaphragm — classically remembered as arising from C3, C4, C5 (\"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive\"). The lecture audio referenced \"T3 to T5\" for this, which doesn't match standard anatomy teaching — worth dou…"]},{"s":"Hypoxia — Signs, Symptoms & Chronic Changes▾","a":"hypoxia-signs-symptoms-chronic-changes","k":["Hypoxia — Signs, Symptoms & Chronic Changes▾","Hypoxia = inadequate tissue oxygenation at the cellular level. Untreated, it can cause cardiac dysrhythmias (the heart needs oxygen too).","Restlessness is a huge, early cue — a restless patient is never a good sign. Other signs: inability to concentrate, decreased LOC, dizziness, behavioral changes, difficulty staying still or lying flat, feeling \"air hungry,\" orthopnea (needi…","Cyanosis (blue discoloration) is a late and unreliable sign of hypoxia. Central cyanosis (tongue, soft palate, around the eyes) indicates true hypoxia. Peripheral cyanosis (extremities, nail beds, earlobes) is usually a vasoconstriction pro…","Chronic hypoxia (e.g. COPD, cystic fibrosis) produces visible changes over time: cyanotic nail beds, sluggish cap refill, clubbing of the fingers, and barrel chest — a normal anteroposterior-to-transverse chest ratio is about 1:2, but in ch…","Lifestyle/developmental factors: smoking and vaping (encourage cessation; also watch for secondhand smoke exposure), obesity, air pollution, malnutrition (weakens the patient), sedentary lifestyle (exercise is protective), substance use, an…"]},{"s":"Airway Clearance: Cough, Secretions & Positioning▾","a":"airway-clearance-cough-secretions-positioning","k":["Airway Clearance: Cough, Secretions & Positioning▾","Dyspnea is a subjective sensation of difficult/uncomfortable breathing. Ask: does it return to baseline after exertion? DOE = dyspnea on exertion. Have the patient rate it on a 0–10 scale, and compare to their normal baseline.","Cough is a key assessment: frequency, productive (sputum present) vs. non-productive (dry — often allergies, asthma, GERD, post-nasal drip), sputum characteristics (bloody = hemoptysis, thick/thin, odor), chronic vs. acute, and strong vs. w…","Coughing is the most effective way to clear secretions — better than suctioning. Adequate hydration thins secretions so they're easier to move. A deep breath before coughing helps trigger it. Manage pain (e.g. after chest/abdominal surgery)…","Mobilization: get the patient up and moving — it promotes lung expansion and gas exchange, and is one of the biggest factors separating a short recovery from a long one.","Positioning: sitting up is the best position to reduce shortness of breath (expands the thoracic cavity). Change position frequently — helps prevent atelectasis, mobilize secretions, and avoid skin breakdown.","Chest physiotherapy (requires an order, not independent nursing action) mobilizes thick secretions via postural drainage, chest percussion, and chest vibration (often via a vest). Postural drainage principle: lay on the unaffected side to p…","Suctioning is a sterile procedure (oral, nasotracheal, or by respiratory therapy) used when a patient can't cough effectively — keep each pass under 10 seconds; consider pre-oxygenating first.","Incentive spirometer: promotes lung expansion through deep breathing, helps prevent/treat atelectasis. Make sure post-op patients actually know how to use it correctly (a common gap) and have one ordered at the bedside."]},{"s":"Oxygen Therapy Devices▾","a":"oxygen-therapy-devices","k":["Oxygen Therapy Devices▾","FiO2 = fraction of inspired oxygen. Room air is 21% FiO2 — supplemental oxygen delivers higher than that.","Requires a healthcare provider order except in an emergency (get an order after the fact). Follow the rights of medication administration — verify the flow rate at the wall matches what's ordered. Application can be delegated to a nursing a…","Safety: oxygen is highly flammable — no open flames, candles, or smoking; consider an \"oxygen in use\" sign.","Nasal cannula — 1–6 L/min → ~24–44% — Safe, well tolerated, but FiO2 varies. Skin breakdown risk at cheeks/ears/nose. Tubing dislodges easily. Humidify if >4 L.","Simple face mask — 6–12 L/min → ~35–50% — Best for short periods or transport. Avoid with claustrophobia or aspiration risk. Watch for skin breakdown and don't let the patient eat with it on.","Partial rebreather — 6–11 L/min → ~60–75% — Middle-ground device, not commonly used in practice. Reservoir bag should stay partially inflated; no flaps on the mask.","Non-rebreather — 10–15 L/min → ~80–95% — For critical patients, often a step before intubation. Has one-way valve + flaps preventing rebreathing of exhaled air. Needs hourly (or more) assessment.","Venturi (\"Vinnie\") mask — 4–12 L/min → ~24–60% — High-flow device — delivers a precise FiO2 via color-coded valves. Best for patients (e.g. chronic lung disease) who need tightly regulated, constant oxygen concentration.","Face tent — — → ~24–100% — Loose-fitting aerosol mask, common in post-op patients — provides humidity, essentially a \"blow-by.\"","High-flow nasal cannula — Up to 100% — Precise, high-flow delivery — for critical patients.","Reservoir bag flaps are the visual cue: a non-rebreather has flaps on the exhalation ports; a partial rebreather does not. Humidification is used any time supplemental oxygen exceeds 4 L/min or runs longer than 24 hours, to prevent drying m…","Oxygen toxicity is a real complication of too much supplemental oxygen — always use the lowest FiO2 that achieves the desired result. Watch for chest pain/heaviness, coughing, dyspnea, muscle twitching, nausea/GI upset, seizures, and visual…"]},{"s":"Tracheostomy — Overview, Indications & Tube Types▾","a":"tracheostomy-overview-indications-tube-types","k":["Tracheostomy — Overview, Indications & Tube Types▾","An endotracheal (ET) tube goes through the mouth — used when a patient is on a ventilator. A tracheostomy bypasses the mouth/nose entirely through a surgically made opening (stoma) in the neck; a patient can be ventilated through either.","Indications: acute airway obstruction, airway protection (e.g. after neck/throat/cancer surgery), facilitating secretion removal (e.g. after a stroke with weak/ineffective cough), and prolonged intubation (roughly 7–10 days — beyond that, a…","A trach can be temporary or permanent — reassure concerned family members of this. Removing it is called decannulation.","Plastic, disposable inner cannula — Metal, reusable inner cannula (cleaned, not replaced)","Has a cuff — No cuff","Sizes typically 6 (smaller/petite) or 8 (more common) — Sized the same way as Shiley","Used more commonly in the hospital setting — Used for longer-term/permanent trachs","The obturator (rounded-tip guide used only during insertion, then immediately removed) should always be kept at the bedside — if the trach becomes dislodged, it's needed to reinsert the outer cannula safely."]},{"s":"Tracheostomy — Cuff Management & Communication▾","a":"tracheostomy-cuff-management-communication","k":["Tracheostomy — Cuff Management & Communication▾","The cuff creates a snug seal in the trachea — prevents aspiration and lets the ventilator deliver full breaths without air leaking around the tube.","Inflated: when the patient is mechanically ventilated, or specifically ordered (e.g. inflated with meals). Deflated: the default on a med-surg/progressive-care floor — check the pilot balloon on assessment to tell which state it's in.","Cuff inflation/deflation is managed by respiratory therapy, not the student nurse — as a student you only observe. The steps (if you observe): suction the mouth first (secretions sitting on top of the cuff could be aspirated once it deflate…","Overinflation risks: increased mucosal pressure → ischemia, cartilage softening/erosion, and even a tracheoesophageal fistula.","Communication: a passive mirror valve (Passy-Muir-style speaking valve) requires the cuff to be deflated to use, and requires a provider's okay. Not all patients tolerate it — it can cause anxiety or feelings of respiratory distress. Speech…"]},{"s":"Tracheostomy — Nursing Care & Emergency Response▾","a":"tracheostomy-nursing-care-emergency-response","k":["Tracheostomy — Nursing Care & Emergency Response▾","Nursing problems: ineffective airway clearance (secretions/plugging), impaired verbal communication, risk for infection (the stoma is direct access to the lower airway), impaired swallowing, body image disturbance, anxiety, and pain (especi…","Assessment: tube type and size, cuff status (inflated/deflated), patient comfort/pain, and oxygenation status generally. Trach care is typically done every 12 hours; suctioning as indicated (more often if needed).","Keep at the bedside: the obturator, and spare trach tubes — one the same size as the patient's current tube, and one a size smaller (in case of swelling/edema).","If the outer cannula/whole tube comes out: (1) insert the obturator into the replacement outer cannula, (2) extend the patient's neck and open the stoma, (3) insert the outer cannula (with obturator inside) into the stoma, (4) immediately r…","Accidental decannulation most often happens during coughing or patient transfers — checking that the trach ties are secure is part of every room assessment."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A student nurse is teaching a patient about lung function. Which statement correctly describes tidal volume?","A \"Tidal volume is the amount of air you exhale after a normal breath in, and it varies with your health status.\"","B \"Tidal volume is determined mainly by your age, gender, and height, similar to your total lung capacity.\"","C \"Tidal volume is the total amount of air your lungs can hold after a maximal inhalation.\"","D \"Tidal volume increases permanently once you develop an obstructive or restrictive lung disease.\"","A patient with longstanding chronic obstructive lung disease has a pulse oximetry reading of 93% and appears comfortable with no signs of distress. What is the nurse's most appropriate action?","A Increase supplemental oxygen since normal SpO2 is 95-100%","B Call the provider to report a critical hypoxemic reading","C Switch the probe site since the reading is likely inaccurate","D Verify the order for this patient's prescribed SpO2 target","On auscultation, a nurse hears loud, low-pitched rumbling sounds throughout a patient's lung fields. The patient is alert and has a strong, effective cough. What should the nurse do first?","A Notify the provider immediately, as rhonchi signals airway obstruction","B Suction the patient now, since suctioning clears secretions better than a cough","C Have the patient cough forcefully, then reassess the lung sounds","D Document the lung sounds as wheezing secondary to bronchospasm","Two patients are receiving oxygen via reservoir bag masks. Patient A's mask has an inflated reservoir bag and one-way valves with flaps over the exhalation ports. Patient B's mask also has an inflated reservoir bag, but there are no flaps a…","A Patient A has a non-rebreather; Patient B has a partial rebreather","B Both patients have non-rebreathers, since both show an inflated reservoir bag","C Patient A has a partial rebreather; Patient B has a non-rebreather","D The presence or absence of flaps does not distinguish between these two devices","A patient with chronic obstructive lung disease has a provider order to maintain a very precise, constant FiO2 because the patient is sensitive to fluctuating oxygen levels. Which oxygen delivery device is the best choice?","A Nasal cannula, because it is best tolerated for long-term precise oxygen delivery","B Non-rebreather mask, because it delivers the highest and most stable FiO2 available","C Simple face mask, because it is designed for patients needing constant oxygen concentrations","D Venturi mask, because color-coded valves deliver an exact, tightly regulated FiO2","A patient with long-standing chronic obstructive lung disease is being assessed for physical changes associated with chronic hypoxia. Which findings should the nurse expect on assessment? Select all that apply.","A Clubbing of the fingers","B Sluggish capillary refill","C An anteroposterior-to-transverse chest ratio approaching 1:2","D Cyanotic nail beds","E An anteroposterior-to-transverse chest ratio approaching 1:1 (barrel chest)","F Bounding peripheral pulses","A patient's tracheostomy tube completely dislodges from the stoma. The nurse retrieves the spare trach tube and obturator kept at the bedside and inserts the obturator into the replacement outer cannula. What should the nurse do next?","A Secure the new trach ties around the patient's neck","B Extend the patient's neck and open the stoma","C Immediately remove the obturator from the cannula","D Assess the patient's stoma and lung sounds","A patient has been receiving high-flow supplemental oxygen at a high FiO2 for several days and begins to develop new symptoms. Which findings should the nurse recognize as possible signs of oxygen toxicity? Select all that apply.","A Chest pain or heaviness","B Muscle twitching","C Increased urine output","D New visual changes","E Bradycardia that resolves quickly with rest","F Nausea and GI upset","A post-op patient's respiratory rate rises from a baseline of 16 to 24 during ambulation to the bathroom. Ten minutes after returning to bed and resting, the rate is still 30 and has not come down. Which finding should most concern the nurs…","A A respiratory rate of 24 during ambulation, because normal RR is 12-20 at all times","B The rate returning to baseline after exertion, because that means increased work","C The rate of 24 during ambulation, because tachypnea always indicates hypoxia","D The persistent rate of 30 at rest, because a rate above 27 risks cardiac arrest","A patient has a normal respiratory rate and adequate movement of air into and out of the lungs, but arterial blood work reveals poor oxygen exchange at the alveolar-capillary level despite that adequate air movement. This patient's primary …","A Ventilation","B Diffusion","C Perfusion","D Compliance","A nursing student is observing the respiratory therapist prepare to deflate a mechanically ventilated patient's tracheostomy cuff so a speaking valve trial can begin. Which action should occur immediately before the cuff is deflated?","A Insert the obturator into the outer cannula in case the tube dislodges","B Ask the patient to cough forcefully to mobilize secretions before deflation","C Suction the patient's mouth to remove secretions pooled above the cuff","D Suction the trachea through the inner cannula to clear the lower airway","A patient has been orally intubated and mechanically ventilated for 9 days with no clear plan for extubation in the near future. What is the most likely reason the care team would now consider converting to a tracheostomy?","A An ET tube can only safely remain in place for 24 hours before a tracheostomy is required","B Endotracheal tubes cannot be used to deliver mechanical ventilation beyond the first few days","C Prolonged intubation increases the risk of airway damage, and a tracheostomy is more comfortable","D A tracheostomy eliminates the patient's need for any further mechanical ventilation","In a cold exam room, a nurse notes a patient's fingertips and nail beds appear slightly bluish, but the tongue and the area around the mouth remain pink, and the patient is talking comfortably with no distress. How should the nurse interpre…","A This represents central cyanosis and indicates immediate hypoxia requiring supplemental oxygen","B This represents peripheral cyanosis, most consistent with vasoconstriction rather than an oxygenation problem","C This finding cannot be interpreted without a pulse oximetry reading first","D This represents late-stage hypoxia because cyanosis only appears once tissue oxygenation has significantly failed","A patient with a long-term, permanent tracheostomy who will be managing their own trach care at home has a metal tube with a reusable inner cannula that is cleaned rather than replaced, and no cuff. Which type of tube does this describe?","A A Shiley tube, a disposable plastic tube used in the hospital","B A cuffed tracheostomy tube used for mechanical ventilation","C An endotracheal tube, used for short-term airway management","D A Jackson tube, typically used for longer-term tracheostomies","A nurse caring for several patients on oxygen therapy considers delegating tasks to an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP). Which task is appropriate to delegate?","A Applying the nasal cannula at the flow rate specified in the order","B Assessing whether the current oxygen device is still appropriate for the patient","C Evaluating whether the patient's oxygenation has improved since starting therapy","D Teaching the patient's family how to safely use oxygen at home","A patient is receiving oxygen via nasal cannula at 5 L/min for an anticipated several-day hospital stay. Which action should the nurse plan to include in this patient's care?","A No humidification is needed, since nasal cannula oxygen is always well tolerated without it","B Add humidification only if the patient later reports nasal or mucous membrane dryness","C Add humidification, since the flow rate exceeds 4 L/min and therapy is expected to continue past 24 hours","D Switch the patient to a Venturi mask, which is the only device requiring humidification","A nurse caring for a patient with an inflated tracheostomy cuff notes the pilot balloon feels unusually firm, suggesting the cuff is overinflated. If this pressure isn't addressed, this patient is at greatest risk for which complication?","A Aspiration of secretions pooled above the cuff","B Accidental decannulation during a patient transfer","C Fistula formation between the trachea and esophagus","D Oxygen toxicity from an excessive FiO2 setting","A nurse enters the room of a patient recovering from thoracic surgery and finds the patient repeatedly shifting position, unable to settle, and saying \"something feels off,\" even though vital signs are still within normal limits. What shoul…","A Recognize restlessness as an early cue of hypoxia and assess respiratory status","B Reassure the patient that anxiety after surgery is normal and encourage relaxation","C Document the finding as expected post-anesthesia behavior and continue rounding","D Wait until vital signs change before initiating further respiratory assessment","A patient has a right lower lobe infiltrate, and the provider orders postural drainage to help mobilize secretions. How should the nurse position the patient?","A On the right side, in reverse Trendelenburg, to help gravity pull secretions upward out of the affected lobe","B On the left side, in Trendelenburg, so gravity drains secretions from the affected lobe","C Supine and flat, to evenly distribute secretions throughout the airway","D In high Fowler's position, to reduce work of breathing while draining secretions","A provider suspects a patient has tuberculosis and orders sputum studies. Which instruction should the nurse give regarding collection?","A \"We'll need a single sputum sample any time of day in a clean container.\"","B \"This test requires collecting sputum for 24 continuous hours in one pooled container.\"","C \"You'll need a bronchoscopy to obtain this sample since sputum can't confirm TB.\"","D \"We'll need morning sputum samples on three consecutive days, in a sterile container.\""]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-cardiovascular.html","t":"Cardiovascular","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 5","Perfusion, Cardiac Output & Ejection Fraction","Hypertension","Hypotension & Orthostatic Hypotension","Hyperlipidemia","Venous Thromboembolism (VTE): DVT & PE","Diagnostics: Labs, Chest X-ray & EKG","Heart Sounds","Nursing Assessment & Care of the Cardiovascular Patient","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Covers perfusion and cardiac output basics, blood pressure (hypertension and hypotension), hyperlipidemia and venous thromboembolism, and the cardiovascular diagnostics and nursing assessment a fundamentals student is expected to know."]},{"s":"Perfusion, Cardiac Output & Ejection Fraction▾","a":"perfusion-cardiac-output-ejection-fraction","k":["Perfusion, Cardiac Output & Ejection Fraction▾","Perfusion = passage of fluid through the circulatory system or lymphatic system to an organ or tissue.","Cardiac output (CO) = volume of blood pumped by the heart in one minute. CO = stroke volume (mL/beat) × heart rate (beats/min).","Alterations in cardiac output = anything that alters heart rate, stroke volume, or myocardial contractility — examples include medications, disease processes, and activity.","Ejection fraction (EF) = the percentage of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each contraction, expressed as a percentage.","Normal EF is greater than 50%. An EF less than 40% indicates heart failure.","Blood pressure reflects the force of blood against arterial walls; pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic pressure.","Pulsus alternans — a pulse that alternates in strength/amplitude beat to beat — can be a sign of left ventricular failure.","CPR review — remember the sequence as C-A-B: Compressions, Airway, Breathing."]},{"s":"Hypertension▾","a":"hypertension","k":["Hypertension▾","Normal — Less than 120 — and — Less than 80","Elevated — 120–129 — and — Less than 80","Hypertension Stage 1 — 130–139 — or — 80–89","Hypertension Stage 2 — 140 or higher — or — 90 or higher","Hypertensive Crisis — Higher than 180 — and/or — Higher than 120","Hypertensive crisis (systolic >180 and/or diastolic >120) requires the patient to consult a doctor immediately — this is a medical emergency.","Non-modifiable risk factors: family history, age, race/ethnicity (African Americans have higher incidence and often more severe hypertension).","Modifiable risk factors: obesity, high sodium intake, physical inactivity, excessive alcohol use, stress, smoking.","DASH diet (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) is the recommended dietary pattern — emphasizes whole grains, vegetables, fruits, low-fat dairy, lean protein, nuts/seeds/legumes, while limiting sodium, sweets, and fats.","Complications of uncontrolled hypertension include heart disease, stroke, kidney disease, and vision loss."]},{"s":"Hypotension & Orthostatic Hypotension▾","a":"hypotension-orthostatic-hypotension","k":["Hypotension & Orthostatic Hypotension▾","Hypotension can result from many causes — dehydration/fluid loss, medications, prolonged bed rest, cardiac problems, and severe infection (septic shock) among others. Treatment targets the underlying cause.","Symptoms: dizziness, lightheadedness, syncope (fainting), blurred vision, fatigue, weakness.","Orthostatic (postural) hypotension = a drop in systolic BP of ≥20 mm Hg, or in diastolic BP of ≥10 mm Hg, or the onset of lightheadedness/dizziness when moving from lying to standing.","Diagnostic protocol: measure BP and HR lying down (after 5 minutes), then standing at 1 minute, then standing again at 3 minutes — record symptoms at each position.","Nursing care: have the patient change positions slowly (dangle at the bedside before standing), stay close by/use a gait belt for safety, encourage adequate hydration, and teach the patient to sit or lie down immediately if symptomatic."]},{"s":"Hyperlipidemia▾","a":"hyperlipidemia","k":["Hyperlipidemia▾","Cholesterol is either exogenous (from diet) or endogenous (made by the liver).","Chronic hyperlipidemia contributes to atherosclerosis — plaque buildup in artery walls — which can progress to coronary artery disease (CAD) and peripheral artery disease (PAD).","Dietary teaching: reduce saturated and trans fats, increase fiber, limit cholesterol intake, increase physical activity.","Total cholesterol — Below 200 mg/dL","LDL (\"bad\" cholesterol) — Below 100 mg/dL","HDL (\"good\" cholesterol) — Above 40–60 mg/dL (higher is better)","Triglycerides — Below 150 mg/dL","A fasting lipid profile requires no food or drink (other than water) for typically 9–12 hours prior — that's the \"F\" for fasting. Must be drawn fasting to be accurate."]},{"s":"Venous Thromboembolism (VTE): DVT & PE▾","a":"venous-thromboembolism-vte-dvt-pe","k":["Venous Thromboembolism (VTE): DVT & PE▾","A deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a blood clot forming in a deep vein, most often in the legs. If it breaks loose and travels to the lungs, it becomes a pulmonary embolism (PE) — a life-threatening emergency.","Risk factors: immobility/prolonged bed rest, surgery (especially orthopedic), pregnancy, oral contraceptives/hormone therapy, smoking, obesity, malignancy, prior history of DVT/PE, and long travel.","Signs/symptoms of DVT: unilateral leg swelling, warmth, redness, and tenderness/pain in the calf; a difference in calf circumference between legs is a key assessment finding.","Signs/symptoms of PE: sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea, anxiety — a medical emergency requiring immediate attention.","Diagnosis: venous ultrasound (Doppler) for DVT; CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) for PE.","Nursing care/prevention: measure and compare calf circumference bilaterally, apply TED hose (graduated compression stockings) and/or sequential compression devices (SCDs) as ordered, encourage ankle pumps/calf pumping exercises and early am…","An IVC filter (inferior vena cava filter) may be placed in patients who can't tolerate anticoagulation, to catch clots before they reach the lungs.","Never massage a leg suspected of having a DVT — this can dislodge the clot."]},{"s":"Diagnostics: Labs, Chest X-ray & EKG▾","a":"diagnostics-labs-chest-x-ray-ekg","k":["Diagnostics: Labs, Chest X-ray & EKG▾","Hemoglobin (Hgb) — Females: 12–16 g/dL. Males: 14–18 g/dL.","Hematocrit (Hct) — Females: 37–47%. Males: 42–52%.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit (H&H) are part of the CBC and are drawn on almost every hospitalized patient. Hemoglobin is the iron-containing pigment on red blood cells that oxygen binds to for transport. Hematocrit is the percentage of total b…","A chest X-ray assesses for fluid accumulation in the lungs and heart size. Typically an anterior-posterior (AP) and a lateral view are taken; the patient removes metal objects and holds their breath briefly for each image.","EKG (ECG) reflects the heart's electrical conduction. A normal sinus rhythm means the atria and ventricles contract in the correct sequence at a normal rate. An EKG is a snapshot in time — typically about 5 minutes of recorded activity.","Telemetry monitors heart activity continuously (as opposed to an EKG's single snapshot), viewed at the nurses' station or bedside monitor. It's common for hospitalized patients to be on telemetry.","Electrode placement mnemonic: \"White on right, snow over grass; smoke over fire\" — white electrode on the patient's right side, with green below white (\"snow over grass\"), and black above red (\"smoke over fire\"). If an electrode falls off, …","\"White on right, snow over grass; smoke over fire\""]},{"s":"Heart Sounds▾","a":"heart-sounds","k":["Heart Sounds▾","A normal heart has two sounds per beat: S1 and S2 (\"lub-dub\") — should be crisp, with only one sound at each point.","A third or fourth sound is never normal: S3 is a ventricular gallop (sounds like \"Ken-TUCK-y\"), and S4 is an atrial gallop. Both indicate cardiovascular dysfunction.","Murmurs are a swishing sound caused by a problem with a valve (restricted or \"floppy\"/incompetent).","Clicks occur in patients with a mechanical heart valve — the metal components closing together makes an audible click.","Rubs are a scratchy sound from friction of the pericardial sac (pericardial friction rub)."]},{"s":"Nursing Assessment & Care of the Cardiovascular Patient▾","a":"nursing-assessment-care-of-the-cardiovascular-patient","k":["Nursing Assessment & Care of the Cardiovascular Patient▾","Assessment: cardiac/vascular history, vital signs and oxygen saturation, skin color/temperature/hydration, level of consciousness (decreased LOC can reflect decreased cerebral perfusion), peripheral pulses (assessed bilaterally and simultan…","Possible NANDA nursing diagnoses: risk for unstable blood pressure, activity intolerance, decreased cardiac output, ineffective tissue perfusion, fatigue, ineffective health maintenance, risk for injury (e.g. from orthostatic hypotension).","Common interventions: strict intake and output monitoring, oxygen therapy, telemetry, medications (diuretics, digoxin, vasodilators, ACE inhibitors, beta blockers, antiplatelets), lab monitoring, heart-healthy/DASH diet, stress reduction, a…","Patient teaching: make the plan patient-centered with the patient's input for better buy-in; smoking cessation; limit alcohol; manage stress, hyperlipidemia, and diabetes (all modifiable risk factors); encourage appropriate nutrition and ex…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A male client's CBC shows a hemoglobin of 13.2 g/dL and a hematocrit of 39%. How should the nurse interpret these results?","A Both values are within normal limits for a male.","B The hemoglobin is normal, but the hematocrit is elevated for a male.","C The hemoglobin is low for a male, but the hematocrit is within normal range.","D Both values are below the normal range for a male and should be reported.","A nurse is caring for several clients at high risk for venous thromboembolism. Which nursing actions are appropriate to help prevent a DVT? Select all that apply.","A Apply graduated compression (TED) stockings as ordered.","B Encourage ankle pump and calf-pumping exercises.","C Massage the calves during morning care to stimulate circulation.","D Apply sequential compression devices (SCDs) as ordered.","E Encourage early ambulation as the client's condition allows.","F Keep the client on strict bed rest to avoid dislodging any developing clot.","A client asks the nurse why their cholesterol remains high despite following a strict low-cholesterol diet for months. Which explanation should the nurse provide?","A The liver also produces cholesterol, so diet alone may not control levels.","B All cholesterol in the body comes from dietary intake, not from the liver.","C Cholesterol levels are not affected by liver function in healthy adults.","D The client's diet must not actually be reducing cholesterol as reported.","A nurse is teaching a client about risk factors for hypertension. Which factor should the nurse identify as non-modifiable?","A Consuming a high-sodium diet, since sodium directly raises blood volume.","B Race/ethnicity, since African Americans have a higher incidence and greater severity.","C Chronic psychological stress, since stress hormones raise blood pressure acutely.","D Physical inactivity, since a sedentary lifestyle contributes to weight gain.","While auscultating heart sounds, a nurse hears an extra sound immediately after S2, in a rhythm the instructor describes as sounding like 'Ken-TUCK-y.' The nurse should recognize this as which finding?","A A normal variant heard in some healthy young adults, since extra sounds are common with a vigorous heartbeat.","B S4, an atrial gallop that occurs just before S1 and is heard as 'da-lub-dup.'","C A pericardial friction rub caused by inflammation of the pericardial sac.","D S3, a ventricular gallop that always indicates cardiovascular dysfunction.","A student nurse is reviewing the CPR sequence in preparation for clinical. Which order reflects the current recommended sequence?","A Airway, Breathing, Compressions","B Breathing, Compressions, Airway","C Compressions, Airway, Breathing","D Airway, Compressions, Breathing","A home health nurse measures a client's blood pressure as 190/128 mmHg. The client has a mild headache but feels well otherwise and wants to finish yard work before addressing it. What should the nurse do first?","A Tell the client to recheck the blood pressure in 24 hours and call the office if it is still elevated.","B Instruct the client to seek immediate medical attention before returning to any activity.","C Teach the client about the DASH diet and schedule a follow-up clinic visit for next week.","D Have the client rest quietly for 30 minutes, then recheck the blood pressure before deciding.","A clinic nurse reviews a client's blood pressure trend: three readings over the past month averaged 142/88 mmHg. According to the AHA classification, how should the nurse categorize this reading?","A Hypertension Stage 2, because the systolic value alone meets that category.","B Hypertension Stage 1, because both values fall in the 130-139/80-89 range.","C Elevated blood pressure, because the diastolic value is below 90 mmHg.","D Normal blood pressure, since only one of the two values is elevated.","A client develops a rapid dysrhythmia with a heart rate of 160 beats/min. Despite the fast rate, the client's blood pressure drops and they become dizzy. Which explanation best fits this situation using the cardiac output relationship?","A Cardiac output is unaffected by heart rate, so the drop in BP has an unrelated cause.","B At very fast rates, ventricular filling time shortens, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall.","C A faster heart rate always raises cardiac output proportionally, so CO must be normal here.","D Cardiac output equals heart rate divided by stroke volume, so the faster rate raised CO.","A nurse is performing a focused cardiovascular assessment. Which actions and findings are correct for this assessment? Select all that apply.","A Assess peripheral pulses bilaterally and simultaneously, grading each 0-4+.","B Note that a decreased level of consciousness may reflect decreased cerebral perfusion.","C Assess for jugular venous distention with the head of the bed at a 90-degree angle.","D Recognize that peripheral edema implies impaired venous return.","E Use a continuous EKG snapshot lasting several hours to monitor the client's rhythm.","F Palpate and compare calf tenderness and circumference bilaterally.","A client recently diagnosed with hypertension asks the nurse for dietary guidance. Which meal choice best reflects the recommended dietary pattern the nurse should suggest?","A A cured deli meat sandwich with a side of pickles","B A cheeseburger with french fries and a salted pretzel","C Canned vegetable soup with added salt for flavor","D Grilled chicken, a whole-grain roll, and steamed vegetables","Two days after hip replacement surgery, a client suddenly becomes short of breath, reports chest pain, and appears anxious. Vital signs show tachycardia and tachypnea. What is the nurse's priority action?","A Elevate the client's legs and apply TED hose, since compression may relieve the dyspnea.","B Notify the provider immediately and prepare the client for emergency evaluation.","C Palpate and massage the calves bilaterally, since this may reveal the source of the emboli.","D Document the findings and reassess in 30 minutes, since trends will clarify the diagnosis.","During assessment, a nurse observes visible jugular venous distention with the head of the bed elevated to 45 degrees. This finding is most consistent with which condition?","A Fluid volume excess","B Fluid volume deficit","C Normal venous pressure","D Decreased cerebral perfusion","A client is scheduled for a fasting lipid profile in the morning. Which instruction should the nurse provide?","A Avoid all food and beverages, including water, for 24 hours before the test.","B No food or drink except water is needed for approximately 9-12 hours before the blood draw.","C Only fatty and fried foods need to be avoided in the 12 hours before the test.","D Fasting is not required as long as the client avoids caffeine that morning.","A client's echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 33%. The nurse understands this finding is most consistent with which condition?","A Normal, healthy ventricular function that requires no further intervention.","B Borderline ventricular function that should simply be rechecked in one year.","C Increased stroke volume compensating for an abnormally slow heart rate.","D Heart failure, since this value falls well below the 40% cutoff.","A client with a newly diagnosed DVT has a strong contraindication to anticoagulant therapy due to active gastrointestinal bleeding. Which intervention would the nurse anticipate to prevent the clot from reaching the lungs?","A Placement of an inferior vena cava (IVC) filter","B Administration of a higher dose of anticoagulation","C Strict bed rest with the affected leg elevated","D Bilateral TED hose to keep the clot from migrating","A client's fasting lipid panel returns: total cholesterol 210 mg/dL, LDL 92 mg/dL, HDL 55 mg/dL, triglycerides 130 mg/dL. Which single value should the nurse identify as outside the target range?","A Total cholesterol, target below 200 mg/dL","B LDL cholesterol, target below 100 mg/dL","C HDL cholesterol, target above 40-60 mg/dL","D Triglycerides, target below 150 mg/dL","A nurse palpates a client's radial pulse and notes that the strength of each beat alternates between strong and weak, while the rhythm and rate remain regular. What should the nurse recognize about this finding?","A It is a benign variant commonly seen with anxiety and requires no further action.","B It suggests dehydration, and the nurse should encourage oral fluids.","C It is called pulsus alternans and can be a sign of left ventricular failure.","D It indicates a mechanical heart valve is functioning normally.","A nurse performs orthostatic vital sign measurements. Lying for 5 minutes: BP 128/76 mmHg, HR 72. Standing at 1 minute: BP 112/70 mmHg, HR 88, and the client reports feeling dizzy. How should the nurse interpret this data?","A Orthostatic hypotension is not present, because the systolic drop of 16 mmHg is less than the required 20 mmHg threshold.","B Orthostatic hypotension is not present, because the diastolic drop of 6 mmHg is less than the required 10 mmHg threshold.","C Orthostatic hypotension is present, because the onset of dizziness on standing meets the diagnostic criteria even though neither BP threshold was reached.","D Orthostatic hypotension cannot be confirmed until the client is rechecked standing at 3 minutes, regardless of the dizziness reported at 1 minute.","A client recovering from a urinary tract infection develops fever, confusion, and a blood pressure of 82/48 mmHg with a rapid, thready pulse. Which underlying cause should the nurse suspect as most likely responsible for this hypotension?","A Prolonged bed rest deconditioning","B Sepsis progressing to septic shock","C A new antihypertensive medication","D Chronic dehydration from poor oral intake"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-diabetic-care-nutrition.html","t":"Diabetic Care & Nutrition","secs":[{"s":"Diabetic Care & Nutrition","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 6","Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes","Diagnostic Labs & Criteria","Prediabetes & Pharmacologic/Sick-Day Management","Insulin Therapy","Hypoglycemia, Hyperglycemia & Insulin Pumps","Chronic Complications & Diabetic Foot Care","GI Basics, Malnutrition & Factors Affecting Nutrition","Nutritional Assessment: Labs & the Nursing Process","Diet Types & Progression","Dysphagia & Feeding Assistance","Enteral & Parenteral Nutrition","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Combines two lecture sets: diabetic care (types, diagnosis, insulin, hypo/hyperglycemia, chronic complications) and nutrition (GI basics, nutritional assessment, diet types, dysphagia, enteral/parenteral feeding). Both feed directly into pa…"]},{"s":"Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","a":"type-1-vs-type-2-diabetes","k":["Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","Type 1: autoimmune destruction of the pancreatic beta cells (genetic predisposition + environmental trigger). Typically diagnosed in younger patients, with abrupt onset. Only 5–10% of diabetes cases. Patients make no endogenous insulin and …","Type 2: beta cells wear out and the body becomes insulin resistant, causing the liver to increase glucose production. More common in adults over 40–45. Can go undiagnosed for years since patients are often screened by risk factors, not symp…","Type 2 risk factors — non-modifiable: family history, age >45, race/ethnicity (African American, Hispanic, Pacific Islander, American Indian have higher incidence), history of gestational diabetes. Modifiable: physical inactivity, high body…"]},{"s":"Diagnostic Labs & Criteria▾","a":"diagnostic-labs-criteria","k":["Diagnostic Labs & Criteria▾","Fasting blood glucose — <100 mg/dL (normal <126 by lecture) — 100–125 mg/dL — ≥126 mg/dL","Casual/random glucose — <200 mg/dL — — — ≥200 mg/dL (with symptoms)","Hemoglobin A1C — 4–6% — 5.7–6.4% — ≥6.5%","OGTT (2-hr) — <140 mg/dL — 140–199 mg/dL — ≥200 mg/dL","A random/casual glucose greater than 300 mg/dL is considered a medical emergency.","Hemoglobin A1C reflects average glucose control over the past 3 months — used both for diagnosis and to evaluate treatment effectiveness. For patients with diagnosed diabetes, an acceptable range is 6–8%, with a target around 7%.","Fasting glucose = no food or drink for at least 8 hours prior.","OGTT (oral glucose tolerance test) — used almost exclusively to diagnose gestational diabetes. Fasting glucose is drawn, the patient drinks the glucose solution, then glucose is checked every 30 minutes up to 2 hours. Fasting should be <110…","Diagnosis requires at least one of: A1C ≥6.5%, fasting glucose ≥126, OGTT 2-hr ≥200, or classic symptoms (3 P's, unexplained weight loss, random glucose ≥200) plus a hyperglycemic crisis (≥300). Type 1 additionally requires islet cell autoa…","Diabetes also increases LDL/triglycerides and decreases HDL on a lipid panel."]},{"s":"Prediabetes & Pharmacologic/Sick-Day Management▾","a":"prediabetes-pharmacologic-sick-day-management","k":["Prediabetes & Pharmacologic/Sick-Day Management▾","Prediabetes = impaired fasting glucose and/or impaired glucose tolerance. Usually asymptomatic, but long-term damage can already be occurring. Teaching is the most important intervention — diet, exercise, close glucose monitoring, and regul…","Oral diabetic medications are started low and increased gradually based on A1C and fasting glucose; used most often for type 2. In the hospital, oral agents are often held and replaced with insulin during acute illness, since insulin allows…","Sick-day management: illness causes physiologic stress, which raises blood glucose and increases risk of DKA/HHS. Patients should notify their provider, check glucose more frequently (as often as every 2 hours), keep taking their medication…","Call the provider if: urine ketones present, blood glucose >250, fever >101.5°F not responding to Tylenol, confusion/disorientation, rapid breathing, persistent nausea/vomiting/diarrhea, unable to tolerate liquids, or illness lasting longer…"]},{"s":"Insulin Therapy▾","a":"insulin-therapy","k":["Insulin Therapy▾","Nursing tries to mimic the body's normal insulin release with a basal-bolus regimen: rapid/short-acting (bolus) insulin before meals, plus a background long-acting (basal) dose once daily (e.g. glargine/Lantus, typically at bedtime).","Inhaled insulin is FYI only — not commonly used. Know the relative onset/peak/duration ordering of rapid → short → intermediate → long acting.","Insulin is a high-alert medication. Never give insulin without first knowing the patient's current blood glucose. Before administering, confirm: the onset/peak/duration of the insulin being given, the patient's diet order and oral intake (i…"]},{"s":"Hypoglycemia, Hyperglycemia & Insulin Pumps▾","a":"hypoglycemia-hyperglycemia-insulin-pumps","k":["Hypoglycemia, Hyperglycemia & Insulin Pumps▾","Hypoglycemia = blood glucose <70 mg/dL (though patients with chronically uncontrolled/high glucose may feel symptomatic at higher numbers). Symptoms: sweating, blurry vision, dizziness, anxiety, hunger, irritability, shakiness, tachycardia,…","Rule of 15 (conscious patient, able to swallow): give 15 g of simple carbohydrate (4 oz juice, regular soda, 3 glucose tabs, 1 tbsp honey, 5–8 Life Savers — avoid sugars with fat, which delay absorption). Recheck glucose in 15 minutes; repe…","If unconscious/unable to swallow: IM glucagon or IV D50 (25–50 mL dextrose).","Hyperglycemia — generally 250–300+ mg/dL; anything above 300 is an emergency. Causes: illness/infection, self-management issues, physiologic stress. Symptoms are vague (weakness, fatigue, blurry vision, headache, nausea/vomiting/diarrhea) a…","DKA (diabetic ketoacidosis) and HHS (hyperglycemic hyperosmolar syndrome, the type 2 equivalent) are life-threatening crises usually seen with glucose in the 500–700+ range, with electrolyte abnormalities that can be fatal.","Insulin pumps deliver a continuous basal infusion of rapid/regular insulin plus bolus doses; patients still check glucose about 4x/day. Pumps and CGMs are typically deactivated in acute inpatient care in favor of standard glucose monitoring…"]},{"s":"Chronic Complications & Diabetic Foot Care▾","a":"chronic-complications-diabetic-foot-care","k":["Chronic Complications & Diabetic Foot Care▾","Macrovascular damage (large vessels): coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease, cerebrovascular disease. Women with diabetes have 4–6× the cardiovascular risk; men have 2–3×.","Microvascular damage (capillaries): retinopathy (retinal damage — frequent eye exams), nephropathy (kidney damage — diabetes is a leading cause of end-stage renal disease, especially with hypertension), and neuropathy (nerve damage — impair…","Diabetic foot care is a major nursing focus: neuropathy causes loss of protective sensation (LOPS), so patients don't feel injuries; combined with poor wound healing and decreased blood flow, this is why foot ulcers and lower-extremity ampu…"]},{"s":"GI Basics, Malnutrition & Factors Affecting Nutrition▾","a":"gi-basics-malnutrition-factors-affecting-nutrition","k":["GI Basics, Malnutrition & Factors Affecting Nutrition▾","Enteral = nutrition delivered by way of the GI tract (includes normal eating and tube feedings). Parenteral = nutrition delivered outside the GI tract (IV, i.e. TPN). \"If the gut works, use it\" — enteral is always preferred over parenteral.","GI tract path: esophagus → esophageal sphincter → stomach (acidic) → pyloric sphincter → small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) → large intestine/colon (ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid, rectum) → anus. Water is reabsorbed in …","Malnutrition can mean undernourished or overweight/obese — both are malnutrition. Malnourished patients on admission are at far greater risk for complications (including sepsis), higher readmission and mortality rates, and increased cost of…","Factors affecting nutrition: appetite (decreased by illness, medications, pain, depression), past negative food experiences, disease/illness, medications that alter taste, income (healthy food costs more), education level, physical/function…","Older adults: same vitamin/mineral needs as younger adults, but chronic illness, polypharmacy, and normal GI aging changes (decreased teeth/gum health, saliva, taste buds, thirst mechanism, HCl production, slower metabolism) plus cognitive …","Cultural considerations: assess for food restrictions/abstentions, religious practices (e.g. fasting, avoiding pork), and don't assume all members of a culture eat identically."]},{"s":"Nutritional Assessment: Labs & the Nursing Process▾","a":"nutritional-assessment-labs-the-nursing-process","k":["Nutritional Assessment: Labs & the Nursing Process▾","Anthropometry = measurement of body size/proportions — height, weight, BMI, skinfold/fat measures. No single lab confirms nutritional status; results are affected by fluid balance, liver/kidney function, and comorbidities.","Total protein = albumin + globulin. Albumin makes up about 60% of total protein, is synthesized in the liver, has a 21-day half-life, and is a better indicator of chronic nutritional status. Normal range is roughly 3.5–5 g/dL. Albumin also …","Prealbumin has a much shorter half-life (2 days), making it a better indicator of acute nutritional changes.","Hemoglobin (14–18 males, 12–16 females) — if low, the patient may benefit from iron-rich foods.","Nutrition history should cover diet history, health history/comorbidities, activity level, medications, age, and psychosocial factors (cognitive disorders, alcohol/drug use).","A malnourished patient may show: fatigue, apathy, cachexia (gaunt, wasted appearance), pale integument (from low hemoglobin/pallor), poor hair/nail quality, and abnormal vital signs. Nursing diagnosis: imbalanced nutrition, less than body r…"]},{"s":"Diet Types & Progression▾","a":"diet-types-progression","k":["Diet Types & Progression▾","Regular diet: no restrictions.","Modified texture diets (least to most restrictive of chewing): mechanical soft (foods softened/smaller, no raw fruits/veg/nuts/seeds) → chopped (~½ inch pieces) → ground (~¼ inch) → minced (~⅛ inch) → pureed (smooth, no chewing).","Clear liquid diet: anything liquid you can see through, with no residue — broth, juices without pulp (apple, grape, not orange/tomato/prune), gelatin, black coffee/tea, clear soda. Full liquid diet: everything on clear, plus anything liquid…","Therapeutic diets: consistent carbohydrate (formerly \"ADA diet\"/\"no concentrated sweets\" — balances carbs/fat/protein by calorie count), cardiac/heart-healthy (low salt, low saturated fat, low cholesterol), low residue (low fiber/roughage —…","Fluid-restricted diet: for patients retaining excess fluid — classic examples are heart failure and renal failure, as well as hyponatremia (restricting fluid concentrates the serum and raises sodium). Nurses spread the allowed mL across 24 …","NPO = nothing by mouth (pre-procedure, resting the gut, e.g. a Crohn's/colitis flare). Being NPO for 5–7+ days may prompt starting TPN. Always confirm before giving anything by mouth, even ice chips, without an order.","Diet advancement (typically clear liquid → full liquid → regular) depends on the patient tolerating the current diet: no nausea/vomiting, present bowel sounds, no abdominal distension. Advancing too early can make the patient sick."]},{"s":"Dysphagia & Feeding Assistance▾","a":"dysphagia-feeding-assistance","k":["Dysphagia & Feeding Assistance▾","Dysphagia (spelled with a g) = difficulty swallowing. Dysphasia (spelled with an s) = difficulty talking — a patient can have either or both, commonly after a stroke.","Warning signs: uncoordinated/slow/weak speech, weak gag reflex, delayed swallow, drooling/pocketing food, regurgitation.","Silent aspiration = food/fluid enters the airway without the usual cough response (due to decreased sensation) — may only become apparent later via adventitious lung sounds or a resulting pneumonia within 24 hours. Complications of dysphagi…","Liquids may be ordered thickened (nectar, honey, or spoon-thick) to reduce aspiration risk — a patient ordered honey-thickened liquids may not have thin liquids at all. A barium swallow study can evaluate swallowing safety.","Nursing care: check for oral pocketing, never leave a patient with altered LOC unattended while eating, avoid sedatives/hypnotics before meals in at-risk patients, avoid straws (increase aspiration risk), and don't rush (\"double swallow\") t…","Feeding assistance: protect safety/independence/dignity, keep the tray within reach, clear clutter/odors from the environment, offer smaller/more frequent meals (\"grazing\"), honor food preferences, and provide good oral hygiene. For a patie…"]},{"s":"Enteral & Parenteral Nutrition▾","a":"enteral-parenteral-nutrition","k":["Enteral & Parenteral Nutrition▾","Enteral nutrition (tube feeding) is preferred whenever the gut works — via nasogastric/nasoduodenal/nasojejunal tube (short-term, <4 weeks), or a surgically/endoscopically placed tube for longer-term use: PEG (percutaneous endoscopic gastro…","Parenteral nutrition (TPN) = IV nutrition given through a central line because it's highly concentrated (a dilute version can run peripherally, but standard TPN in a peripheral vein causes phlebitis). Reserved for when the gut isn't usable …","Confirming tube placement: the only definitive method is X-ray. Injecting air and auscultating (\"whoosh test\") is outdated and unreliable — do not rely on it. After X-ray confirmation, ongoing placement can be checked by testing the pH of a…","Signs of feeding intolerance: high gastric residual volume, nausea, cramping, vomiting, severe diarrhea (tube feedings are hypertonic and can pull water into the gut).","Gastric residual: check per hospital policy — general rule of thumb is to hold feeding and recheck in an hour if residual is >250 mL, and hold + notify the provider if >500 mL. Aspirated residual is returned to the patient, not discarded.","Administration: bolus/intermittent vs. continuous. Start slow (e.g. ~20 mL/hr) and advance gradually to goal rate as tolerated. Head of bed at minimum 30°, ideally 45° or higher, to reduce aspiration risk.","Medication administration via feeding tube: verify placement first, flush with water before and after each medication, and give only one medication at a time (crushing/mixing multiple medications together changes their chemistry)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nurse is teaching a female patient with diabetes about long-term cardiovascular risk compared to a male patient with a similar diabetes history. Which statement reflects the risk difference described in diabetes education?","A Cardiovascular risk is essentially the same for men and women with a similar diabetes history","B Men with diabetes have 4-6 times the cardiovascular risk of the general population, versus 2-3 times for women","C Cardiovascular risk in women involves only microvascular complications, while men develop macrovascular disease","D Women with diabetes have 4-6 times the cardiovascular risk of the general population, versus 2-3 times for men","A patient taking metformin for type 2 diabetes is scheduled for a contrast imaging procedure next week and is also admitted for an acute illness in the meantime. Which nursing consideration applies?","A Metformin should be continued without interruption both before the procedure and during the acute illness","B Metformin should be held before the procedure, and oral agents are often replaced with insulin during acute illness","C Metformin dosing should be doubled during acute illness to compensate for stress-related hyperglycemia","D Metformin only needs to be held during the acute illness, not before the contrast procedure","During a home visit, a nurse teaches a patient with longstanding type 2 diabetes and peripheral neuropathy about foot care. The patient says, 'I don't need to check my feet every day since I would feel it if something were wrong.' What is t…","A \"You're right that pain would alert you, but daily inspection is still recommended to check for swelling and blisters.\"","B \"Because neuropathy causes loss of protective sensation, you may not feel an injury, so daily foot inspection is essential.\"","C \"Foot injuries in diabetes are usually painful even with neuropathy, so daily checks are mainly for cosmetic reasons.\"","D \"As long as you see your primary care provider for a monofilament test once a year, daily self-checks aren't necessary.\"","A patient recovering from a stroke has slurred, effortful speech but swallows safely with no coughing, pocketing, or delayed swallow noted. How should the nurse document this finding, and what related complication should the nurse remain al…","A Document dysphagia, and monitor for expressive aphasia as a related complication","B Document dysphagia and dysphasia together, since the two always occur as a pair after a stroke","C Document dysphagia, and monitor for further difficulty talking as the primary concern","D Document dysphasia, and monitor for silent aspiration as a related complication","A nurse prepares to administer a scheduled dose of rapid-acting insulin to a patient with type 2 diabetes. Which actions should the nurse take before giving the dose? Select all that apply.","A Check the patient's current blood glucose level","B Confirm the onset, peak, and duration of the insulin being administered","C Verify the patient's diet order and confirm the patient is not NPO","D Identify how the patient's hypoglycemia would present if it develops","E Administer the dose as soon as it is due, since blood glucose can be checked afterward if the patient seems stable","F Skip verifying diet status since insulin dosing is not affected by oral intake","A nurse checks the gastric residual volume before the next scheduled bolus tube feeding and obtains 400 mL. The patient has no nausea or distension. What should the nurse do?","A Discard the residual, give the scheduled feeding as ordered, and document a normal finding","B Hold the feeding, return the residual to the patient, and recheck the residual in an hour","C Hold the feeding and notify the provider, since a residual over 300 mL requires notification","D Discard the residual, then reduce the feeding volume by half for the next 24 hours","A nurse admits a patient who is significantly overweight but has poor dietary intake, low muscle mass, and recent unintentional weight changes suggesting nutrient deficiency. Which statement about this patient's nutritional status is accura…","A This patient cannot be malnourished because malnutrition only applies to underweight patients","B This patient's weight alone rules out any nutritional risk or need for further assessment","C This patient may still be malnourished, since malnutrition includes both undernutrition and obesity","D This patient's malnutrition should be documented as a medical diagnosis rather than a nursing diagnosis","A nurse is caring for a patient with heart failure who has a 1500 mL/24-hour fluid restriction. How should the nurse best implement this order?","A Distribute the allowed fluid volume across the entire 24-hour period rather than giving it all at once","B Withhold all fluids until the evening, then allow the patient to drink the full 1500 mL at dinner","C Apply the restriction only to water, since other beverages and IV fluids do not count toward the total","D Increase the restriction to 2500 mL/24 hours if the patient reports thirst, since patient comfort takes priority","A patient with newly diagnosed diabetes asks how the hemoglobin A1C test differs from a fingerstick glucose check. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","A \"A1C measures your glucose level at the exact moment blood is drawn, similar to a fingerstick.\"","B \"A1C only confirms a diagnosis and cannot be used to track how well treatment is working over time.\"","C \"A1C reflects your overall blood glucose control averaged over the past three months.\"","D \"A1C must be repeated every time you eat, since it reflects only the last few hours of glucose exposure.\"","A nurse is completing a risk assessment for type 2 diabetes on a 50-year-old patient. Which risk factor identified in the assessment is modifiable, meaning it is a priority target for the nurse's teaching plan?","A A family history of type 2 diabetes in both parents","B Hispanic ethnicity","C Elevated blood pressure and high cholesterol","D A prior history of gestational diabetes","A conscious, alert patient with type 1 diabetes reports feeling shaky and sweaty. Fingerstick glucose is 58 mg/dL. The patient is able to swallow safely. What should the nurse do first?","A Give 15 g of a simple carbohydrate and recheck the glucose in 15 minutes","B Administer IM glucagon immediately to prevent loss of consciousness","C Give a snack of peanut butter and crackers for more sustained glucose","D Push IV D50 dextrose immediately to correct the glucose level faster","A 22-year-old presents with a 2-week history of excessive thirst, hunger, and urination, and has lost weight despite eating more. Labs confirm the patient makes no endogenous insulin. Which statement correctly distinguishes this patient's l…","A This type accounts for the majority of diagnosed diabetes cases and typically develops gradually over years","B This type results from autoimmune destruction of the pancreatic beta cells and has an abrupt onset","C This type is most often diagnosed in adults over age 45 who are screened based on risk factors rather than symptoms","D This type can typically be managed with oral agents alone before eventually progressing to insulin","A patient with a history of type 2 diabetes is admitted with confusion, rapid breathing, and a random blood glucose of 620 mg/dL. Which clinical picture does this most likely represent?","A Mild hyperglycemia requiring only close monitoring and a repeat check in several hours","B Hypoglycemia with paradoxical symptoms due to a chronically elevated baseline glucose","C Prediabetes with impaired glucose tolerance and no need for urgent treatment","D A life-threatening hyperglycemic crisis such as HHS, requiring emergency treatment","A patient's gut is nonfunctional after extensive bowel surgery, and the provider orders full-strength total parenteral nutrition. Why must this be administered through a central line rather than a standard peripheral IV?","A Central lines allow a faster infusion rate, which is required for adequate caloric delivery","B Peripheral veins cannot accommodate any continuous infusion, regardless of concentration","C TPN at this concentration causes phlebitis when infused into a peripheral vein","D Central line placement is required by policy for any nutrition that bypasses the GI tract","A patient recovering from bowel surgery is ordered a clear liquid diet. Which item should the nurse remove from the tray as inappropriate for this diet order?","A Apple juice without pulp","B Plain black coffee","C A cup of milk","D Lemon gelatin","A nurse is administering three separate scheduled medications through a patient's established feeding tube. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","A Verify tube placement before giving any medication","B Flush the tube with water before giving each medication","C Flush the tube with water after giving each medication","D Give the medications one at a time rather than combined","E Crush and mix all three medications together in one syringe to save time","F Confirm placement using the air-insufflation \"whoosh\" method before each dose","A nurse is asked to confirm placement of a newly inserted nasogastric feeding tube before the first feeding is started. Which method should the nurse use?","A Obtaining an X-ray to confirm the tube position before the feeding is started","B Injecting air into the tube while auscultating over the stomach for a \"whoosh\" sound","C Aspirating secretions and testing the pH of the aspirate, without prior imaging","D Asking the patient to swallow water and observing for coughing during the swallow","A patient with diabetes calls the clinic reporting a cold with a temperature of 100.8°F, nausea without vomiting, and a home glucose reading of 210 mg/dL. The patient is tolerating clear fluids and taking all scheduled medications. What sho…","A Continue sick-day management at home with the current medications, more frequent glucose checks, and extra fluids","B Go to the emergency department immediately, since any fever with nausea in a diabetic patient is an emergency","C Stop all diabetes medications until the fever and nausea resolve in order to avoid hypoglycemia","D Notify the provider immediately because the glucose reading of 210 mg/dL exceeds the emergency threshold","A patient has been advanced from a clear liquid diet to a full liquid diet after abdominal surgery. Before advancing the patient further toward a regular diet, what should the nurse assess?","A Whether the patient has completed at least 5-7 days on the full liquid diet as required","B Whether the patient's serum albumin level has returned to a normal value","C Whether the patient can tolerate solid food based on personal preference alone","D Whether the patient is tolerating the current diet without nausea, vomiting, or distension","A patient who normally manages type 1 diabetes with an insulin pump is admitted to the hospital for an unrelated acute illness. What should the nurse expect regarding the patient's diabetes management during this inpatient stay?","A The pump will continue to be used exactly as at home, with no change in monitoring","B The pump and any continuous glucose monitor are deactivated in favor of standard glucose checks","C The pump will be switched to deliver only long-acting basal insulin for the duration of the stay","D The patient will be transitioned to oral diabetic agents instead of insulin during the hospitalization"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-urinary-bowel-elimination.html","t":"Urinary & Bowel Elimination","secs":[{"s":"Urinary & Bowel Elimination","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 7","Urinary Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It","Urinary Retention & UTIs/CAUTIs","Urinary Incontinence","Urinary Assessment: I&O, Urine Characteristics & Testing","Nursing Care for Urinary Problems","Urinary Catheters: Types & Nursing Care","Bowel Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It","Constipation & Impaction","Diarrhea & C. difficile","Bowel Incontinence, Flatulence & Hemorrhoids","Colon Cancer Screening & GI Assessment","NG Tubes & Bowel Training","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Covers both elimination systems: urinary retention/incontinence, UTIs and CAUTIs, catheter types and care, then bowel elimination problems (constipation, impaction, diarrhea, C. diff, incontinence, hemorrhoids), GI assessment, and NG tube/b…"]},{"s":"Urinary Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It▾","a":"urinary-elimination-basics-factors-affecting-it","k":["Urinary Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It▾","Micturition (voiding) is a complex process involving the bladder, urinary sphincters, and CNS: the bladder contracts while the urethral sphincter and pelvic floor relax. The brain can respond immediately or delay the urge briefly.","Factors affecting urinary elimination: growth/development (bladder control develops around 18–24 months), sociocultural factors, psychological factors (anxiety preventing pelvic floor relaxation), personal habits/privacy, fluid intake (caff…","Older adults: decreased nephrons, decreased muscle tone, decreased bladder capacity, a shortened interval between the first urge and an urgent need to void, increased bladder irritability/contractions during filling, and higher risk of urin…"]},{"s":"Urinary Retention & UTIs/CAUTIs▾","a":"urinary-retention-utis-cautis","k":["Urinary Retention & UTIs/CAUTIs▾","Urinary retention = inability to partially or completely empty the bladder — pressure, discomfort, pain/tenderness over the symphysis pubis, restlessness, diaphoresis. Can be acute or chronic (chronic: decreased stream volume, straining, fr…","Diagnosed via post-void residual (PVR) — urine remaining in the bladder after voiding, measured by bladder scanner (ultrasonography, less invasive) or straight catheterization. Bladder scanning is an independent nursing intervention.","UTIs: most commonly caused by E. coli. Upper UTI = kidney infection (flank tenderness); lower UTI = bladder/urethra infection (dysuria, urgency, frequency, suprapubic tenderness, foul-smelling/cloudy urine). Bacteriuria alone (without sympt…","Risk factors for UTI: indwelling catheter, urinary retention, urinary/fecal incontinence, poor perineal hygiene, shorter female urethra, frequent intercourse, uncircumcised status.","CAUTI (catheter-associated UTI) — a major hospital-acquired complication tied directly to catheter presence and dwell time; hospitals are not reimbursed for treating a CAUTI. Prevention: hand hygiene, sterile insertion technique, proper sec…"]},{"s":"Urinary Incontinence▾","a":"urinary-incontinence","k":["Urinary Incontinence▾","Urinary incontinence = involuntary loss of urine; often multifactorial.","Overflow: poor bladder emptying or obstruction (e.g. BPH) — dribbling/leakage.","Stress: leakage with effort/exertion (laughing, coughing, sneezing) — often women, related to weakened pelvic floor muscles (e.g. after childbirth) or sphincter injury.","Urge: an overactive bladder that involuntarily contracts, often with too little warning to reach the bathroom in time — common in older adults, sometimes idiopathic.","Functional: inability to reach the bathroom in time for unrelated reasons (mobility limitations, needing a cane, etc.).","Risk factors: more common in women and older adults; men with BPH; obesity, multiple pregnancies/vaginal births, neurologic disorders (Parkinson's, CVA, spinal cord injury, MS), certain medications, confusion/dementia, immobility, depressio…"]},{"s":"Urinary Assessment: I&O, Urine Characteristics & Testing▾","a":"urinary-assessment-i-o-urine-characteristics-testing","k":["Urinary Assessment: I&O, Urine Characteristics & Testing▾","Normal urine output: greater than 30 mL/hr. Concerning if less than 30 mL/hr for more than 2 hours. Also a concern if an awake patient hasn't voided in 6 hours.","Color: normal is pale straw to amber (concentration-dependent — first morning void is often darker). Hematuria = blood in urine (bright red to light pink). Medications can also change color (blue, orange, green).","Clarity: normal is transparent — urine that sits gets cloudy, so assess freshly voided urine when possible. Thick/cloudy at voiding suggests bacteria/WBCs.","Odor: normal is odorless or a slight ammonia smell; an offensive odor may indicate UTI. Odor strengthens the longer urine sits.","Urinalysis (UA): collect a freshly voided or catheter specimen (clamp near the insertion site for a catheter sample — never take urine from the drainage bag). Urine culture and sensitivity: clean-catch midstream specimen; send within 30 min…","KUB (kidney-ureter-bladder) X-ray: evaluates size/shape/symmetry/structures and can detect urinary calculi (kidney stones); no special prep (not NPO)."]},{"s":"Nursing Care for Urinary Problems▾","a":"nursing-care-for-urinary-problems","k":["Nursing Care for Urinary Problems▾","Fluid intake goal: about 2,300 mL/day if no fluid restriction is needed — helps flush solutes and limit bladder irritability. Stop fluids ~2 hours before bedtime to prevent nocturia.","Retention care: assess for bladder distention, assist to a normal voiding position, run water/flush the commode, apply a cold compress to the lower abdomen, encourage double voiding to help fully empty the bladder. Crede's method requires a…","Infection prevention: follow hospital protocol, assess for signs/symptoms of infection, perform good perineal hygiene, encourage regular voiding and adequate fluids, and teach front-to-back wiping for females.","Incontinence care: be respectful of the patient's feelings, consider pelvic floor muscle training, lifestyle changes, bladder retraining/toileting schedules, intermittent catheterization if needed, and meticulous skin care (identify/treat e…"]},{"s":"Urinary Catheters: Types & Nursing Care▾","a":"urinary-catheters-types-nursing-care","k":["Urinary Catheters: Types & Nursing Care▾","Single-lumen/intermittent (\"in-and-out\") catheter: no balloon — inserted, drains, removed.","Indwelling (Foley) catheter: stays in place with a balloon; some are temperature-sensing (very accurate, common in ICU).","Three-way/three-lumen: used for bladder irrigation. Coudé tip (curved): helps bypass an enlarged prostate in BPH patients.","Suprapubic catheter: placed through the abdominal wall directly into the bladder, sutured in place — used for urethral blockage, urologic surgery, or long-term catheter needs.","External catheters: PureWick (external suction device for females) and condom catheter (males) both reduce infection risk but are not foolproof — patients still need hourly checks to confirm they're actually dry, not a false sense of securi…","Nursing care: regular pericare (front to back for females; retract foreskin for uncircumcised males), secure the catheter to prevent movement in either direction, empty the drainage bag when half full, prevent kinks, keep the bag below the …","Post-removal: patients are at risk for retention — note the removal time; if not voided within 6–8 hours, bladder scan (in-and-out catheterize if >400 mL); monitor ability to void and measure output."]},{"s":"Bowel Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It▾","a":"bowel-elimination-basics-factors-affecting-it","k":["Bowel Elimination Basics & Factors Affecting It▾","Factors: age (bowel control develops around 2–3 years; older adults have more difficulty), diet/fiber, fluid intake, physical activity (sedentary → constipation risk), psychological/personal habits, positioning, pain (hemorrhoids, fissures,…","Older adults: difficulty chewing, slowed esophageal emptying, impaired absorption, weakened sphincters, decreased HCl/lipase, slowed peristalsis, and decreased sensation to defecate."]},{"s":"Constipation & Impaction▾","a":"constipation-impaction","k":["Constipation & Impaction▾","Constipation is a symptom, not a disease — generally fewer than 3 bowel movements/week, but always ask the patient their normal pattern. Symptoms: infrequent stools, discomfort, bloating, hard/dry stools.","Causes: irregular habits, low fiber, low fluid intake, inactivity, stress, medications (especially opioids), advanced age, ignoring the urge to defecate, GI disorders. Untreated straining risks hemorrhoids, anal fissures, fecal impaction, a…","Laxatives/cathartics (patho-pharm detail not required, but know the safety point): only bulk-forming agents (fiber/Metamucil) are safe for daily use. Emollients (docusate/Colace, stool softeners), osmotics, and stimulant cathartics should o…","Enema (NMA) care: position the patient left lateral Sims', have them retain the solution as long as possible, slow the infusion rate (lower the bag) if they cramp, and stop if the abdomen becomes rigid. Document amount instilled and results…","Impaction = unrelieved constipation with inability to expel hardened, retained stool — can progress to bowel obstruction. Highest risk: debilitated patients, spinal cord injury, confused/unconscious patients. Watch for oozing liquid stool a…"]},{"s":"Diarrhea & C. difficile▾","a":"diarrhea-c-difficile","k":["Diarrhea & C. difficile▾","Diarrhea: loose, watery, frequent/urgent stools. Causes: foodborne pathogens, food intolerance/allergy, certain surgeries, diagnostic testing, and enteral tube feedings (a sign of feeding intolerance). Main complications: dehydration and sk…","Antidiarrheals (e.g. loperamide, diphenoxylate/atropine) decrease intestinal motility — determine the cause first, since some causes (infection like C. diff, IBD flares) need treatment of the underlying cause, not just symptom control. Some…","C. diff: a healthcare-associated, spore-forming bacterium causing severe diarrhea, abdominal pain, bloating. Spores survive on surfaces and are not killed by alcohol-based hand sanitizer — must wash hands with soap and water. Risk factors: …","C. diff precautions: contact/spore isolation, clean surfaces with bleach, soap-and-water hand hygiene for everyone entering the room, send a stool sample for confirmation (results ~48 hrs). Treatment: fluids, nutrition, antibiotics (PO/IV),…"]},{"s":"Bowel Incontinence, Flatulence & Hemorrhoids▾","a":"bowel-incontinence-flatulence-hemorrhoids","k":["Bowel Incontinence, Flatulence & Hemorrhoids▾","Bowel incontinence = inability to control passage of stool/gas. Causes: muscle/nerve damage, sphincter dysfunction, constipation or diarrhea, large stool volumes, surgery, rectal prolapse. Risk factors: age, female sex, nerve damage (spinal…","Flatulence: abdominal distension, cramping, bloating, pain. Causes: constipation, food intolerances, GI disease (gastroenteritis, IBS, Crohn's, celiac), stress. Care: avoid gas-producing foods, small frequent meals, eat/drink slowly, limit …","Hemorrhoids: dilated/engorged rectal veins from increased venous pressure (straining, pregnancy, heart failure, liver disease, prolonged toilet sitting). External hemorrhoids are visible/purplish and may need excision; internal ones aren't …"]},{"s":"Colon Cancer Screening & GI Assessment▾","a":"colon-cancer-screening-gi-assessment","k":["Colon Cancer Screening & GI Assessment▾","Colon cancer is the #2 leading cause of cancer death in the U.S. (men and women combined). Risk factors: African American race, diets high in red/processed meat and low in fiber, obesity, age 50+, inactivity, alcohol/tobacco use, family his…","Screening typically starts at age 45; frequency depends on risk/findings. Minimums: flexible sigmoidoscopy every 5 years, colonoscopy every 10 years. Stool DNA/occult blood tests are used but are less accurate than direct colonoscopy visual…","GI assessment: usual elimination pattern, stool description, diet/fluid intake, GI history/surgeries, medications, emotional state, activity level. The Bristol stool chart is used to describe stool form/consistency (know that it exists and …","No single blood test diagnoses most GI disorders; if stool blood is detected, an H&H is often ordered to gauge blood loss. Fecal occult blood test (FOBT): checks for hidden blood (screens for cancer/unexplained anemia) — needs samples from …"]},{"s":"NG Tubes & Bowel Training▾","a":"ng-tubes-bowel-training","k":["NG Tubes & Bowel Training▾","An NG tube can be used for feeding/medication (see Nutrition) or, more distinctly in bowel care, for gastric decompression — keeping the stomach empty via low wall suction.","NG tube assessment: nasal skin integrity, tube position/migration (chart the tube's cm marking), and suction drainage (amount, color, clarity). Confirm placement with pH strips or abdominal X-ray. Too much suction can erode the gastric muco…","Bowel training (for chronic constipation or fecal incontinence): a consistent, patient-centered daily routine — choose a realistic time (around the patient's normal schedule/work hours), offer fluids to stimulate defecation, assist to the c…","Older adult considerations: encourage cancer screening, adequate fiber/fluids, regular activity, and remember older adults compensate less well for fluid loss from diarrhea — may need IV rather than PO fluids."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nurse needs to obtain a urine specimen from a patient with an indwelling catheter for a urinalysis. What is the correct technique?","A Disconnect the catheter from the tubing and collect urine as it drains","B Aspirate urine directly from the drainage bag using a sterile syringe","C Empty the drainage bag and pour a specimen into a sterile container","D Clamp the tubing briefly, then withdraw urine from the specimen port","A postoperative patient has not voided in several hours and reports suprapubic pressure and discomfort. A bladder scan confirms a full bladder. Which nursing interventions are appropriate at this time? (Select all that apply.)","A Assist the patient to a normal voiding position (e.g., standing or sitting upright)","B Run water at the sink or pour warm water over the perineum to stimulate voiding","C Encourage double voiding to help the bladder empty more completely","D Perform Crede's method on the bladder without first obtaining a provider order","E Apply a cold compress to the lower abdomen to stimulate voiding","F Immediately insert an indwelling catheter as the first-line intervention","An older adult with chronic constipation asks the nurse which of their bowel medications is safe to take every day, long-term, without risk of dependence. Which class should the nurse identify?","A Stimulant cathartic agents","B Osmotic laxative agents","C Bulk-forming fiber agents","D Emollient stool softeners","A patient has had an indwelling urinary catheter in place for 4 days for strict intake/output monitoring but is now hemodynamically stable and no longer critically ill. Hospitals are not reimbursed for treating catheter-associated UTIs (CAU…","A Irrigating the catheter with normal saline every shift for patency","B Advocating for catheter removal once it is no longer necessary","C Changing the catheter every 72 hours to reduce biofilm buildup","D Increasing the frequency of perineal care to twice daily","While administering a cleansing enema, the nurse notes the patient's abdomen has become rigid and the patient reports sudden, severe abdominal pain. What should the nurse do first?","A Slow the infusion rate by lowering the enema bag","B Reposition the patient onto their right side and continue","C Stop the procedure immediately and notify the provider","D Encourage the patient to retain the solution a few minutes longer","A patient reports a sense of incomplete bladder emptying after voiding. The nurse wants to assess the post-void residual (PVR) volume. Which action can the nurse initiate without a provider order?","A Insert a straight catheter immediately to measure residual volume","B Order a KUB X-ray to visualize the degree of bladder filling","C Administer a diuretic to assess response and estimate retention","D Use a bladder scanner to estimate the residual urine volume","A nurse is caring for a patient newly diagnosed with Clostridioides difficile (C. diff) infection who is being placed on precautions. Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? (Select all that apply.)","A Wash hands with soap and water before and after providing care","B Instruct visitors and staff to use alcohol-based hand sanitizer when entering the room","C Clean the patient's room and equipment surfaces with a bleach-based disinfectant","D Place the patient in a private room with contact/spore isolation precautions","E Send a stool sample to confirm the diagnosis, expecting results in about 48 hours","F Apply standard precautions only, since C. diff spreads via airborne droplets","A nurse is preparing to catheterize a male patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) who has a history of difficult catheter insertion. Which type of catheter is designed to help navigate this situation?","A A coudé-tip catheter with a curved end","B A three-way catheter for continuous irrigation","C A suprapubic catheter placed through the abdominal wall","D A temperature-sensing indwelling catheter","A nurse observes a new graduate nurse caring for a patient with an indwelling catheter. Which action by the new nurse requires the preceptor to intervene?","A Placing the drainage bag on the patient's bed during transport to radiology","B Emptying the drainage bag when it is about half full","C Documenting the catheter as still clinically necessary during shift assessment","D Keeping the drainage tubing free of kinks and dependent loops","A nurse is caring for a female patient using a PureWick external catheter system to reduce infection risk compared to an indwelling catheter. Which nursing action is still necessary?","A Restrict the patient's oral fluid intake to reduce output","B Perform hourly checks to confirm the patient is actually staying dry","C Replace the device with an indwelling catheter after 24 hours","D Discontinue perineal hygiene checks since the device manages moisture","A postoperative patient has an indwelling catheter with hourly urine output measured. Over the past 3 hours, hourly output has been 25 mL, 22 mL, and 20 mL. What should the nurse do?","A Wait one more hour before reporting since only one hour has elapsed below threshold","B Continue routine monitoring since output above 15 mL/hr is expected postoperatively","C Increase the IV fluid rate independently to boost output before reassessing","D Notify the provider, since output has been below 30 mL/hr for more than 2 hours","A nurse assesses a debilitated, bedbound patient with no documented bowel movement in 5 days. The chart shows several episodes of loose, liquid stool over the past 24 hours, charted as \"diarrhea.\" On assessment the nurse notes a firm, diste…","A Administer the PRN antidiarrheal medication for the loose stools","B Hold the patient's stool softener because the stools are loose","C Perform a digital rectal exam to check for a hardened stool mass","D Document the loose stool as diarrhea and reassess in 24 hours","A nurse is administering a cleansing enema. The patient reports mild cramping, but the abdomen remains soft and non-distended. What should the nurse do?","A Stop the enema immediately and notify the provider","B Raise the enema bag to infuse the solution more quickly","C Lower the enema bag to slow the rate of infusion","D Reposition the patient to supine and continue at the same rate","A 68-year-old woman reports that she leaks small amounts of urine whenever she laughs, coughs, or lifts her grandchild, but does not experience leakage at other times. Which type of urinary incontinence does this describe?","A Urge incontinence","B Overflow incontinence","C Functional incontinence","D Stress incontinence","A patient's indwelling catheter was removed at 8:00 AM. By 3:00 PM, the patient has not voided. What should the nurse do?","A Reinsert an indwelling catheter immediately without further assessment","B Continue waiting, since up to 12 hours without voiding is expected after catheter removal","C Perform a bladder scan, and in-and-out catheterize if volume exceeds 400 mL","D Encourage oral fluids and recheck again the following morning","A patient has an NG tube connected to suction for gastric decompression. The nurse notes the suction is set higher than the provider's order, and the drainage has become blood-tinged. What is the priority nursing concern?","A The high suction setting is likely causing erosion of the gastric mucosa","B The tube has migrated into the esophagus and needs repositioning","C The patient is developing a paralytic ileus from prolonged gastric suction","D The elevated suction is pulling stomach acid into the lungs","A nurse is teaching a patient how to correctly collect a fecal occult blood test (FOBT) sample at home. Which instruction is correct?","A Collect a sample from one area of a single stool and send it immediately","B Collect samples from 2 different areas of a formed stool, across 3 different stools, and avoid red meat beforehand","C Collect a liquid stool sample from 3 different areas on the same day","D Collect samples from 3 different areas of a formed stool, across 2 different stools, and eat red meat to improve sensitivity","A 46-year-old patient with no significant risk factors or family history asks the nurse when colon cancer screening should begin and how often a colonoscopy is needed if results are normal. What should the nurse explain?","A Screening starts at age 45, with colonoscopy repeated every 10 years","B Screening starts at age 50, with colonoscopy repeated every 5 years","C Screening starts at age 40, with colonoscopy repeated every 3 years","D Screening starts at age 45, with flexible sigmoidoscopy repeated every 10 years","The nurse determines that digital removal of stool is necessary for a patient with confirmed fecal impaction. Which action is correct?","A Insert one gloved, lubricated finger and quickly extract the entire stool mass at once","B Double-glove, lubricate, and break up and remove the stool in sections","C Perform the procedure without lubrication to improve tactile sensation","D Delegate the procedure to unlicensed assistive personnel to save time","A urinalysis on an asymptomatic older adult admitted for hip surgery incidentally reveals bacteria in the urine. The patient has no dysuria, fever, or new confusion, and vital signs are stable. What is the most appropriate nursing action?","A Anticipate an order for antibiotics to treat the bacteriuria","B Recognize that bacteriuria without symptoms is not routinely treated","C Insert an indwelling catheter to obtain a sterile specimen for culture","D Notify the provider that the patient has a UTI requiring isolation"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-integumentary.html","t":"Integumentary","secs":[{"s":"Integumentary","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 8","Skin Assessment Basics","Skin Color Changes","Pressure Injuries: Risk Factors & Staging","Other Skin Damage & Prevention Interventions","Wound Types & Factors Affecting Healing","Wound Assessment: Color, Measurement & Drainage","Wound Dressings","Surgical Wound Complications","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Covers skin assessment and abnormal findings, pressure injury risk/staging/prevention, wound types and assessment (measurement, drainage), wound dressings, and surgical wound complications (hemorrhage, dehiscence, evisceration, infection)."]},{"s":"Skin Assessment Basics▾","a":"skin-assessment-basics","k":["Skin Assessment Basics▾","The skin has two layers: the epidermis (outer, includes the basal layer where cells proliferate and slough) and the dermis (inner — strength/support/protection; contains capillaries, sweat glands, nerve endings, hair follicles). Skin is the…","Assessment components: color (uniformity, pallor — check mucous membranes/lips/palms in dark skin tones), moisture (dry vs. diaphoretic — diaphoresis at rest is a significant finding), temperature (warm is normal; cold/hot can indicate infe…","Pitting edema: excess fluid buildup, usually in dependent areas (legs/ankles/feet if mobile; sacrum/genitalia if bedbound). Must actually palpate to assess. Graded on a 0–4+ pitting scale."]},{"s":"Skin Color Changes▾","a":"skin-color-changes","k":["Skin Color Changes▾","Pallor (loss of color) — in darker skin tones can look gray; check mucous membranes. Suggests anemia, shock, or poor blood flow. Ask the patient/family if this is their normal baseline.","Cyanosis (blue discoloration) — in darker skin tones can look yellowish-brown or gray; check nail beds, lips, mucosa, palms. Indicates hypoxia or impaired venous return.","Jaundice (yellow discoloration) — seen in skin, sclera, and mucous membranes; indicates liver dysfunction/red blood cell breakdown. In darker skin, check the palms (sclera can have a normal slight yellow tint in some patients).","Erythema (redness) — can indicate the start of pressure-related skin breakdown, inflammation, vasodilation, sun exposure, or elevated temperature. Palpate for warmth/texture change since redness itself is harder to see in darker skin tones.…"]},{"s":"Pressure Injuries: Risk Factors & Staging▾","a":"pressure-injuries-risk-factors-staging","k":["Pressure Injuries: Risk Factors & Staging▾","Pressure injury (pressure ulcer, decubitus ulcer, \"bed sore\") = localized tissue injury from unrelieved prolonged pressure — compresses capillaries, cuts off blood flow, and causes tissue ischemia. Can also be caused by a medical device (na…","Pressure injuries are a nursing-sensitive quality indicator — a patient outcome directly influenced by nursing care (along with falls and CAUTIs) — meaning nursing owns this outcome.","Three factors in development: pressure intensity (body weight, surface hardness), duration (time without repositioning), and tissue tolerance (perfusion, nutrition, aging, hydration status).","Staging: Stage 1 (non-blanchable redness, intact skin) through Stage 4 (full-thickness with exposed bone/muscle/tendon) — know that a higher stage is worse; exact staging criteria are FYI-level detail.","Deep tissue injury (DTI): persistent non-blanchable deep red/maroon/purple discoloration from pressure/shear at the bone-muscle interface — looks like a bruise but is not traumatic in origin.","Unstageable: the wound bed is obscured by slough or eschar, so the true depth/stage can't be determined until debrided.","Blanchable vs. non-blanchable: press a reddened area — if it turns white/lighter and the redness returns after release, it's blanchable (better prognosis, skin may still recover). Non-blanchable redness suggests deep tissue damage is alread…","Braden Scale: a risk assessment tool ranging 6–23 — a lower score = higher risk. Used every shift for acute/progressive-care patients; less reliable in critical care (a separate scale, Jackson-Cubbin, is used in the ICU — not testable detai…"]},{"s":"Other Skin Damage & Prevention Interventions▾","a":"other-skin-damage-prevention-interventions","k":["Other Skin Damage & Prevention Interventions▾","MASD (moisture-associated skin damage): from prolonged exposure to urine/stool. Prevent with frequent checks and barrier creams — keep the patient dry.","Intertrigo: inflammatory dermatitis from moist skin rubbing against itself in skin folds (under breasts/pannus, in armpits) — can develop yeast and painful cracked skin; needs to stay clean/dry, and a yeast infection needs a prescription an…","Periwound/peristomal damage: skin breakdown around a wound or stoma from enzyme/exudate exposure — ensure a well-fitting stoma appliance and keep surrounding skin dry.","Prevention interventions: adequate nutrition/protein supplementation, incontinence and moisture management (barrier cream, wicking absorbent pads — adult diapers can give a false sense of security), frequent repositioning (turn at least eve…","Nursing's role: thorough, consistent assessment of at-risk areas and any medical devices (nasal cannula, NG tube, braces), and catching redness while it's still blanchable — before it progresses to non-blanchable or open skin breakdown."]},{"s":"Wound Types & Factors Affecting Healing▾","a":"wound-types-factors-affecting-healing","k":["Wound Types & Factors Affecting Healing▾","Acute wounds (trauma, surgical incision) proceed through the normal timely repair process and return to normal function — clean, well-approximated edges heal well.","Chronic wounds (pressure ulcers, vascular insufficiency wounds, diabetic ulcers) fail to progress through normal healing and don't return to normal structure/function — often because impaired blood flow can't deliver the oxygen/nutrients ne…","Factors affecting healing: nutrition (deficiencies in vitamin A, C, zinc, copper, and protein delay healing — high-protein diet/supplements and multivitamins may be ordered; monitor albumin/prealbumin), tissue perfusion (oxygenated blood de…","Wound management has 3 key components: assessment, cleansing, and protection."]},{"s":"Wound Assessment: Color, Measurement & Drainage▾","a":"wound-assessment-color-measurement-drainage","k":["Wound Assessment: Color, Measurement & Drainage▾","Wound color: red = healthy/\"beefy red\" granulation tissue (good). Yellow = purulent drainage or slough (dead cells/exudate — can harbor bacteria/hide infection; a wound care team consult is appropriate). Black = eschar (dead tissue) — usual…","Measurement: in centimeters — first measurement is head-to-toe, second is side-to-side, third is depth. Tunneling and undermining are charted by clock position, with 12 o'clock toward the patient's head. Example: \"full-thickness, red wound,…","Closed/surgical wounds: skin edges should be well-approximated (staples/sutures/tissue adhesive); note any drains/tubes, pain control, and odor (infected wounds often have a noticeable odor).","Photograph wounds regularly (often daily) to track progression objectively over time.","Serous — Clear, watery, slightly yellow — Normal — like blister fluid; seen in clean wounds","Sanguineous — Red (bright = active bleeding; dark/brown = older blood) — Blood — deep or highly vascular wounds","Serosanguineous — Pale pink (mix of serous + sanguineous) — Common in new wounds","Purulent — Thick, yellow/tan/green/brown, cloudy/milky — Infection — contains WBCs, tissue debris, bacteria","Amount is usually documented subjectively as scant, moderate, large, or copious (weighing a dressing is possible — 1 g ≈ 1 mL of drainage — but rarely done in practice). Also assess and document odor, consistency, and the integrity of the s…"]},{"s":"Wound Dressings▾","a":"wound-dressings","k":["Wound Dressings▾","Woven gauze/sponges: absorb exudate. Non-adherent material (\"Adaptic\"): doesn't stick to the wound bed — protects new tissue during frequent dressing changes.","Wet-to-dry (really wet-to-damp): mechanical debridement until granulation forms; a moist wound bed supports epithelial cell movement and wound closure.","Transparent/self-adhesive (Tegaderm): use cautiously — can pull off healthy surrounding skin on removal.","Hydrocolloid (Duoderm): occlusive, swells with exudate, maintains a moist environment; typically changed about every 3 days (sooner if saturated).","Hydrogel: mostly water, gels on contact with exudate, promotes autolytic debridement and rehydrates the wound bed — used for infected/necrotic wounds, not heavily draining ones (may need a secondary occlusive dressing).","Alginate: non-adherent, conforms to the wound shape, absorbs exudate while maintaining moisture. Collagen: powder/paste/granules/gel that helps stop bleeding and promote healing.","Wound VAC (negative-pressure/vacuum-assisted closure): foam in the wound bed under an occlusive dressing, connected to negative pressure (commonly −125 mm Hg, per order) — increases tissue perfusion, decreases swelling, stimulates granulati…"]},{"s":"Surgical Wound Complications▾","a":"surgical-wound-complications","k":["Surgical Wound Complications▾","Evisceration (viscera protruding through a wound opening) is an emergency: stay with the patient, cover the wound/organs with a sterile saline-soaked dressing (do not attempt to reinsert), position supine with knees bent (or low Fowler's) t…","Hemorrhage: blood vessel damage — may show as sanguineous drainage, swelling/distention, or a developing hematoma (a localized blood collection — larger and firmer than a simple bruise). Watch for subtle vital sign changes (rising HR, falli…","Dehiscence: partial or total separation of a sutured wound's underlying layers — usually occurs 3–11 days post-op, before collagen has fully formed. Risk factors: obesity, coughing, infection, poor suture technique, decreased blood flow. Ma…","Evisceration = dehiscence with visceral organs protruding through the opening — often preceded by straining (coughing, straining to defecate, vomiting, sneezing), a sudden \"popping\"/giving-way sensation, and a marked increase in serosanguin…","Shared risk factors for dehiscence/evisceration: chronic disease (diabetes, PVD), advanced age, obesity, invasive abdominal surgery, vomiting/coughing/sneezing/straining, dehydration, malnutrition, ineffective suturing, infection.","Surgical site infection: typically presents 2–11 days post-op — pain, redness/swelling, purulent drainage, fever, chills, odor, increased pulse/respiratory rate, elevated WBC count. Risk factors: age extremes, immune suppression, impaired c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nursing student is taught to assess skin turgor as part of a hydration assessment. Which technique reflects the correct approach, especially in an older adult patient?","A Pinch the skin on the back of the hand, since aging does not affect skin elasticity there","B Assess turgor only in dependent areas such as the ankles, since fluid pools there first","C Skip turgor assessment in older adults since it is not a reliable indicator at any site","D Assess turgor at the collarbone or upper arm, since these sites are less affected by aging","A nurse is assessing a patient with darkly pigmented skin for early signs of skin breakdown and circulatory changes. Which assessment techniques are appropriate? (Select all that apply.)","A Check the mucous membranes, lips, and palms for pallor rather than relying on overall skin tone","B Assume any area that looks the same color as the surrounding skin cannot be an early pressure injury","C Check the nail beds, lips, and mucosa for a bluish, yellowish-brown, or gray tint suggestive of cyanosis","D Rely solely on visual inspection for erythema, since redness is equally visible across all skin tones","E Palpate for warmth and changes in skin texture, since redness may be harder to visually detect","F Ask the patient or family whether the finding matches the patient's normal baseline coloring","A nurse notes a persistent area of deep purple, non-blanchable discoloration over a patient's heel. The patient has no history of trauma to the area but has had limited mobility for several days. Which explanation should guide the nurse's u…","A This is a normal bruise from an unwitnessed minor injury requiring no action","B This is a stage 1 pressure injury, since the skin over the area is intact","C This is cyanosis suggesting impaired venous return in the lower extremity","D This is a deep tissue injury from pressure and shear at the bone-muscle interface","A nurse identifies a reddened, warm area of skin on a patient's lower leg suspected to be cellulitis and outlines its border with a skin marker. What is the primary purpose of this action?","A To determine whether the redness is spreading over time","B To make the area easier to photograph for the medical record","C To numb the area before the provider examines it","D To indicate where a dressing should be applied","A nurse assesses a reddened area over a bedbound patient's sacrum. When pressure is applied with a gloved finger, the area turns white; when pressure is released, the redness returns within a few seconds. How should the nurse interpret this…","A This is non-blanchable erythema, indicating deep tissue damage has occurred","B This is blanchable erythema, indicating intact blood flow and possible recovery","C This finding indicates the area has progressed to an unstageable pressure injury","D This finding indicates a deep tissue injury at the bone-muscle interface","A provider orders a wound VAC (negative-pressure wound therapy) for a patient with a large, non-healing wound. Which statement reflects correct understanding of this therapy?","A The nurse may adjust the pressure setting independently based on the volume of drainage in the canister","B This therapy is primarily indicated for small, clean, well-approximated surgical incisions","C A provider order must specify the negative pressure setting and dressing change frequency","D The dressing is changed daily regardless of the canister volume or provider order","A nurse is selecting a dressing for a patient with fragile periwound skin who needs an occlusive dressing that maintains a moist wound environment and typically doesn't need to be changed daily. Which dressing best fits this need, and what …","A Alginate, which is changed daily; transparent film dressings absorb heavy exudate well without risk","B Hydrocolloid, changed every 3 days; transparent dressings like Tegaderm can strip healthy skin","C Hydrogel, changed every 3 days; hydrocolloid dressings should be avoided due to skin stripping risk","D Hydrocolloid, changed daily; alginate dressings are contraindicated in patients with fragile skin","A patient is 7 days post-op and develops a fever of 101.8 F, increasing wound pain, purulent drainage, and an elevated WBC count. Which complication is this presentation most consistent with?","A Wound dehiscence, which typically presents with these findings in the first week post-op","B Surgical site infection, which commonly presents 2-11 days post-op","C Hemorrhage, evidenced by the rising temperature and increasing wound pain","D Evisceration, which is preceded by fever and purulent drainage before organs protrude","Six hours after abdominal surgery, a nurse notes the patient's heart rate has risen from 82 to 108 and blood pressure has dropped from 128/76 to 106/62. A firm, tender area of swelling is developing near the incision. Which complication sho…","A Surgical site infection, because fever is the earliest sign of a wound problem","B Dehiscence, because the wound layers are separating beneath the swelling","C Hemorrhage, because a hematoma and falling blood pressure suggest active bleeding","D MASD, because moisture trapped near the incision is causing tissue damage","A nurse enters a postoperative patient's room and finds a loop of bowel protruding through an open abdominal incision. The patient reports a sudden popping sensation and increased drainage on the dressing. Which actions should the nurse tak…","A Attempt to gently reposition the protruding bowel back through the incision before the provider arrives","B Remain with the patient and call for help rather than leaving the room to notify the provider by phone","C Cover the wound and protruding organs with a sterile dressing soaked in saline","D Position the patient supine with the knees bent to reduce tension on the abdomen","E Keep the patient NPO in anticipation of a return to surgery","F Offer the patient a small amount of water to keep the mouth from feeling dry","A nurse assesses a wound dressing and finds thick, cloudy, yellow-green drainage with a foul odor. What does this finding most likely indicate, and what should the nurse do?","A This is serosanguineous drainage typical of a new wound; continue routine care","B This is purulent drainage indicating infection; notify the provider promptly","C This is sanguineous drainage from a highly vascular wound; apply a pressure dressing","D This is serous drainage similar to blister fluid; no intervention is needed","A nurse is assessing a bedbound patient for dependent edema. Where should the nurse prioritize checking for pitting edema in this patient, and how is the finding documented?","A The ankles and feet, graded on a 0-4+ scale, since gravity pulls fluid there regardless of mobility","B The sacrum and genitalia, graded on a 0-4+ scale, since gravity pulls fluid there while supine","C The sacrum and genitalia, documented as simply present or absent without a grading scale","D The hands and face, graded on a 0-4+ scale, since these areas are easiest to visually inspect without palpation","A nurse is planning pressure injury prevention for an immobile patient. Which intervention reflects the recommended minimum turning frequency to reduce pressure injury risk?","A Reposition the patient at least every 4 hours to allow adequate rest","B Reposition the patient once per shift and rely on the specialty mattress for pressure relief","C Reposition the patient only when they report discomfort","D Reposition the patient at least every 2 hours and use pillows to float the heels off the bed","A nurse reviews Braden Scale scores for four patients on a med-surg unit. Which patient is at the greatest risk for developing a pressure injury?","A A patient with a Braden score of 22","B A patient with a Braden score of 18","C A patient with a Braden score of 15","D A patient with a Braden score of 10","A nurse is reviewing labs for a patient with a slow-healing wound. The provider wants to know if the patient's nutritional status has improved over just the past several days, rather than over recent weeks. Which lab value should the nurse …","A Albumin, because it reflects chronic nutritional status","B Hemoglobin, because it reflects immediate protein intake","C Prealbumin, because it reflects more acute nutritional status","D Serum glucose, because it reflects short-term protein stores","A patient is 5 days post-op from abdominal surgery. The nurse notes serosanguineous drainage has increased and the incision edges appear separated, but no abdominal organs are visible. Which is the priority nursing action?","A Notify the provider, limit straining, and apply wet-to-damp dressings to promote granulation","B Reassure the patient this is normal healing at this stage and continue routine dressing changes","C Apply a sterile saline-soaked dressing, notify the provider, and prepare for evisceration","D Apply an occlusive hydrocolloid dressing over the incision and reassess it in 3 days","A nurse is caring for two patients: one with urinary incontinence who has redness and skin breakdown in the perineal area, and another with painful, cracked, moist skin in the folds beneath a pannus. How should the nurse best differentiate …","A Both are the same condition, so the location of the skin breakdown does not matter for treatment","B The perineal breakdown is intertrigo from friction, while the skin fold breakdown is MASD from urine exposure","C Both conditions are treated the same way, by keeping the area covered with an occlusive dressing","D The perineal breakdown is MASD from urine exposure, while the skin fold breakdown is intertrigo from friction","A nurse cares for a patient with a colostomy and notes redness and skin breakdown around the stoma site. Which intervention is most appropriate to address this finding?","A Apply a wet-to-dry dressing directly over the peristomal skin","B Ensure the appliance fits well and keep the peristomal skin dry","C Increase the frequency of appliance changes to twice daily regardless of fit","D Apply an antifungal cream to the peristomal skin as a first-line measure","A nurse is caring for a patient with a diabetic foot ulcer that has failed to progress through the expected stages of healing for several weeks. Which underlying mechanism most likely explains this chronic wound's failure to heal?","A Impaired blood flow limits oxygen and nutrient delivery to the tissue","B The wound edges are too well-approximated, which slows granulation","C The wound was covered with an occlusive dressing too soon after injury","D The patient's dermis has stopped producing new epidermal cells entirely","A nurse is documenting a wound measuring 4 cm in one direction, 2 cm in a perpendicular direction, and 1.5 cm in depth, with a tunnel noted toward the area closest to the patient's feet. How should the nurse chart the wound dimensions and t…","A 2 x 4 x 1.5 cm, with the tunnel charted near 12 o'clock, since 6 o'clock is oriented toward the head","B 1.5 x 4 x 2 cm, with the tunnel charted near 6 o'clock, since depth is measured first","C 4 x 2 x 1.5 cm, with the tunnel charted near 6 o'clock, since 12 o'clock is oriented toward the head","D 4 x 2 x 1.5 cm, with the tunnel charted near 12 o'clock, since the deepest point is always at 12 o'clock"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-neurosensory-pain.html","t":"Neurosensory & Pain","secs":[{"s":"Neurosensory & Pain","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 9","Neuro Assessment Basics: ABCs, GCS & LOC","Neuro Diagnostics: X-ray, CT, MRI & EEG","Neuro Patient Safety & Care","Sensory Deprivation & Overload","Sensory Deficits & Nursing Care","Headaches & Migraines","Pain Basics: Physiology & Types","Pain Assessment","Non-Pharmacological Pain Management","Pharmacological Pain Management","PCA & Epidural Analgesia","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Combines two lecture sets: neurosensory (neuro assessment/ABCs/GCS, diagnostics, patient safety/care, sensory deprivation/overload, sensory deficits, headaches) and pain (physiology/types, assessment, non-pharmacological and pharmacological…"]},{"s":"Neuro Assessment Basics: ABCs, GCS & LOC▾","a":"neuro-assessment-basics-abcs-gcs-loc","k":["Neuro Assessment Basics: ABCs, GCS & LOC▾","Always check ABCs first with any neuro decline — a GCS of 8 or lower means the patient typically can't protect their own airway. If a patient is declining neurologically, first rule out reversible physiologic causes.","The 4 H's to check with any neuro change: Hypoxia, Hypoglycemia (checking blood glucose is part of stroke protocol — hypoglycemia can mimic stroke symptoms), Hypotension, Hypoventilation.","GCS scoring: 3–15 total (3 is the lowest possible — a GCS of 0 doesn't exist). Severe brain injury: ≤8. Moderate: 9–12. Mild: 13–15. You don't need to memorize every point value, but should recognize an 8 is far worse than a 14.","Level of consciousness terms (best to worst): Alert (awake, easily aroused, responsive) → Lethargic/somnolent (drowsy, awakens to voice, responds appropriately but slowly — normal if just woken from sleep, abnormal if constant) → Obtunded (…","Pupil changes and gag reflex loss are late signs of neurologic decline — unequal pupils in a patient with known head injury/stroke is an emergency.","A focused neuro assessment is needed for: any abnormal finding on a basic assessment, known neuro disorders, trauma/head injury, drug-induced states, or patient-reported neuro complaints."]},{"s":"Neuro Diagnostics: X-ray, CT, MRI & EEG▾","a":"neuro-diagnostics-x-ray-ct-mri-eeg","k":["Neuro Diagnostics: X-ray, CT, MRI & EEG▾","Skull/spinal X-ray: shows bones only — good for fracture, spinal alignment; cannot show a brain bleed or stroke. C-collars can stay on during neck X-rays.","CT scan: the gold standard for acute stroke/brain bleed identification — fast 3D imaging of bone/tissue/organs; IV contrast needed to evaluate circulation. Requires informed consent, screening for iodine/shellfish allergy (contrast reaction…","MRI: more detailed than CT, no radiation exposure, but expensive/less available and takes longer — usually done after CT if the CT is inconclusive. Requires screening for metal (implants, devices, old surgical hardware) and removing medicat…","EEG (electroencephalogram): monitors brain electrical activity — used to diagnose seizures, evaluate sleep disorders, and can be part of confirming brain death. Electrodes attached with a conduction paste that's hard to remove from hair (do…","Universal safety note: always confirm an armband is on before any imaging procedure — techs will not proceed without one."]},{"s":"Neuro Patient Safety & Care▾","a":"neuro-patient-safety-care","k":["Neuro Patient Safety & Care▾","Fall precautions: bed in lowest position, call light in reach, adequate lighting, clear floor, frequent/hourly rounding.","Communication: calm approach, assume the patient can hear you unless told otherwise, explain care in simple instructions, involve speech-language pathology (SLP) for communication difficulty.","Nutrition/hydration: assume dysphagia risk and keep NPO until a formal swallow evaluation clears the patient (part of stroke protocol) — educate family not to bring food/water. May need enteral tube feeding or, as a last resort, TPN. Monito…","Skin: assess bony prominences and any neglected side, turn every 2 hours, elevate heels, consider specialty mattresses.","Mobility: passive (nurse-assisted) vs. active (patient-performed) range of motion — prevents contractures and supports strength; get patients up to a chair when possible (also helps prevent DVT).","Elimination: monitor for constipation (from immobility) and urinary retention (may require a long-term catheter in severe deficits); keep incontinent patients clean/dry.","Controlled environment: limit disturbances, protect uninterrupted sleep, maintain a normal sleep-wake cycle, involve family in care planning.","Seizure precautions: padded bed rails, working suction available, oxygen available — bring this forward from prior coursework. Patients with brain injury/bleeding are at increased seizure risk from irritated brain tissue.","Handoff report: for a patient at GCS 15 with no deficits, a brief statement suffices; for anything less than 15, give detailed specifics (which side is weak, what's changed) so the incoming nurse can detect any new acute change."]},{"s":"Sensory Deprivation & Overload▾","a":"sensory-deprivation-overload","k":["Sensory Deprivation & Overload▾","Three components of any sensory experience: reception (stimulation of a receptor), perception (interpreting the stimulus — impaired by anything affecting LOC), and reaction (the response).","Factors influencing sensory function: age (extremes at higher risk for overload or deprivation), amount of meaningful stimuli (hospitalization removes normal stimuli like pets/family/work), amount of stimuli (excess → overload), social inte…","Sensory deprivation: caused by isolation, loss/impairment of senses, confinement, emotional disorders, brain injury, or certain medications. Effects — cognitive (decreased attention/memory/problem-solving), affective (boredom, depression, a…","Sensory overload: excessive stimuli prevent a meaningful brain response — common in the ICU (alarms, lights, conversations). Symptoms: fatigue, irritability, disorientation, decreased problem-solving, racing thoughts, restlessness, anxiety …","Nursing care — deprivation: short, meaningful stimulation throughout the day, tactile stimulation (hair brushing, back rubs), frequent reorientation, controlled visitor numbers, environmental variety (window seating, larger clock), assistiv…"]},{"s":"Sensory Deficits & Nursing Care▾","a":"sensory-deficits-nursing-care","k":["Sensory Deficits & Nursing Care▾","Vision: presbyopia (age-related farsightedness). Care: announce your presence, stay in their visual field, speak in warm/normal tones (not necessarily louder), explain care before starting, orient to the room, keep paths clear, describe foo…","Hearing: presbycusis (age-related, usually symmetrical hearing loss) — always check for cerumen (earwax) impaction first. Care: amplify sound appropriately, ensure hearing aids are in and working, add flashing lights for alarms/safety, spea…","Taste/smell: xerostomia (dry mouth) affects taste. Care: well-seasoned/appealing food, separate textures, stimulate appetite with pleasant aromas, remove unpleasant odors. Safety for smell loss: working smoke detectors, checking food expira…","Tactile: seen with peripheral neuropathy (diabetes), spinal cord injury, and phantom limb pain after amputation. Care: therapeutic touch (hair brushing, back rubs) when appropriate, protect insensate pressure areas, watch for hyperesthesia …","Communication deficits — aphasia: expressive aphasia = understands language but cannot produce/express words. Receptive aphasia = cannot understand spoken/written language, though words can still be spoken. Care: be patient, don't rush, use…"]},{"s":"Headaches & Migraines▾","a":"headaches-migraines","k":["Headaches & Migraines▾","Three main types: tension, migraine, and cluster headaches.","Migraines: recurring, unilateral throbbing pain — more common in females; may have a preceding aura (e.g. visual disturbances). Most common ages 25–55, family history is a risk factor.","Cluster headaches: more common in males.","First rule out intracranial/extracranial disease (e.g. head CT for a \"worst headache of my life\" presentation).","Treatment: NSAIDs/acetaminophen/aspirin (often disappointing alone) — stronger analgesics/opioids provide little added benefit and can worsen headaches. Triptans are migraine-specific (affect serotonin, cause vasoconstriction) — taken at th…"]},{"s":"Pain Basics: Physiology & Types▾","a":"pain-basics-physiology-types","k":["Pain Basics: Physiology & Types▾","Pain is subjective — \"whatever the experiencing person says it is, existing whenever and wherever the person says it does\" (McCaffery). There is no objective way to confirm or deny a patient's reported pain.","Nociception process: transduction (nociceptors convert a stimulus into an action potential) → transmission (the signal travels via nerve fibers to the spinal cord/brain) → perception (the brain/cerebral cortex interprets it as pain) → modul…","Acute pain: protective, short duration, limited tissue damage — but if untreated can threaten recovery. Shows classic sympathetic signs: ↑BP, ↑pulse, ↑respiratory rate, dilated pupils, diaphoresis.","Chronic pain: lasts >3–6 months, no longer protective, and physiological signs fade even though pain intensity is unchanged — making it easy for providers to underestimate. Linked to depression, insomnia, anorexia, hopelessness, and increas…","Cancer-related pain: can stem from the tumor itself or from treatment (chemo, radiation, associated infection risk).","Nociceptive pain: from pain receptors, responds well to opioids/analgesics, described as aching/gnawing/pounding — somatic (bones/joints/muscles/skin, well-localized) or visceral (internal organs, poorly localized, may be referred — e.g. pa…","Neuropathic pain: nerve injury/abnormal sensory processing (spinal cord injury, diabetic neuropathy, phantom limb) — described as burning/shooting/electrical. Treated with adjuvant analgesics like gabapentin (an anticonvulsant repurposed fo…","Idiopathic pain: chronic pain without a known cause, often exceeding what's typical for the underlying condition."]},{"s":"Pain Assessment▾","a":"pain-assessment","k":["Pain Assessment▾","Pain assessment combines subjective (what the patient reports) and objective data (heart rate, BP, respiratory rate, grimacing, guarding, moaning, restlessness). Absence of visible behavioral signs does not mean absence of pain — stoicism v…","PQRSTU: Provocative/palliative (what worsens/relieves it), Quality (descriptive words), Region/radiation, Severity (0–10 or an appropriate scale), Timing/treatment (onset, constancy, what's been tried), Understanding (what the pain prevents…","Pain scales: numeric (0–10, vertical or horizontal), visual analog, simple descriptive (mild → worst possible), word-bank scales for patients who struggle to describe pain, and the Wong-Baker FACES scale for patients 3 years and older or wi…","Set an achievable, realistic pain goal collaboratively (e.g. \"pain ≤3/10\") — complete elimination (0) often isn't achievable. Establish this before a painful event (e.g. pre-op), not during peak pain when the patient can't engage.","Assess → intervene → reassess → repeat. Factors that alter the pain experience: age, fatigue (heightens perception), genetics, cognitive/neurologic function (may need caregiver input), previous pain experience, support systems/coping mechan…"]},{"s":"Non-Pharmacological Pain Management▾","a":"non-pharmacological-pain-management","k":["Non-Pharmacological Pain Management▾","Used alone or alongside pharmacological treatment — for moderate-to-severe pain, use pharmacological measures first and add these; can improve physical functioning and quality of life.","Relaxation/guided imagery: alters cognitive/affective pain perception, can lower BP and heart rate.","Distraction: engages the reticular activating system to inhibit painful stimuli (TV, music, coloring, reading) — especially effective in children.","Music therapy: effective for acute and chronic pain, decreases sympathetic response and muscle tension; requires a provider order/consult in the hospital setting.","Cutaneous stimulation: massage, heat/cold, or a TENS unit (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation, requires an order). Heat is generally better for chronic pain; cold and TENS are generally better for acute pain. Always prioritize safe…","Always also address the underlying cause of the pain when possible, not just the symptom."]},{"s":"Pharmacological Pain Management▾","a":"pharmacological-pain-management","k":["Pharmacological Pain Management▾","General approach: start with the least intense medication/dose and escalate as needed; assess → medicate → reassess.","Non-opioids (mild-moderate pain): acetaminophen (Tylenol) — analgesic and antipyretic, no anti-inflammatory effect, 24-hour maximum of 4 grams (hepatotoxicity risk), IV form available/useful for NPO patients. NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen, as…","Opioids (moderate-severe pain): morphine, hydromorphone, fentanyl, oxycodone, hydrocodone, codeine — sometimes combined with acetaminophen (e.g. oxycodone/APAP). Ideally short-term. Side effects: constipation (consider a stool softener), GI…","Start low, go slow with opioid dosing — cancer patients may need much higher doses due to tolerance.","Adjuvant analgesics: medications that aren't primary pain drugs but enhance pain control — e.g. gabapentin (neuropathic pain), lidocaine patches. Anti-anxiety medications and sleep aids can help overall comfort but are not pain medications …","Respiratory depression is a serious opioid adverse effect — highest risk in opioid-naive patients and those also receiving benzodiazepines (cumulative effect). Know the patient's baseline respiratory (and neuro) status before administering.…","Around-the-clock (ATC) dosing: scheduled (not PRN) dosing that maintains constant therapeutic blood levels and avoids the peak-and-trough pattern of pure PRN dosing — often paired with PRN non-opioids/adjuvants for breakthrough pain.","Range order medications: a dose that varies across a prescribed range (e.g. morphine 2–6 mg IV q2h PRN) — start at the lower end for an opioid-naive patient, titrate based on ongoing assessment.","Tolerance: needing more drug for the same effect after repeated exposure — occurs with chronic opioid use (e.g. cancer patients), not a sign of addiction, and doesn't occur with short-term appropriate use.","Dependence: withdrawal symptoms (tachycardia, sweating, insomnia, agitation, diarrhea) if the drug is stopped abruptly after repeated exposure — also not a sign of addiction; requires gradual tapering.","Addiction: psychological dependence — using the drug for its mind-altering effects rather than pain relief. A distinct mental health/brain chemistry issue from tolerance/dependence — patients with addiction still deserve adequate pain treat…"]},{"s":"PCA & Epidural Analgesia▾","a":"pca-epidural-analgesia","k":["PCA & Epidural Analgesia▾","PCA (patient-controlled analgesia): IV opioid delivery programmed per provider order, often starting with a loading dose. The patient self-administers a bolus by pushing a button, limited by a lockout interval (e.g. every 10 minutes) and a …","Delivered via Y-tubing connected to a compatible IV fluid (typically 0.9% normal saline) to help push the small-volume medication doses along.","Critical safety/teaching point: only the patient may press the PCA button — family members pressing it \"to help\" is a leading cause of PCA-related overdose/respiratory depression. Patients on a PCA require a pulse oximeter and a standing Na…","Epidural analgesia: a form of regional anesthesia (must be preservative-free) that treats pain locally and reduces total systemic opioid need — used for abdominal or lower-body surgery/labor. Can run as a continuous infusion or PCA-style. S…","Epidural nursing care: monitor the insertion site (bleeding/redness/dislodgement), assess for urinary retention, treat as a fall risk especially with first ambulation (extra staff support), and monitor coagulation labs (platelets, INR, PTT)…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A patient with a history of chronic pancreatitis reports new-onset aching pain in the right shoulder with no history of trauma or shoulder injury. The nurse should recognize this most likely represents:","A Somatic pain, which is typically well-localized to the joint","B Cutaneous pain from irritation of the skin overlying the pancreas","C Neuropathic pain related to diabetic complications of pancreatitis","D Referred visceral pain, since organ pain may present in a distant area","A patient with moderate osteoarthritis pain has been taking the maximum recommended dose of ibuprofen but reports the pain relief has plateaued and the medication 'just doesn't work as well anymore.' The nurse understands this is best expla…","A Developing tolerance to the NSAID, requiring a dose increase","B Opioid-induced hyperalgesia from concurrent opioid use","C The ceiling effect of non-opioid analgesics such as ibuprofen","D Early dependence on the NSAID requiring a gradual taper","A postoperative patient is receiving scheduled acetaminophen 650 mg PO q4h and is also taking an OTC combination cold medication from home that contains acetaminophen. What is the nurse's priority action?","A Hold the scheduled acetaminophen dose and administer only the cold medication","B Encourage the patient to switch to ibuprofen for the remainder of the stay","C Calculate the patient's total daily acetaminophen intake from all sources","D Administer both medications as ordered since acetaminophen is well tolerated","A postoperative, opioid-naive patient receiving IV morphine plus a scheduled benzodiazepine for anxiety is found with a respiratory rate of 6 breaths/min and is difficult to arouse. After confirming the airway is open, which medication shou…","A Naloxone; one dose will fully reverse sedation for as long as the morphine lasts","B Flumazenil; it directly reverses the opioid-induced respiratory depression","C Additional morphine at a lower dose; this will slow the respiratory rate to normal","D Naloxone; repeat dosing and close monitoring will likely be needed afterward","A patient with a history of migraines calls the clinic reporting she has been taking her prescribed triptan every morning for the past two weeks 'just to prevent a migraine from starting.' What is the most appropriate response by the nurse?","A Triptans should be taken at the onset of migraine symptoms or aura, not used daily for prevention","B This is an appropriate use of the medication, since triptans work best when taken preventatively","C The patient should double the dose if taken preventatively to achieve an adequate serotonin effect","D Triptans are more effective for cluster headache prevention and should be switched to daily NSAID use instead","A nurse is teaching a patient with newly diagnosed chronic osteoarthritis pain about non-pharmacological options to use at home. Which teaching point is most appropriate?","A \"Ice packs will likely work best for your day-to-day chronic pain management.\"","B \"A TENS unit can be purchased over the counter and used without a provider order.\"","C \"Cutaneous stimulation should only be used if medications have completely failed.\"","D \"Heat therapy is better for chronic pain, while cold and TENS units help acute pain.\"","A 68-year-old admitted with a subdural hematoma is assessed hourly. On this check, the nurse notes the patient opens eyes only to pain, makes incomprehensible sounds, and shows abnormal flexion (decorticate posturing) to painful stimuli — a…","A Notify the provider and prepare to reassess in one hour per routine protocol","B Document the GCS score of 7 and continue hourly neuro checks as ordered","C Assess the patient's airway and prepare for advanced airway management","D Reorient the patient and increase environmental stimulation to raise the GCS","A patient is admitted following an acute ischemic stroke with new left-sided facial droop and slurred speech. The family requests permission to bring the patient's favorite home-cooked meal. What is the nurse's best response?","A \"That's fine, as long as you cut the food into small pieces to prevent choking.\"","B \"He can eat as soon as he tells us he feels ready to try a few bites.\"","C \"He needs to remain NPO until a formal swallow evaluation has cleared him.\"","D \"Solid foods are fine, but he should avoid drinking liquids until therapy clears him.\"","An older adult with presbycusis documented in the history reports new difficulty hearing the nurse during morning assessments. What should the nurse do first?","A Arrange for a hearing aid fitting evaluation","B Speak in a louder tone into the patient's better ear","C Refer the patient for a cochlear implant evaluation","D Check both of the patient's ears for cerumen impaction","A nurse is communicating with a patient recovering from a stroke who can follow multi-step commands appropriately and clearly understands conversation, but is unable to produce the words she wants to say. This is most consistent with:","A Receptive aphasia, and the nurse should use simple written material instead of speech","B Expressive aphasia, and the nurse should use simple short yes/no questions to ease communication","C Receptive aphasia, and the nurse should speak loudly and slowly to improve comprehension","D A hearing deficit rather than a language deficit, requiring an audiology referral","A nurse is caring for a patient receiving continuous epidural analgesia after abdominal surgery. Which finding requires the most immediate follow-up?","A The patient reports mild pruritus along the trunk","B The patient has not voided in six hours and reports lower abdominal fullness","C The patient's most recent platelet count and INR are both abnormal","D The patient rates pain as 4/10 at the epidural site during movement","A patient with chronic low back pain lasting 8 months rates pain as 8/10 but has a normal heart rate, blood pressure, and respiratory rate, with no diaphoresis or pupil changes. The nurse should recognize that:","A The patient is likely exaggerating the severity of the pain since vital signs are normal","B Normal vital signs reflect physiologic adaptation over time, not a true decrease in pain intensity","C The patient's pain has become acute in nature because vital signs have stabilized","D A pain reassessment is unnecessary since objective findings do not support the reported severity","Family brings in a patient with new-onset slurred speech, facial droop, and confusion that began 20 minutes ago. Stroke protocol is activated. Before the patient goes for CT imaging, which assessment is essential to obtain first?","A Fingerstick blood glucose level","B Pupillary response to light","C Glasgow Coma Scale documentation","D Full PQRSTU pain assessment","A patient presents to the ED with sudden severe headache described as 'the worst headache of my life,' along with new right-sided weakness. Which diagnostic test should the nurse anticipate being ordered first to rule out a bleed?","A Skull X-ray, because it can be done at the bedside without transport","B EEG, to assess for seizure activity contributing to the weakness","C MRI, because it provides the most detailed images without radiation","D CT scan, because it is the fast gold-standard test in an emergency","A nurse is developing a plan of care for a patient with significant residual deficits after a large stroke, including impaired mobility and decreased responsiveness. Which nursing actions are appropriate to include in the plan of care? Sele…","A Turn and reposition the patient at least every 2 hours, elevating the heels off the bed","B Implement seizure precautions, including padded side rails, working suction, and oxygen at the bedside","C Encourage getting the patient up to a chair when possible to help support strength and reduce DVT risk","D Limit family involvement in care planning to minimize overstimulation and confusion","E Protect uninterrupted sleep periods and try to maintain the patient's normal sleep-wake cycle","F Turn the patient every 4 hours instead of every 2 hours to avoid unnecessarily disrupting rest","The spouse of a postoperative patient tells the nurse, 'My husband is in pain but keeps falling asleep before he can press the PCA button, so I've been pressing it for him when I see he's uncomfortable.' What is the most important teaching …","A This is acceptable as long as the spouse presses the button only when the patient appears to be in pain","B Only the patient may press the PCA button, since patient self-administration is the key safety feature","C The spouse should instead notify the nurse each time the patient needs a dose so the nurse can press the button","D The lockout interval prevents overdose regardless of who presses the button, so this practice poses minimal risk","A nurse caring for a patient with a known traumatic brain injury notes the left pupil is now 6 mm and sluggish while the right pupil remains 3 mm and brisk — a change from the prior assessment. What is the nurse's priority action?","A Notify the provider immediately, as this represents a neurologic emergency","B Document the finding and reassess pupils again at the next scheduled check","C Dim the room lights and allow the patient to rest before reassessing","D Recheck the pupils in 15 minutes to confirm the finding before notifying anyone","A nurse is caring for several patients on long-term opioid therapy for chronic cancer pain. Which statements accurately reflect the concepts of tolerance, dependence, and addiction as they apply to opioid therapy? Select all that apply.","A A patient who now requires a higher morphine dose than six months ago to achieve the same pain relief is demonstrating tolerance, not addiction","B A patient who develops tachycardia, sweating, and agitation after an opioid dose is abruptly stopped is showing signs of dependence and needs a gradual taper, not evidence of addiction","C Any patient requesting pain medication before the next scheduled dose is exhibiting drug-seeking behavior consistent with addiction","D Addiction is defined by using the medication for its mind-altering psychological effects rather than for pain relief","E Tolerance and dependence are both expected physiologic responses to chronic opioid exposure and do not by themselves indicate addiction","F A cancer patient requiring unusually high opioid doses due to tolerance should be treated the same as a patient exhibiting true addictive behavior, with equally cautious dose limitations","A patient in the ICU for three days becomes increasingly restless and irritable, reports racing thoughts, and shows new difficulty concentrating on simple questions. The nurse's first consideration should be:","A Early-onset dementia, warranting a formal cognitive evaluation","B Sensory overload from the excessive stimuli of the ICU environment","C Opioid-induced sedation, warranting holding the next scheduled dose","D A primary psychiatric mood disorder requiring psychiatric consultation","A patient with chronic pain from a spinal cord injury is scheduled for an MRI. During the pre-procedure interview, which finding should prompt the nurse to notify the provider before the patient goes to imaging?","A The patient reports mild claustrophobia and requests a blanket","B The patient has an old surgical clip and is unsure what it is made of","C The patient has a documented latex allergy noted in the chart","D The patient took their oral pain medication four hours before the scan"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-older-adults-discharge.html","t":"Older Adults & Discharge Planning","secs":[{"s":"Older Adults & Discharge Planning","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 10","Aging Myths, Ageism & Body System Changes","Functional & Cognitive Changes","Care Settings & Acute Care Basics","Acute Care Complications in Older Adults","Leading Health Concerns & Nutrition","Falls: Risk Factors & Prevention","Polypharmacy, Assessment & Atypical Illness Presentation","Psychosocial Health: Elder Mistreatment, Communication & Teaching","Discharge Planning Basics","Critical Aspects of Discharge Teaching","Coordinating Discharge: Case Management, Social Services & Follow-Up","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","Combines two lecture sets: care of the older adult (aging myths, physiological/functional/cognitive changes, care settings, acute-care complications, falls, polypharmacy, atypical illness presentation, elder mistreatment) and discharge plan…"]},{"s":"Aging Myths, Ageism & Body System Changes▾","a":"aging-myths-ageism-body-system-changes","k":["Aging Myths, Ageism & Body System Changes▾","\"Old age\" is often defined starting at 65; the 65+ population is projected to roughly double by 2060. Chronological age says very little about an individual — avoid myths/stereotypes (assuming older adults are ill, forgetful, rigid, uninter…","Ageism = prejudice/discrimination based on age — can undermine an older patient's self-confidence and limit their access to care. Recognize it and don't buy into it.","Cardiovascular: decreased myocardial contractility (↓ cardiac output), cardiac hypertrophy, stiffer heart valves, lower resting heart rate with a blunted exercise response, and reduced perfusion to the extremities (cold hands/feet). Hyperte…","Respiratory: decreased respiratory muscle strength and lung expansion → a weaker cough (higher pneumonia/infection risk), increased AP diameter, and fewer alveoli/cilia. Kyphosis (upper spine curvature, from osteoporosis/disc degeneration/c…","GI: increased trunk/abdominal fat, slower peristalsis, decreased saliva, faster gastric emptying → reduced hunger and food tolerance.","Urinary: prostate hypertrophy in men (urinary retention, frequency, incontinence, UTI risk); decreased bladder capacity and pelvic floor muscle tone (stress incontinence, more common in women but possible in men too).","Integumentary: decreased turgor and subQ tissue (feel cold more easily), decreased skin resilience/moisture, thinning/graying hair.","Musculoskeletal: muscle strength declines with muscle mass loss (regular exercise slows this); osteoporosis is more common in postmenopausal women but also affects men, especially with poor nutrition/mobility.","Immune: slower immune response and wound healing, reduced antibody production, decreased response to immunizations."]},{"s":"Functional & Cognitive Changes▾","a":"functional-cognitive-changes","k":["Functional & Cognitive Changes▾","Functional status = capacity to safely perform ADLs (activities of daily living) and IADLs (instrumental ADLs — transportation, meal prep, shopping). ADL performance is a sensitive indicator of overall health; PT/OT are often part of a comp…","Cognitive changes: significant memory loss, confusion, or disorientation are NOT a normal part of aging and always warrant further assessment — a common misconception is that they are. Subtle cognitive changes (from reduced brain cells/alte…","Psychosocial changes: retirement, social isolation, changes in sexuality, housing/environment changes (having to leave a home they've long managed independently), and loss/grief from the death of loved ones."]},{"s":"Care Settings & Acute Care Basics▾","a":"care-settings-acute-care-basics","k":["Care Settings & Acute Care Basics▾","Older adults use a range of settings: community/home-based care, acute care (hospital), restorative care, and long-term/institutional care. Nurses help patients and families make placement decisions — encourage collaborative family involvem…","What to look for in a facility: private rooms if desired, Medicare/Medicaid certification, adequate/background-checked staffing, quality of food and meal options, recreational activities, and family-involvement policies.","In acute care, prioritize comfort, safety, nutrition, hydration, and skin integrity — identify and treat the underlying cause of any problem, promote independence and dignity, and include the patient in their own care."]},{"s":"Acute Care Complications in Older Adults▾","a":"acute-care-complications-in-older-adults","k":["Acute Care Complications in Older Adults▾","Delirium: risk increases with an unfamiliar environment, sleep deprivation, sensory impairment, new medications, anesthesia, hypoxia, and infection. Nursing care: encourage family visits, provide memory cues, reorient frequently, compensate…","Dehydration/malnutrition: watch for medications that suppress appetite and physical inability to reach/eat food — these seemingly minor issues can become life-threatening.","Hospital-acquired infections and urinary incontinence: incontinence causes include medications, restricted mobility, inability to reach the bathroom in time, confusion, or an untreated UTI.","Falls: both intrinsic and extrinsic risk factors are elevated in hospitalized older adults (see the dedicated falls section below).","Restorative care (home or long-term care setting) focuses on continued recovery from acute illness or support for chronic conditions, aiming to regain/improve ADLs and IADLs. Remember: aging alone does not inevitably cause disability or dep…"]},{"s":"Leading Health Concerns & Nutrition▾","a":"leading-health-concerns-nutrition","k":["Leading Health Concerns & Nutrition▾","Leading causes of death in older adults: heart disease (#1), cancer (#2), chronic lung disease (#3, strongly tied to smoking), stroke (#4). Smoking is a shared risk factor for all four — cessation counseling is always worthwhile. Alcohol ab…","Nutrition: encourage calorie-dense, high-nutrient foods; home-delivered meals are valuable for those living alone. Assess dental health/denture fit. Factors affecting nutrition: lifelong habits, cultural/religious preferences, situational f…","Improving intake in care settings: eat with others, make food accessible, ensure the patient is comfortable/positioned well before the tray arrives, provide mouth care/handwashing before meals, eliminate unpleasant odors, involve a dietitia…","Pain is not a normal part of aging — it's a symptom signaling something is wrong and requires good assessment skills, especially since older adults often underreport it.","Medication use/polypharmacy is one of the greatest challenges for older adults (see below)."]},{"s":"Falls: Risk Factors & Prevention▾","a":"falls-risk-factors-prevention","k":["Falls: Risk Factors & Prevention▾","Falls are the leading cause of fatal injury in older adults and can be a life-changing event (e.g. hip fracture).","Intrinsic risk factors (inside the body): medication side effects, chronic conditions, mobility issues (arthritis), peripheral neuropathy (diabetes), gait instability (Parkinson's disease).","Extrinsic risk factors (environment): poor lighting, missing handrails/grab bars, poorly designed stairwells, slippery/uneven surfaces, not using assistive devices correctly, improper footwear.","Prevention: medication review, toileting plan, adequate lighting, appropriate footwear, call bell within reach, reduce clutter, ensure alarms are set as needed, answer call lights promptly, frequent rounding, and reorient confused patients …"]},{"s":"Polypharmacy, Assessment & Atypical Illness Presentation▾","a":"polypharmacy-assessment-atypical-illness-presentation","k":["Polypharmacy, Assessment & Atypical Illness Presentation▾","In an older adult, new-onset confusion is often the first (or only) sign of infection or acute illness — a UTI, pneumonia, or even an MI may present primarily as confusion rather than the classic textbook symptoms (fever, bladder pain, ches…","Polypharmacy = concurrent use of multiple medications, affecting about 40% of older adults — increases drug-drug interaction risk. Best practice: one provider coordinates/manages all medications; communicate every medication to every specia…","Assessment considerations: allow extra time and rest periods, account for vision/hearing deficits, and recognize early indicators of acute illness — new mental status change, new falls, appetite loss, dizziness, new incontinence.","Atypical presentation by setting: ambulatory patients may report only fatigue or decreased activity tolerance without classic acute symptoms — ask specifically about bowel habits, confusion, and increasing dyspnea (a cardiac history + new d…","Watch for drug toxicity from polypharmacy combined with age-related decline in kidney/liver function."]},{"s":"Psychosocial Health: Elder Mistreatment, Communication & Teaching▾","a":"psychosocial-health-elder-mistreatment-communication-teaching","k":["Psychosocial Health: Elder Mistreatment, Communication & Teaching▾","Elder mistreatment = an intentional act or failure to act that causes or risks harm to an older patient — includes physical, emotional, and sexual abuse, financial exploitation, and neglect/abandonment. If suspected, interview/assess the pa…","Reality orientation: frequently reinforces correct person/place/time (clocks, calendars, whiteboards) for a confused patient.","Validation therapy: an alternative approach that does not insist the confused patient agree with objective reality, but instead reflects sensitivity to the meaning behind their statements/behaviors — used when reality orientation isn't appr…","Teaching older adults: assess readiness to learn first (not if anxious/in pain), speak clearly/slowly in a normal tone, present one idea at a time, minimize distractions, and use the teach-back method to confirm understanding. For hearing d…"]},{"s":"Discharge Planning Basics▾","a":"discharge-planning-basics","k":["Discharge Planning Basics▾","Discharge planning determines what level of care a patient needs next (home, home health, rehab, etc.). The provider authorizes discharge, but nurses play a huge advocacy role — speak up if bedside assessment suggests a patient isn't ready,…","Why it matters: good discharge planning improves health outcomes and reduces readmissions (hospitals aren't fully reimbursed for same-diagnosis readmissions within a defined window). Poor planning risks medication mismanagement, health decl…","Key considerations: adequate time to teach and get a return demonstration (never rush this), how much home assistance is needed, the patient's learning style (verbal/written/hands-on — always send home written instructions too), and ensurin…","What makes discharge planning difficult: time pressure to free up beds, family hesitancy/discomfort taking over care tasks (e.g. wound packing) — listen to these concerns; and social issues like transportation delays.","The care team decides discharge readiness together, but the nurse can and should appeal/request reevaluation if the patient or caregiver isn't truly ready.","Post-discharge sites of care: home, home health (e.g. IV antibiotics, wound care), skilled nursing facility (based on ADL independence level), inpatient vs. outpatient rehab (condition-specific units, e.g. spinal cord injury or stroke), lon…"]},{"s":"Critical Aspects of Discharge Teaching▾","a":"critical-aspects-of-discharge-teaching","k":["Critical Aspects of Discharge Teaching▾","Medication reconciliation: review home meds vs. new hospital meds, teach the purpose/administration/side effects of anything new, coordinate with pharmacy on drug interactions and which old medications to stop/continue, and address access (…","Diagnosis & when to seek help: ensure understanding of the diagnosis and treatment given, and be specific about exactly when to call the provider vs. go to the ED immediately (e.g. difficulty breathing, chest pain, BE-FAST stroke signs).","Procedures/surgeries: brief description of what was done, home/follow-up care, provider-specific instructions for showering/dressing changes, and clear guidance on which wound changes are normal healing vs. an ED-worthy concern (e.g. minor …","Diet: clarify if the discharge diet differs from their usual one, give specific food guidance, involve a dietitian for complex needs (e.g. newly diagnosed diabetes, texture-modified diets) and caregiver teaching for any special preparation …","Activity: weight-bearing status, physical therapy follow-up frequency, lifting restrictions (a practical reference: a gallon of milk is about 9 lbs), and any durable medical equipment (DME — walkers, crutches) with PT/OT involvement; assess…"]},{"s":"Coordinating Discharge: Case Management, Social Services & Follow-Up▾","a":"coordinating-discharge-case-management-social-services-follow-up","k":["Coordinating Discharge: Case Management, Social Services & Follow-Up▾","Case management/social services coordinate durable medical equipment, home health referrals (wound care, ADL assistance, PT/OT/speech), and financial assistance for medications. They also help assess practical home-life needs (running water…","Follow-up appointments: generally schedule primary care follow-up within a week, plus any relevant specialist follow-up (e.g. a cardiac surgeon after heart surgery). Consider access barriers (distance, satellite clinics, telehealth options)…","Discharge planning is highly interdisciplinary (social work, case management, dietary, pharmacy, PT/OT, speech-language pathology) — the nurse isn't responsible for doing everything personally, but is very often the one coordinating across …"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","During a home visit, a nurse notes that an older adult who previously managed her own grocery shopping and medication administration independently now needs assistance with both tasks, though she denies any new symptoms. What should the nur…","A It is an expected consequence of normal aging and needs no further investigation.","B It reflects an IADL decline, but only ADL decline is clinically significant.","C It likely signals an underlying problem and should be reported for evaluation.","D It should be addressed only if her basic ADLs become affected as well.","A 70-year-old patient tells the nurse that her physician's office repeatedly schedules her for shorter appointment slots than younger patients 'because there's not much more that can be done for someone my age,' despite her having several t…","A Ageism — prejudice or discrimination based on the patient's age.","B Elder mistreatment — neglect of the patient by a healthcare provider.","C A normal, expected reduction in the need for preventive care with age.","D An appropriate triage decision based on the patient's overall prognosis.","A 79-year-old man is postoperative day 1 following abdominal surgery in an unfamiliar hospital room. He received general anesthesia and is on a new opioid regimen. Overnight, he becomes acutely disoriented and agitated. Which nursing interv…","A Keep the room dim and minimize interaction with him to promote rest.","B Restrict family visits at the bedside to reduce overstimulation.","C Administer a sedative promptly to control the agitation before assessing him.","D Reorient him frequently and encourage family presence at the bedside.","An older adult with dementia repeatedly states that she needs to leave to pick up her children from elementary school, though her children are now grown adults. Reminding her of the correct date and her children's ages causes visible distre…","A Firmly correct her with the current date and remind her that her children are adults until she accepts it.","B Use a whiteboard and clock in her room to reinforce person, place, and time at each interaction.","C Acknowledge the feelings behind her statement rather than insisting that she accept objective reality.","D Avoid the topic entirely and change the subject whenever she brings up her children.","A patient is scheduled for discharge home after a total knee replacement. The provider has written discharge orders, but the bedside nurse notes the patient is unable to safely transfer from bed to chair without maximal assistance and becom…","A Proceed with discharge as ordered, since the provider has already authorized the patient to leave.","B Discharge the patient but arrange for home health to visit within 24 hours to reassess safety.","C Ask the family to stay with the patient for the first few days instead of delaying discharge.","D Communicate these findings to the care team and request reevaluation of discharge readiness.","During a health assessment, a nursing student states, 'Her blood pressure is elevated, but that's expected since she's 84 years old.' Which response by the supervising nurse is most accurate?","A 'You're right — hypertension is an expected, unavoidable consequence of aging, so no action is needed.'","B 'Hypertension is common in older adults, but it is not an expected part of aging and still needs treatment.'","C 'Elevated blood pressure in older adults is usually white coat syndrome and can be disregarded.'","D 'Since decreased cardiac output is a normal aging change, elevated blood pressure needs no treatment.'","A nurse is caring for a hospitalized older adult who has been eating poorly since admission. Which of the following actions are appropriate to help improve the patient's nutritional intake? Select all that apply.","A Arrange for the patient to eat meals with other patients in a common area when possible","B Provide oral care and handwashing before the meal tray arrives","C Restrict the patient's fluid intake to reduce the risk of incontinence during meals","D Eliminate unpleasant odors from the room before mealtime","E Encourage the patient to remain in bed and avoid activity to conserve energy for eating","F Involve a dietitian in the plan of care","A nurse is providing health teaching to an older adult who continues to smoke. Which statement by the nurse best reflects accurate knowledge of leading health risks in older adults?","A 'Smoking cessation is important only for preventing lung disease, not for heart disease or cancer.'","B 'Heart disease is the leading cause of death in older adults, and quitting smoking is still worthwhile at your age.'","C 'Since stroke is the leading cause of death in older adults, quitting smoking mainly reduces stroke risk.'","D 'Cancer is the leading cause of death in older adults, and cessation counseling won't help at your age.'","A nurse is finalizing discharge instructions for a patient recovering from pneumonia with no other complications. When should the nurse advise the patient to schedule a follow-up appointment with their primary care provider?","A Within about one week of discharge from the hospital.","B Within 24 hours of discharge, regardless of condition.","C Only if new symptoms develop, not routinely.","D Within 4 to 6 weeks, to allow full recovery first.","A 75-year-old patient with osteoarthritis tells the nurse, 'I've just been dealing with the pain in my knees for years, it's part of getting old.' Which response best reflects accurate nursing knowledge?","A 'Chronic pain in older adults is expected and typically resolves on its own without treatment.'","B 'That's true, most older adults live with chronic pain, so as long as it's tolerable, we don't need to intervene.'","C 'Since pain tolerance decreases with age, your pain is likely being over-reported and doesn't need further evaluation.'","D 'Pain is a symptom that something is wrong, not a normal part of aging, and it's important that we assess and manage it properly.'","A patient is being discharged after hip surgery with a partial weight-bearing restriction on the affected leg and a walker for ambulation. Which instruction should the nurse include in discharge teaching about activity?","A 'Follow your prescribed weight-bearing status and avoid lifting more than about 9 pounds until cleared.'","B 'You can resume lifting objects heavier than a gallon of milk, about 9 pounds, whenever you feel ready.'","C 'Since you have been given a walker, you no longer need to follow your weight-bearing restrictions.'","D 'Weight-bearing and lifting limits are the same for every hip surgery patient, so yours are not specific.'","An 86-year-old man with Parkinson's disease and peripheral neuropathy from long-standing diabetes is admitted to a unit with dim hallway lighting and no grab bars in the bathroom. The nurse is planning fall prevention interventions. Which o…","A His gait instability from his chronic conditions.","B The dim lighting in the hallway outside his room.","C The absence of grab bars in the bathroom he uses.","D The slippery flooring in front of the shower.","An 82-year-old woman with well-controlled type 2 diabetes is brought to the emergency department by her daughter, who reports, 'She just seems confused today, that's not like her.' Vital signs: T 37.9°C (100.2°F) oral, HR 96, BP 128/78, RR …","A The patient is experiencing normal age-related cognitive decline and should be reassessed at her next primary care visit.","B New-onset confusion in an older adult often represents the first or only sign of an underlying acute illness such as infection.","C Because she denies dysuria and chest pain, a urinary tract infection and cardiac event can be ruled out without further testing.","D Confusion in this context is most consistent with early-stage dementia and warrants an outpatient neurology referral.","A family is trying to choose a long-term care facility for their father and asks the nurse what to consider. Which response is most appropriate?","A 'Since all certified facilities meet the same minimum standards, there's no need to compare them.'","B 'The most important factor is choosing the facility closest to your home, regardless of anything else.'","C 'Private rooms are required by law in every facility, so that shouldn't factor into your decision.'","D 'Compare certification, staffing, food, and activities using Medicare's Care Compare website.'","A nurse at a hospital affiliated with UK's 'meds to beds' program is preparing discharge medication teaching for a patient. The patient asks how much medication supply the program typically sends home with patients. What is the most accurat…","A 'The program sends home about a 7-day supply, just until your follow-up visit.'","B 'The program sends home a 90-day supply, so you won't need a refill for months.'","C 'The program sends home enough medication to last about 30 days after discharge.'","D 'The program provides only a starter dose of one to two days' worth of medication.'","A home health nurse visits an 80-year-old widow who lives with her adult son, who manages her finances. The nurse notices unexplained bruising on the patient's upper arms in a pattern suggestive of grip marks, and the son answers all questi…","A Document the bruising as noted and continue the visit as scheduled, since the son is present as a support person.","B Confront the son directly about the bruising in front of the patient to observe his reaction.","C Find a way to interview and assess the patient privately, away from the son, before deciding on next steps.","D Wait until the next scheduled visit to reassess whether the bruising has resolved before taking action.","A nurse is assessing an 83-year-old woman with a pronounced upper spine curvature related to osteoporosis. The nurse understands that this finding, combined with normal age-related respiratory changes, places the patient at increased risk f…","A Increased alveolar surface area leading to hyperoxygenation.","B Reduced rib mobility and weaker cough, increasing pneumonia risk.","C Increased respiratory muscle strength compensating for the curvature.","D Decreased anteroposterior chest diameter improving lung expansion.","A 78-year-old man is admitted for a scheduled hip replacement. Medication reconciliation reveals he takes 11 prescription medications from three different providers, plus two over-the-counter supplements. The nurse identifies this as polyph…","A About 40% of older adults; one provider should coordinate and manage all of the patient's medications, with every prescriber informed of the full list.","B About 25% of older adults; each specialist should independently manage the medications they prescribe without coordinating with the others.","C About 60% of older adults; medication reconciliation should be performed only if the patient reports a new symptom.","D About 40% of older adults; the pharmacy alone is responsible for reconciling medications, and providers do not need to be informed of OTC supplements.","A nurse is teaching a group of family caregivers about normal versus abnormal aging changes. Which statement by a caregiver indicates a need for further teaching?","A 'Significant memory loss and disorientation are not normal aging changes and should always be evaluated further.'","B 'Some subtle changes in memory can occur with normal aging due to changes in brain cells and neurotransmitters.'","C 'Since my father is 79, it's expected that he'll become increasingly confused and forgetful — there's nothing to worry about.'","D 'If my mother suddenly becomes confused, I should let her care team know rather than assuming it's just her age.'","A nurse is caring for several older adult patients on a medical-surgical unit. Which of the following findings should prompt the nurse to suspect early acute illness and warrant further assessment? Select all that apply.","A A patient who has become newly incontinent of urine over the past two days","B A patient who reports decreased appetite that started this week","C A patient who has always required a cane for ambulation and continues to use it safely","D A patient who is newly dizzy when standing up","E A patient who has a stable, longstanding mild hearing deficit","F A patient who fell for the first time last night after being steady on their feet for years"]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-palliative-hospice-eol.html","t":"Palliative, Hospice & End of Life","secs":[{"s":"Palliative, Hospice & End of Life","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review · Topic 11","Palliative vs. Hospice: Key Distinctions","Eligibility, Payment & the \"Live Discharge\"","The Interdisciplinary Team","Why Communication Is Hard & the Nurse's Central Role","Responding to Emotions: NURSE Statements","\"I Wish\"/\"I Worry\" Statements & the Power of Silence","Advance Care Planning & Legal Documents","Ethical Issues in Serious Illness & End-of-Life Care","Factors Influencing EOL Care & Medical Aid in Dying","The Dying Experience & Syndrome of Imminent Dying","Family Concerns, Morphine, Grief & Bereavement","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions","From the guest lecture on palliative, hospice, and end-of-life care (Dr. Abigail Latimer, clinical social worker). Covers palliative vs. hospice care, the interdisciplinary team, serious-illness communication skills, advance care planning a…"]},{"s":"Palliative vs. Hospice: Key Distinctions▾","a":"palliative-vs-hospice-key-distinctions","k":["Palliative vs. Hospice: Key Distinctions▾","Most people assume palliative and hospice are the same thing or are always delivered together — they are related but distinct, and knowing the difference matters for coordinating care and talking with other providers.","Palliative care is an extra layer of support for anyone with a life-threatening illness, regardless of life expectancy — someone can receive palliative care at time of diagnosis, all the way through curative treatment, even if they're expec…","Hospice care requires an expected life expectancy of 6 months or less — a physician has to certify this prognosis for the patient to be eligible.","Historically, palliative and hospice were offered together only at the very end, once \"nothing else could be done.\" Care has since moved upstream: palliative care is now ideally involved from time of diagnosis as a layer of support alongsid…","Both are delivered by an interdisciplinary team and share the same underlying goal: comfort care and reducing suffering, physically and psychosocially, in the context of serious illness."]},{"s":"Eligibility, Payment & the \"Live Discharge\"▾","a":"eligibility-payment-the-live-discharge","k":["Eligibility, Payment & the \"Live Discharge\"▾","Can curative treatments continue? Generally yes with palliative care, generally no with hospice — but there's an important exception. Eligibility and continued treatment decisions are also shaped by payer rules, not just philosophy of care.","Worked example — dialysis on hospice: a patient admitted to hospice for a cancer diagnosis cannot also continue chemotherapy for that cancer. But if the same patient also has kidney failure that is not the diagnosis driving their hospice en…","Payment: hospice is paid for by Medicare, Medicaid, and most private insurance; palliative care payment depends more on the patient's insurance/coverage. This payer distinction is part of why continuing curative treatment alongside hospice …","How long can hospice services continue? For as long as the physician keeps certifying that the patient still wants comfort-focused care and still has a ≤6-month prognosis. Hospice patients don't automatically get discharged the day they hit…","\"Live discharge\": when a patient's condition stabilizes or improves enough that they no longer meet hospice's 6-month prognosis, they exit hospice care. This isn't rare — holistic symptom management in hospice can sometimes reduce the physi…"]},{"s":"The Interdisciplinary Team▾","a":"the-interdisciplinary-team","k":["The Interdisciplinary Team▾","In hospice care, the nurse drives care coordination — nurses and nurse aides spend the most time with the patient and work fairly autonomously to deliver services. The physician is also involved (recertifying eligibility, managing complex n…","In hospital-based palliative care, the physician has more of a primary role, and the nurse has somewhat less of a leadership role on paper — though the bedside nurse is still functionally part of the interdisciplinary team, even without a f…","Social workers on both teams work at the top of their licensure — not just case management, but clinical care: psychological assessment and intervention as part of the whole-person, holistic approach that ties physical, psychological, psych…"]},{"s":"Why Communication Is Hard & the Nurse's Central Role▾","a":"why-communication-is-hard-the-nurse-s-central-role","k":["Why Communication Is Hard & the Nurse's Central Role▾","Communication is the fulcrum of palliative/hospice care. Agreeing to hospice implies accepting a terminal illness and choosing comfort-focused care over curative treatment — if that acceptance isn't there yet, the patient may not be ready f…","Despite how essential it is, most nurses feel ill-equipped for these conversations. Formal communication training is limited in nursing education, and nurses often feel confident with physical care but uncertain and anxious about addressing…","The nurse has three distinct communication opportunities: the family meeting (ensuring key topics are covered and the family understands the information), the nurse–family conversation at the bedside (eliciting goals/needs and understanding…","Common barriers to having these conversations: fear of provoking emotional distress, not knowing how to handle a patient's emotional outburst, not knowing the \"right\" words, feeling responsible if the conversation shapes the plan of care in…"]},{"s":"Responding to Emotions: NURSE Statements▾","a":"responding-to-emotions-nurse-statements","k":["Responding to Emotions: NURSE Statements▾","Patients can't move forward with treatment decisions if their emotions aren't addressed first. The goal of an accepting response is to accept what the patient says non-judgmentally, acknowledge they're entitled to their own views/feelings, …","When we respond to emotion, we are not trying to fix the emotion. Avoid reflexively saying \"don't be sad\" or reading a patient's continued hope as \"denial\" — patients naturally oscillate between accepting a serious diagnosis (\"I'm really si…","NURSE: Name the emotion (\"I can see where this might be scary\") — this is a hypothesis, and it's fine if the patient corrects it. Understand (\"I can't imagine what this must be like\") — never say \"I totally understand,\" even if you've had a…"]},{"s":"\"I Wish\"/\"I Worry\" Statements & the Power of Silence▾","a":"i-wish-i-worry-statements-the-power-of-silence","k":["\"I Wish\"/\"I Worry\" Statements & the Power of Silence▾","\"I wish\" statements demonstrate alignment with the patient and implicitly acknowledge that what they want isn't going to happen, without directly correcting or contradicting them. Example: instead of correcting a patient who says \"I don't w…","\"I worry\" statements share the possibility of a bad outcome, often used when delivering difficult news — e.g., \"I worry that the chemotherapy treatment might make you really sick,\" or \"I worry that without your care partner present, this co…","Silence is an empathic response in its own right. It can feel uncomfortable or awkward for the nurse, but it shows support, gives patients and families room to talk (and they often will fill that space with information you wouldn't otherwis…"]},{"s":"Advance Care Planning & Legal Documents▾","a":"advance-care-planning-legal-documents","k":["Advance Care Planning & Legal Documents▾","Advance care planning (ACP) is the process of eliciting a patient's goals, preferences, and values — through conversation and/or written documentation — for future medical decisions. Despite its value, ACP conversations happen far less ofte…","Early goals-of-care conversation: held when the relevant clinical decision is not imminent (e.g., discussing future feeding-tube possibilities early in a cancer treatment course). Late goals-of-care conversation: held when the clinical deci…","Living will (a \"health care directive,\" not the same as a last will and testament): a written document describing a patient's wishes about medical treatment if they become unable to communicate — includes naming a health care surrogate and …","Last will and testament: the legal document that determines where a person's property/assets go after death — unrelated to medical treatment decisions.","MOST form (Medical Orders for Scope of Treatment): a portable physician order directing medical treatment — only appropriate for patients with serious or advanced chronic illness, not for the general/healthy population. A living will is the…","Kentucky's two legal ACP documents: the Kentucky Living Will (the state's official form; no attorney required, but it needs either 2 qualifying witnesses or notarization — relatives, heirs, and the patient's own healthcare provider cannot s…"]},{"s":"Ethical Issues in Serious Illness & End-of-Life Care▾","a":"ethical-issues-in-serious-illness-end-of-life-care","k":["Ethical Issues in Serious Illness & End-of-Life Care▾","Informed consent: making sure the patient truly understands the treatment they are agreeing to (or declining) — including understanding and agreeing with hospice philosophy itself before being enrolled.","Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment: raises difficult \"when/how\" questions, and families are rarely ever fully \"ready.\"","DNR/code status conversations are a common source of ethical concern. Providers sometimes use coercive, overly graphic language when discussing code status, effectively intimidating or manipulating a patient away from choosing CPR/intubatio…","Artificial hydration/nutrition: the term \"feeding tube\" is common but somewhat manipulative language, because \"feeding\" implies the emotional act of caring for someone. The more technical terms — artificial hydration, artificial nutrition —…"]},{"s":"Factors Influencing EOL Care & Medical Aid in Dying▾","a":"factors-influencing-eol-care-medical-aid-in-dying","k":["Factors Influencing EOL Care & Medical Aid in Dying▾","End-of-life care decisions are shaped by more than just medical facts. Key influences include ethnicity and intracultural differences (variation even within one culture, e.g. modern vs. traditional expression of beliefs), faith/cultural tra…","Medical Aid in Dying (MAID), formerly called physician-assisted suicide: a competent, terminally ill patient is prescribed a lethal dose of medication, at their own request after counseling, which they themselves choose to take to end their…","MAID is NOT: euthanasia (which involves someone else administering the means), pain medication that may incidentally hasten death, or palliative sedation. Each state that permits MAID has its own specific requirements — for example, the pat…"]},{"s":"The Dying Experience & Syndrome of Imminent Dying▾","a":"the-dying-experience-syndrome-of-imminent-dying","k":["The Dying Experience & Syndrome of Imminent Dying▾","The broader dying experience can last weeks to a year (source: Wisconsin Fast Facts). Common features as death approaches: social withdrawal (patients naturally put distance between themselves and loved ones — distinguish this from depressi…","The syndrome of imminent dying describes the final hours-to-weeks in three phases. Early: bed-bound, losing interest/ability to eat or drink, increased sleeping and delirium (day/night reversal). Middle: further decline to obtundation — slo…"]},{"s":"Family Concerns, Morphine, Grief & Bereavement▾","a":"family-concerns-morphine-grief-bereavement","k":["Family Concerns, Morphine, Grief & Bereavement▾","Family concerns during the dying process: families commonly ask \"are we starving them?\", \"what should we expect?\", \"can they hear what I'm saying?\" Give families concrete information (using everything above) and involve them directly in car…","Morphine does NOT hasten death when properly dosed. This is a persistent myth (\"we gave her morphine and then she died\"). There is no difference in survival with opioid dose or dose changes when accepted dosing guidelines are followed; morp…","Grief and bereavement: Kübler-Ross's stages of grief made the topic of dying and grief more discussable, but the \"stages\" framing has become distorted — grief is fluid, not a strict sequence, and stages are often re-experienced within minut…","Anticipatory grief: normal, natural grief that occurs before a death, mirroring post-death mourning. It includes grief over the anticipated loss of the person as well as anticipated loss of intangible things — future plans, dreams, mileston…","Bereavement refers specifically to the post-mortem grief response, distinct from the anticipatory grief that can occur before the death.","Dual process model of grief (a more accurate alternative to strict \"stages\"): people oscillate between loss-oriented tasks (grief work, feeling intruded upon by grief, breaking bonds/ties) and restoration-oriented tasks (attending to life c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","All 20 Must Know + Extra Practice questions for this section, pulled from the same bank as Build Your Own Exam. Answer each, then submit to see your score and rationales.","A nurse is helping a patient with advanced COPD complete advance care planning documents. The patient asks the nurse to explain the difference between the paperwork options. Which explanation is accurate?","A \"A living will is the same as a last will and testament — both determine how your property is distributed after death.\"","B \"A MOST form is appropriate for any adult, healthy or not, who wants to document their future medical wishes.\"","C \"A last will and testament describes your wishes for medical treatment if you become unable to communicate.\"","D \"A living will records your treatment wishes and names a surrogate; a MOST form is a portable physician order for serious illness.\"","A patient in Kentucky is completing a Kentucky Living Will and asks the nurse who can serve as one of the two required witnesses. Which response is accurate?","A \"Any two adults can serve as witnesses, including children who would inherit from you.\"","B \"Only a licensed attorney is permitted to witness a Kentucky Living Will document.\"","C \"You need either two qualifying witnesses or notarization, and relatives or heirs can't serve.\"","D \"Your own primary care provider is required to serve as one of the two witnesses.\"","A family member tells the nurse, \"We can't let them put in a feeding tube — that feels like we'd be letting her starve if we don't.\" What is the nurse's best response?","A Use the term \"artificial nutrition\" rather than \"feeding tube,\" and explain that decreased interest in food is a natural part of dying.","B Agree that withholding a feeding tube is the same as letting her starve, and encourage the family to consent to insertion.","C Avoid discussing the topic further, since families are not entitled to information about artificial nutrition options.","D Explain that \"feeding tube\" is the most clinically accurate term and should be used consistently to avoid confusing the family.","The spouse of a patient with a terminal diagnosis begins expressing sadness, withdrawal, and grief over the anticipated loss of shared future plans weeks before the patient's death. How should the nurse best interpret this?","A This is bereavement, since any grief response related to a dying patient is classified as bereavement.","B This indicates the spouse has moved into denial and needs referral to formal grief counseling immediately.","C This is anticipatory grief, a normal response that begins before the death and includes intangible losses.","D This is abnormal grief, since a grief response should not begin until after the patient has actually died.","A terminally ill, competent patient in a state that permits Medical Aid in Dying (MAID) asks the nurse to explain what the process actually involves. Which response is accurate?","A \"A physician or family member administers the lethal medication once you give verbal consent.\"","B \"You would be prescribed a lethal dose of medication after counseling and would take it yourself.\"","C \"MAID refers to pain medication doses that are increased enough that they may incidentally hasten your death.\"","D \"MAID involves the care team sedating you to unconsciousness to relieve refractory symptoms.\"","A patient says, \"I just want the doctors to find a cure for my cancer.\" Later, the same patient is about to start a new chemotherapy regimen and lives alone. Which pairing correctly demonstrates an \"I wish\" statement followed by an \"I worry…","A \"I worry we don't have a cure for your cancer\" followed by \"I wish you had someone here with you during treatment.\"","B \"I wish we had a cure for your cancer\" followed by \"I worry that going through chemotherapy alone could be isolating for you.\"","C \"I understand exactly how you feel about wanting a cure\" followed by \"I wish you had more support at home.\"","D \"I wish you had someone with you at home\" followed by \"I worry the chemotherapy won't cure your cancer.\"","A nursing student observes the social worker on an interdisciplinary palliative care team conducting a psychological assessment and providing direct emotional support to a patient, in addition to coordinating community resources. How should…","A The social worker is exceeding their scope, since their role is limited to case management and resource referral, not psychological assessment.","B The social worker is functioning at the top of their licensure, since psychosocial assessment and clinical care are part of that role.","C This work belongs to the chaplain, and the social worker should refer all emotional support needs to spiritual care instead.","D Psychological assessment and emotional support should only be performed by the physician or nurse practitioner.","A patient was admitted to hospice eight months ago with advanced heart failure and a physician-certified prognosis of 6 months or less. At today's visit, the nurse notes the patient's symptoms have significantly improved with the hospice te…","A The patient will undergo a \"live discharge\" from hospice because the prognosis requirement is no longer met.","B The patient will remain on hospice automatically, since the prognosis requirement no longer applies after 6 months enrolled.","C The patient will be required to begin curative treatment for heart failure immediately upon discharge.","D The patient's hospice enrollment will continue unchanged until a full year of enrollment has passed.","A hospice patient in the final hours of life develops loud, gurgling respirations from secretions pooling in the throat. The patient's spouse is distressed and asks the nurse to \"suction it out so she can breathe better.\" What is the nurse'…","A Perform deep suctioning with a Yankauer catheter every 15 minutes until the gurgling sound resolves.","B Explain that the sound is usually not distressing to the patient, and offer position changes and ordered medications instead.","C Explain that the sound indicates the patient is choking and needs immediate deep suctioning to prevent aspiration.","D Tell the spouse that nothing can be done and the secretions must be allowed to accumulate untreated.","A patient's family is comparing hospice and palliative care options and asks the nurse how each is typically paid for. Which response is most accurate?","A \"Hospice is covered by Medicare, Medicaid, and most private insurance, while palliative care coverage depends more on your specific insurance plan.\"","B \"Both hospice and palliative care are covered identically by Medicare regardless of the patient's insurance plan.\"","C \"Palliative care is only available to patients who pay entirely out of pocket, while hospice is fully covered by Medicaid alone.\"","D \"Hospice coverage depends on the patient's individual insurance plan, while palliative care is uniformly covered by Medicare and Medicaid.\"","A hospice nurse is assessing a patient thought to be in the late (hours) stage of the syndrome of imminent dying. Which assessment findings are consistent with this stage? (Select all that apply.)","A Death rattle from secretions pooling at the base of the throat","B Mottled, purplish discoloration of the toes and feet","C Fever, often related to aspiration pneumonia","D Coma with continued breathing","E Obtundation with only brief periods of wakefulness, slow to arouse","F Increased daytime sleeping with reversal of the sleep-wake cycle","A new graduate nurse is orienting to both an inpatient palliative care consult service and a community hospice team and asks a preceptor who \"drives\" the plan of care on each. Which response best reflects how these two teams typically funct…","A On both teams, the social worker drives care coordination day to day, with the nurse and physician assisting.","B On both teams, the physician takes the lead role, with the nurse serving a purely supportive documentation function.","C In hospice, the nurse drives care coordination, while on the hospital palliative team, the physician has more of the primary role.","D In hospice, the physician drives care coordination, while in hospital palliative care, the nurse is the formal team leader.","A grieving family member tells the nurse, \"One day I feel like I'm handling this and starting to build a new routine, and the next day the grief hits me out of nowhere again. Something must be wrong with me.\" Which response best reflects th…","A \"That's concerning — healthy grieving should move steadily through Kübler-Ross's stages without going backward.\"","B \"You should try to stay focused on distraction and new routines, and avoid revisiting the grief itself as much as possible.\"","C \"That back-and-forth usually means you're stuck in the denial stage and haven't yet accepted the loss.\"","D \"That's a normal pattern — grief naturally oscillates between feeling the loss directly and rebuilding your life.\"","A 58-year-old patient with newly diagnosed stage II breast cancer wants to continue aggressive chemotherapy but is struggling with treatment-related nausea, fatigue, and anxiety. The oncology team refers the patient to palliative care. Whic…","A \"You'll need a physician to certify a 6-month or less prognosis before this referral can start.\"","B \"Palliative care can start now and continue alongside your chemotherapy, whatever your prognosis.\"","C \"This referral means your care team feels further chemotherapy won't help you anymore.\"","D \"Palliative care will require you to pause chemotherapy while your symptoms are being managed.\"","During a code status discussion, a physician tells a patient, \"If we do CPR, we'll be cracking your ribs and you'll likely die attached to machines anyway — most people wouldn't want that.\" The patient agrees to a DNR. What is the primary e…","A The physician provided an honest, evidence-based description of likely outcomes that supported informed decision-making.","B The patient lacked the legal capacity to make a code status decision during a serious, acute illness.","C DNR conversations should only occur with the patient's designated health care surrogate, not the patient directly.","D The graphic, coercive framing may have intimidated the patient rather than supporting an autonomous choice.","A hospice patient's daughter tells the nurse, \"I don't want to give my mother her morphine dose — last time we increased it for my grandfather, he died within hours. I think it killed him.\" Which response by the nurse is most accurate?","A \"You're right to be cautious — opioid dose increases at the end of life are strongly associated with hastened death.\"","B \"Respiratory depression from morphine appears well before any drowsiness or confusion, so we watch her breathing closely.\"","C \"We can switch to a non-opioid medication so you don't have to worry about the morphine shortening her life.\"","D \"When morphine is dosed according to accepted guidelines, there's no difference in survival with the dose or with dose changes.\"","A hospice nurse notes that a patient's breathing pattern has become irregular, with periods of apnea lasting approximately 45 seconds between breaths. The patient's family, at the bedside, asks if their loved one has just died. What is the …","A \"Yes, an apnea period of more than 30 seconds means that breathing has permanently stopped.\"","B \"Not necessarily — pauses of 30 seconds to a minute are common in adults at this stage of dying.\"","C \"This pattern only occurs in infants and newborns, so it's an unusual finding for an adult patient.\"","D \"This means your loved one is in pain and needs an immediate increase in opioid medication.\"","A patient enrolled in hospice for metastatic pancreatic cancer also has chronic kidney disease and has been receiving dialysis for two years. The patient's adult child asks the nurse why the physician allowed dialysis to continue after hosp…","A \"Dialysis can continue as long as the family strongly prefers it, since hospice honors family preference.\"","B \"Now that your parent has been enrolled a while, all restrictions on treating the original diagnosis are lifted.\"","C \"Dialysis can continue as a comfort measure because it treats a condition other than the hospice-qualifying cancer.\"","D \"Dialysis is being continued as a curative treatment for the pancreatic cancer that qualified your parent for hospice.\"","A nurse is caring for several patients approaching the end of life and reflects on what shapes end-of-life care decisions. Which factors are identified as influencing how patients and families approach these decisions? (Select all that appl…","A Ethnicity and intracultural differences within the same culture","B Long-standing family relationships, dynamics, and conflicts at the bedside","C Class and access, including insurance and financial resources","D Generational, faith, and cultural traditions in the family","E The specific hospital unit where the patient is currently admitted","F The patient's primary nursing diagnosis listed on the care plan","A patient who just received a terminal diagnosis says to the nurse, \"You have no idea what this is like.\" The nurse previously cared for a family member who died of a similar illness. Using the NURSE framework for responding to emotion, wha…","A \"I can't imagine what this is like — can you tell me more about what you're feeling?\"","B \"Actually, I do understand — I went through something very similar with my own family member.\"","C \"Try not to focus on that right now; let's talk about your treatment options instead.\"","D \"You shouldn't feel that way — plenty of people get through a diagnosis like this one.\""]}]},{"f":"fundamentals-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Fundamentals Review","Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics","The 11 Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of the other 11 Fundamentals Review pages, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. A handful of big ideas (immobility, nutrition, o…"]},{"s":"Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","a":"five-clusters-not-eleven-separate-topics","k":["Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","Eleven pages is a lot to hold in your head at once. All of them fall into one of five groups — knowing the group can matter more than memorizing all eleven titles.","The Professional Frame — Legal & Ethical Issues; Nursing Process, Clinical Judgment & SBAR — Not body systems at all — the rules you practice under and the thinking process you run every patient through. Every other topic quietly assumes bo…","Airway, Breathing & Circulation — Oxygenation & Tracheostomy; Cardiovascular; Neurosensory & Pain — The assessment you perform on everyone: getting oxygen in, moving it around the body, and reading the neuro status and pain that tell you wh…","Intake & Metabolism — Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome; Diabetic Care & Nutrition — What the body takes in and how it uses it. These two overlap more than any other pair in the review — metabolic syndrome is diagnosed off the same waist, glucos…","Elimination & Skin — Urinary & Bowel Elimination; Integumentary — The two systems that go wrong when a patient stops moving. Immobility, incontinence, and poor nutrition feed the same set of complications from both directions.","Care Beyond the Admission — Older Adults & Discharge Planning; Palliative, Hospice & End of Life — Both look past this shift — who the patient is, what happens when they leave, and what happens when they aren't going to get better. Both are…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, working through the clusters in this order touches every recurring theme once before moving on, instead of jumping between unrelated systems."]},{"s":"The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-11-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Legal & Ethical Issues — Scope of practice, HIPAA, informed consent, advance directives and DNR — plus the values and ethical dilemmas sitting underneath all of them.","2 — Nursing Process, Clinical Judgment & SBAR — The nursing process, the Clinical Judgment Model, and how to organize what you say when you call a provider.","3 — Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome — Classifying obesity, its contributors and health risks, the criteria for metabolic syndrome, and the lifestyle interventions for both.","4 — Oxygenation & Tracheostomy — Ventilation, diffusion and perfusion; breath sounds; hypoxia; oxygen delivery devices; and the whole of tracheostomy care.","5 — Cardiovascular — Perfusion and cardiac output, hypertension and hypotension, hyperlipidemia, VTE, and cardiovascular assessment.","6 — Diabetic Care & Nutrition — Type 1 vs. type 2, insulin therapy, hypo- and hyperglycemia — then nutrition, dysphagia, and enteral/parenteral feeding.","7 — Urinary & Bowel Elimination — Retention, UTIs and CAUTIs, incontinence and catheters; constipation, diarrhea and C. difficile.","8 — Integumentary — Skin assessment and color changes, pressure injury staging, wound types and healing, dressings, and surgical wound complications.","9 — Neurosensory & Pain — Neuro assessment (ABCs, GCS, LOC), sensory deprivation and overload, headaches, and the full arc of pain assessment and management.","10 — Older Adults & Discharge Planning — Normal aging vs. illness, falls, polypharmacy, atypical illness presentation, and how a discharge actually gets coordinated.","11 — Palliative, Hospice & End of Life — Palliative vs. hospice, hard conversations, advance care planning, ethical issues, and the dying process."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Advance directives, living wills & code status — Legal & Ethical Issues — Palliative/Hospice/EOL (advance care planning as a conversation rather than a form, and how a code status discussion can be framed coercively)","Immobility as a risk multiplier — Integumentary — Urinary & Bowel Elimination (retention and constipation), Cardiovascular (VTE risk), Older Adults (acute care complications of hospitalization)","Nutrition & malnutrition — Diabetic Care & Nutrition — Integumentary (nutrition as a factor in wound healing), Older Adults (leading health concerns and nutrition), Obesity (lifestyle and nutrition interventions)","The metabolic numbers (waist, glucose, lipids, BP) — Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome — Diabetic Care & Nutrition (the same fasting glucose thresholds used to diagnose diabetes), Cardiovascular (hypertension and hyperlipidemia are two of the sa…","Oxygenation and perfusion as the first assessment — Oxygenation & Tracheostomy — Cardiovascular (perfusion, cardiac output and ejection fraction), Neurosensory & Pain (ABCs before the neuro exam, and ABCs before naloxone)","New-onset confusion & atypical presentation — Older Adults & Discharge Planning — Urinary & Bowel Elimination (UTI in an older adult), Neurosensory & Pain (a change in level of consciousness as an assessment finding)","Falls — Older Adults & Discharge Planning — Cardiovascular (orthostatic hypotension), Neurosensory & Pain (sensory deficits and neuro patient safety), Urinary & Bowel Elimination (urgency and getting to the bathroom in time)","Therapeutic communication — Palliative, Hospice & End of Life — Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome (weight-sensitive assessment and communication), Older Adults (communication, teaching, and recognizing elder mistreatment)","Escalating to the provider (SBAR) — Nursing Process, Clinical Judgment & SBAR — Every \"notify the provider\" moment in the review — hypertensive crisis (Cardiovascular), evisceration (Integumentary), opioid respiratory depression (Neurosenso…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the 11 pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Legal & Ethical Issues — A DNR order does not mean \"do not care\" — it means only that CPR won't be started if breathing or the heart stops. Every other aspect of care continues as normal.","Oxygenation & Tracheostomy — Oxygen toxicity is a real complication of too much supplemental oxygen — use the lowest FiO2 that achieves the result. Watch for chest pain or heaviness, coughing, dyspnea, muscle twitching, nausea, seizures, an…","Cardiovascular — Hypertensive crisis — systolic over 180 and/or diastolic over 120 — is a medical emergency requiring the patient to see a provider immediately.","Diabetic Care & Nutrition — A random glucose over 300 mg/dL is a medical emergency. Insulin is high-alert: never give it without a current blood glucose, and first confirm the insulin's onset/peak/duration, the diet order and NPO status, an…","Integumentary — Evisceration is an emergency — stay with the patient, cover the organs with a sterile saline-soaked dressing (never reinsert), position supine with knees bent, keep NPO, and notify the provider; surgery is likely.","Neurosensory & Pain — Opioid respiratory depression is highest risk in opioid-naive patients and with concurrent benzodiazepines. ABCs first, then naloxone — its half-life is shorter than most opioids, so repeat dosing and close monitoring …","Older Adults & Discharge Planning — New-onset confusion is often the first or only sign of infection or acute illness — a UTI, pneumonia, or even an MI may present as confusion rather than fever, bladder pain or chest pain. Always investiga…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Know the current number before you give the drug.\" Insulin requires a current blood glucose, not an earlier one (Diabetic Care & Nutrition), and an opioid requires a known baseline respiratory and neuro status (Neurosensory & Pain). Two di…","\"More isn't safer.\" Oxygen has a toxicity ceiling (Oxygenation & Tracheostomy), and the naloxone that reverses respiratory depression also reverses the pain control you were trying to provide (Neurosensory & Pain). Both teach that the treat…","\"Airway and breathing come before the thing you were called about.\" ABCs before naloxone, ABCs before the neuro exam, and airway patency as the first priority when a trach comes out — the same ordering appears in three separate topics.","\"In an older adult, the textbook presentation is the exception.\" Confusion instead of fever with a UTI, confusion instead of chest pain with an MI. It's why atypical presentation gets its own section rather than a footnote, and why it also …","\"The conversation is the intervention.\" NURSE statements at end of life, weight-sensitive assessment in obesity care, and discharge teaching for an older adult are all places where what you say is the nursing care, not a preamble to it."]}]}];
